window.GAME_CHANGELOGS={"conquest":{"nm":"Space Conquest","href":"conquest.html","entries":[{"v":"2.1.0","d":"Living worlds: new terrain, foliage everywhere, and every model rebuilt","items":["NEW TERRAIN. The ground blends from soil to real rock on every slope, with layered cliffs, big natural patches of darker and lighter ground so the texture never visibly repeats, and snow, ash or mineral crust on the highest ridges. The land itself has real valleys and mountain ridgelines now","LAKES ON EVERY WORLD. Valleys fill with liquid that matches the planet: glowing alien water at the colony, frozen ice on Vesta, molten lava on Ember Reach, swamp on Verdant Hollow, violet void liquid on Obsidian Moon and black oil on Bastion Prime","FOLIAGE ON EVERY PLANET. Thousands of grass clumps that sway in the wind, trees, shrubs, ferns, glowing flowers, crystal clusters and rocks, with a different plant set for each world: giant mushroom trees and coral shrubs at home, snowy pines and ice crystals on Vesta, dead trees, basalt columns and ember-veined rocks on Ember Reach, jungle trees and ferns on Verdant Hollow, crystal fields on Obsidian Moon and wrecked machinery on Bastion Prime. Plants grow in natural meadows and groves, stay off cliffs and out of lakes, and short ground cover inside your build area hides itself under anything you build","EVERY BUILDING REMODELLED. All of them are rebuilt with bevelled panels, real structural frames, pipe runs, vents, rivets, ladders, handrails and lit screens: a drilling derrick for the Miner, a three-tower rig for the Miner Mk.2, a refractory blast furnace, a four-post hydraulic press, a fractionating column refinery, an arc foundry, a robotic assembly cell, a coal power plant with a spinning flywheel, solar trackers with real cells, lattice power pylons with insulators, freight containers, a gantry crane shipyard, a command centre with a comms array, and new walls, windows, blast doors, foundations, lights and plants","EVERY SHIP REMODELLED. Scout, Fighter, Frigate, Bomber, Warhawk and Vanguard all have new streamlined hulls, swept wings, framed canopies over lit cockpits, engine nacelles, intakes, landing gear, thrusters and antennae. Each ship now rests on its own landing gear when parked","NEW CHARACTERS AND ENEMIES. A new astronaut suit with a gold-visored helmet and life-support pack, new alien soldiers, heavies, snipers and hunter drones, a new hive crawler and hive, new flak turrets, new raider interceptors and a new Sentinel","NEW PROPS. Ore deposits are fractured outcrops split open by veins of ore, the lander you arrived in is rebuilt with its cargo drop, enemy camps get new barracks, sniper towers and sandbag cover, and your station in orbit has a habitat ring and solar wings","Faster rendering: each detailed model merges its static parts, and foliage is drawn in chunks, only on the world you're on"]},{"v":"2.0.0","d":"The Armada Update: a fleet of robot-piloted ships, a fortress world, real graphics","items":["REALISTIC GRAPHICS. Every building, ship and prop is now made of worn metal plates with seams, rivets, grime and scratches that catch the light (real normal and roughness maps, generated in-game), metal reflects the sky around it, and the sun casts real-time shadows from every building, ship and character. Ground on every world has pebbles, cracks and dust layers. Turn it down under Menu → Graphics if your device struggles","REALISTIC PLANETS. Planets are rebuilt from scratch with continents, mountain ranges, craters and polar caps, glowing atmospheres around their edges, and a visible sun in space","NEW EXPLOSIONS. Rolling fireballs that cool from white to deep red, shockwave rings, lingering smoke, spinning debris with ember trails, a real flash of light on nearby hulls, and camera shake","A SATISFACTORY-STYLE PRODUCTION CHAIN. Coal finally matters: the Coal Generator burns it for 75 MW, and the Foundry smelts iron ore with coal into Steel. The Assembler (two animated robot arms) makes Wire, Circuit Boards, Motors and Hull Plating, and the Robotics Bay builds Robot Pilots","MILESTONES. The Hub console is now a tiered milestone tree — Foundations, Industrialization, Fleet Logistics, Armada — each tier opening up the next part of the chain","HAND-FEEDING MACHINES. Every machine panel has Load inputs from backpack and Collect output, so a line can run before it's belted. New machines send unbelted output straight to your stockpile","HANGARS. Build a Hangar and every new ship the Shipyard finishes rolls onto one of its four berths once you already have a flagship. Parked ships repair while the hangar has power. Swap your flagship for any hangar ship whenever you like","FLEET COMMAND AND ROBOT PILOTS. Assign a Robot Pilot to any hangar ship, pick which ships fly the next sortie and choose the formation (Vee, Line, Column or Ring)","SORTIE LAUNCHES. Board your ship at the colony and the hangar doors roll open — your piloted ships lift off their berths, taxi out and climb to form up on your wings. Launch from the Shipyard and they streak up into formation in orbit","FORMATION FLYING AND THE RELEASE BUTTON. Wingmen hold formation and shoot anything that crosses your nose. Press X (or the RELEASE button) and they break formation to hunt targets on their own; press it again and they regroup on you. They follow you down to planets and circle overhead as air cover while you're on foot. Lost wingmen are lost for good","WARHAWK GUNSHIP. A new attack ship: rapid twin plasma cannons and paired homing missiles on F (or the MISSILE button) that lock onto whatever you're aiming at","BASTION PRIME. A colossal fortress world far past the rest of the system, sealed under a planetary shield and circled by an orbital gun ring so big your ship is a grain of rice beside it. Forty turret batteries track and fire on you, and three carriers patrol the ring, launching fighter flights. Destroy all eight reactor cores on the ring's rim and the whole ring tears itself apart, the shield fails, and the planet can finally be landed on","SMARTER ENEMY FIGHTERS. Raiders are proper interceptors now: they lead their shots, bracket you from both sides, overshoot and swing back for another pass, and jink when hit. They go after your wingmen too","ALIEN GARRISONS. Every world has a faction camp with barracks, a sniper tower and cover. Troops patrol until they spot you, alert each other, take cover and peek out to fire, send a flanker around your side, heavies push in with plasma, stalker drones rush you, the sniper paints you with a laser before firing, and the wounded fall back to heal. The barracks sends reinforcements until you destroy it. Wipe out a camp to take the world","FIGHT BACK. You always carry a sidearm now (the Pulse Blaster upgrades it), and every ship's guns work on strafing runs low over a world — left-click while flying","BETTER UI. A frosted-glass HUD with an icon resource strip, an Objective tracker that walks you from your first Miner to conquering the system, a squadron panel with each wingman's hull, navigation markers for every world in space, a galaxy map of the real system, a FIRE button on mobile, and properly styled machine panels","Fixed: machine panel buttons (production targets, loading) showed up as unstyled browser buttons"]},{"v":"1.53.2","d":"Found the actual source of the erratic-looking shots: spread was too loose","items":["The last two patches confirmed the shot itself always was a perfectly straight line and simplified the muzzle flash, but the underlying complaint was still real — tracked it down by firing a stream of shots while smoothly sweeping the mouse and comparing each shot's actual angle: instead of tracking the sweep, the angles jumped around non-monotonically (one sample went -13°, -19°, -12°, -16°, -19° in a row while the mouse moved smoothly the whole time). Regular fire's random spread cone (meant to be a \"roughly here, not exactly here\" reticle) was about 5.7° wide — bigger than the smooth angle change between shots while tracking a target, so shot-to-shot jitter was swamping the actual aim signal, reading as an erratic scatter or swarm instead of a stream of shots landing where the reticle pointed. Both regular and zoomed spread are now roughly 6x tighter (about 0.9° and 0.2°) — confirmed the same sweep test now produces smoothly, monotonically changing shot angles that track the mouse the whole way, matching \"a turret looking at the cursor and shooting directly at it\""]},{"v":"1.53.1","d":"Muzzle flash no longer looks like extra stray bullets","items":["Verified the actual shot itself is a genuinely straight line — sampled a live bolt's velocity across several ticks mid-flight and it's bit-for-bit identical the whole way, so nothing is steering or curving it toward the cursor after it's fired. The odd sideways-then-forward, swarm-like motion was the muzzle flash: firing used to also kick out 3-4 small spark particles with their own randomized velocity roughly along the shot's path, which at a glance read as several extra small projectiles scattering off in their own directions rather than effects tied to the one real shot. That's now a single flash that expands and fades in place at the muzzle (and at the impact point on a hit) with no velocity of its own, so there's nothing left that could be mistaken for a second, oddly-moving bullet"]},{"v":"1.53.0","d":"Fixed shots firing the wrong way, and added real vertical aim","items":["Fixed a real bug: the crosshair's horizontal aim was mirrored — pointing right sent shots left and vice versa. It was computed by hand from the screen-X offset and a manual FOV/tan formula, and that formula had the sign backwards. Rebuilt it on THREE's own camera.setFromCamera raycast instead of hand-rolled trig, which can't have that class of sign bug","Vertical aim now actually works too — pointing the crosshair at the top or bottom of the screen sends shots up or down, not just left or right. Bolts can now genuinely travel off the flat plane they used to be stuck on (hit-detection against enemy ships and the boss now checks vertical distance too, so a shot aimed steeply up or down can actually miss a target it's merely horizontally lined up with, the same way it always could miss left-right)","Shots now fly in a dead-straight line to exactly where the crosshair points, instead of blending in the ship's own velocity — with aim fully independent of the ship's heading (see 1.52.0), the ship can easily be moving in a totally different direction than the crosshair, and that blend was visibly bending shots away from the reticle. A shot now always outruns whatever fired it purely by flying fast enough (18,000/24,000 for the regular bolt and railgun respectively, both comfortably above any ship's own top boosted speed) rather than by inheriting velocity"]},{"v":"1.52.0","d":"Corrected course: the mouse only aims the crosshair now, it doesn't steer the ship","items":["Last patch had the mouse turn the ship's own heading to face the crosshair — wrong call. The mouse now ONLY aims where shots go; it never touches the ship's heading or movement, which are back to being entirely WASD/QE (A/D turns, W/S or the up/down arrows or scroll wheel for throttle, Q/E altitude). The crosshair still tracks the cursor and still marks exactly where fire actually goes — that part was right — it's just genuinely independent of the ship's own nose now, like a turret rather than steering. Confirmed live: turning the ship with A/D no longer touches pcTargetYaw at all, and a shot fired while the mouse and the ship's own heading pointed in very different directions landed within the normal spread tolerance of the crosshair's direction, not the ship's"]},{"v":"1.51.0","d":"Mouse-aim space combat: the ship actually turns to face the crosshair now","items":["Last patch's crosshair moved with the mouse, but the ship itself still only ever turned with A/D — the reticle was purely cosmetic, disconnected from where shots actually went. Movement and aiming are now properly split, the classic mouse-look-plus-WASD-strafe space-shooter setup: the ship's nose eases toward wherever the crosshair sits (so fire genuinely goes where the reticle points), A/D is now a real sideways strafe relative to wherever the nose is currently facing instead of turning, and W/S now work as throttle up/down too, alongside the existing up/down arrows and scroll wheel","Fixed a real bug caught while building this: the first version computed the aim target fresh every single frame from the live chase camera, which re-centers behind the ship's nose almost immediately after any turn — so a mouse sitting anywhere off-center kept meaning \"turn further still\" forever, and the ship never actually stopped rotating. Confirmed live: yaw ran up past -670 radians (over 100 full turns) from just leaving the mouse resting off-center for a couple of seconds. The aim target is now only recomputed when the mouse actually moves, captured as a fixed heading at that moment — the ship eases toward it and holds once it arrives, instead of chasing a target that runs away in lockstep with its own turning"]},{"v":"1.50.0","d":"A real crosshair and aim-down-sights zoom for space combat, plus a fire-direction bug fix","items":["Space flight had no reticle at all — no indication of where fire was actually going to land, which made aiming mostly guesswork. There's now a jagged targeting ring at screen center that shows the actual cone a shot can land in (see the new spread mechanic below), instead of a UI element that's just decorative","Left click now fires (used to be right click) and right click zooms in — a real aim-down-sights: FOV narrows, fire rate drops to about 59% of normal, and the reticle itself shrinks to about 40% its normal size, all easing in together rather than snapping. Both buttons can be held at once (fire while zoomed) — they used to share a single \"which button is down\" value, so holding one while pressing the other would silently cancel the first; each is now tracked independently","Regular (non-zoomed) fire now has a real spread cone instead of a laser-straight line every time, matching what the reticle shows — the whole point of a reticle is \"roughly here,\" and zooming is the payoff for actually needing \"exactly here\"","Fixed a real bug: bolts fired at a fixed absolute speed along the nose with no regard for the ship's own current velocity. At full boost (1.7x) some ships' own top speed could get close to or exceed that fixed bolt speed, so the ship could nearly catch up to or overtake its own shots — the \"bullets don't fly right\" symptom. Bolts now inherit the ship's current velocity on top of their own muzzle speed (the same thing dropped bombs already correctly did), which guarantees a shot is always genuinely outrunning whatever ship fired it, boosting or not","Shooting effects got a real pass: bolts are now a bright core inside a glowing additive shell instead of one flat-colored cylinder, plus a spark burst right at the muzzle when a shot fires and another wherever it actually lands, instead of shots silently appearing and hits silently deleting the bolt with nothing to show for it"]},{"v":"1.49.1","d":"Q/E camera no longer swings to a near-vertical top-down view","items":["Climb/dive pitch has always had a floor but no ceiling, and at any real ship speed it's floored/scaled by forward speed vs vertical speed — hold Q or E while barely moving forward (hovering, just launched, slowed down) and the pitch angle pins itself near 90 degrees. The chase camera's horizontal offset used to scale down with that same angle, so at 90 degrees it collapsed to almost nothing, leaving the camera floating almost directly above the ship looking straight down with no sense of motion or direction — exactly the \"hard to see\" complaint. The camera's behind-the-ship distance is now derived from yaw alone, so it never collapses no matter how steep the pitch gets, and pitch itself only lifts/drops it a bounded amount on top of that — confirmed directly: holding Q while stationary now still pegs the ship's own pitch near 90 degrees, but the camera holds a steady, natural ~27 degree look-down angle throughout instead of swinging toward vertical"]},{"v":"1.49.0","d":"Obsidian Moon: a guardian boss, a new ore, and the Vanguard","items":["A fourth world, Obsidian Moon, now sits in the one quadrant of space that was empty — a small, jagged, violet-hazed moon guarded by a boss ship (\"The Sentinel\") that has to be shot down before landing will even lock on. Confirmed live: flying in and trying to settle while it's alive keeps the targeting hint pinned on \"DEFEAT THE GUARDIAN\" and landing progress refuses to advance no matter how close, slow, or low you are; killing it flips a persistent guardianDefeated flag, the hint switches to the normal LOCKING ON sequence, and the same approach that was stuck at 0% rides straight through to touchdown","The Sentinel has its own top-of-screen boss health bar (separate from your own HULL/SHIELD/BASE readout), spawns once you fly within range of the moon and despawns again if you fly far enough away, fires back at you on its own cooldown, and its defeat is saved permanently — reloading a save made after the kill confirmed the moon stays clear rather than resetting the guardian","The moon's own ore, Obsidian Shard, mines and belts through every existing system (Miners, Storage, Splitters, Mergers, Shipment Launcher/Hub) the same as any other ore — but doesn't feed the regular Constructor/Refinery recipes. Its only use is commissioning the new Vanguard-class ship at the Shipyard (extra to the usual Parts cost), so actually flying one means having beaten the guardian and mined its moon yourself first — confirmed the Shipyard correctly blocks the purchase with a clear \"Not enough Obsidian Shard\" message if you're short, and deducts both costs correctly on success","The Vanguard is the strongest hull in the fleet (200 HP / 70 shield) and fires a railgun instead of the usual bolt or bomb — a distinct larger, faster, violet shot dealing its higher damage through the same hit-detection every other weapon already uses against both regular enemy ships and the boss itself","Fixed a real bug surfaced while testing this: loading an existing save (rather than starting a new colony) copied its stock over exactly as saved, so any resource key added after that save was written — Obsidian Shard included — was simply missing from stock after loading, which would have made mining or crafting it silently break as 0+n=NaN. Loading now backfills any resource the running game knows about but the save predates, defaulting it to 0"]},{"v":"1.48.4","d":"Diving now hits a lot harder","items":["Last patch's camera swing tracked the ship's own visual pitch — which is deliberately kept modest so the hull itself never looks absurd, so at any real cruising speed the swing stayed fairly subtle too. Added a second, separate camera swing driven directly by whether you're actively holding Q, decoupled from the ship's own restrained body angle, so it can push a lot harder without making the ship itself look like it's tumbling — confirmed the camera's relative height above the ship jumps from about 26 to about 97 units the moment you dive. Also added a widening FOV kick while diving (smaller than the boost kick, same idea), the classic rush-of-speed cue on top of the camera swing"]},{"v":"1.48.3","d":"Diving in space actually looks like diving now","items":["The ship's actual altitude was always changing correctly with Q/E in space (confirmed — it even reaches the hard floor if you hold Q long enough), but two things made it look like it wasn't going anywhere. First, the engine particle trail direction was computed from yaw alone, completely ignoring pitch, so nose-down and diving still shot the exhaust out dead-level instead of up-and-behind the ship — pulling the real backward axis off the ship's own orientation instead fixes this in space, low-altitude surface flight, and the launch climb. Second, and bigger: the chase camera's offset was also yaw-only, so it held the exact same relative position no matter how hard the ship pitched, and diving swung the ship increasingly edge-on to a camera that never adjusted to follow. The camera now factors pitch into its own offset too (deliberately not roll, so it doesn't bank with the ship), swinging up and back during a dive to keep it in clear view instead of going edge-on — confirmed numerically: a 17° dive now measurably lifts the camera's relative height from +16 to +26 units above the ship"]},{"v":"1.48.2","d":"Pitch angle stays visible at speed, arrival glide isn't a 140-unit plummet anymore","items":["Last patch's real flight-path pitch angle correctly shrinks toward level at high speed (that's the actual physics of it), but it shrank all the way down to just a couple degrees at or near top throttle — which read as \"the ship barely tilts even though I'm clearly climbing\", not as a correctly shallow climb. The angle now has a floor: it still grows steeper at low speed and shrinks at high speed same as before, but it never drops low enough to look like it's barely doing anything while Q or E is actually held","The glide down when arriving at a world or the colony dropped 140 units in 1.8 seconds — a peak fall speed of about 117 units/sec, which is enormous relative to how small this world actually is (a 300-unit zoneR, a ~45-unit flight ceiling), and read as an out-of-control plummet no matter how gently it eased in and out. Cut to 60 units over the same time (peak speed roughly halved, ~50 units/sec, comparable to the ship's own cruising speed) — still levels off at the same genuinely-medium altitude, just without free-falling most of the way there first"]},{"v":"1.48.1","d":"Real flight-path pitch angle, arrival glide levels off much higher up","items":["Q/E pitch was a fixed cosmetic tilt whenever held — the exact same angle whether crawling forward or at full throttle, and unrelated to the actual (now speed-scaled) climb/descend rate. It's the real flight-path angle now — arctangent of vertical speed over horizontal speed — so changing the throttle visibly changes how steep the climb/dive looks for the same Q/E input: slow and climbing reads as a steep nose-up, fast and climbing reads as a shallow one, matching how an actual aircraft's pitch relates to its climb angle. Confirmed directly: the same climb input measured 13.7° of pitch at low throttle and just 2.9° at full throttle","The glide-in when arriving at a world or the colony used to level off just 9 units above the ground — right on top of the terrain the instant it finished. It now levels off at a genuinely medium altitude (25 units, confirmed exactly), so you're still comfortably high up and have room to actually look around before choosing to descend the rest of the way yourself"]},{"v":"1.48.0","d":"Saving mid-flight now actually restores mid-flight","items":["Closing the game while actually flying — out in open space, or still airborne over a world/home and not yet touched down — always reloaded you back on foot at your stale pre-boarding position instead, since the save format had no concept of \"the ship was in the air\" at all, only ever a parked ship's resting spot. In space that read as spawning at a random corner of whichever world was nearest; over a world it read as spawning away from your own ship, which was quietly restored parked wherever it happened to be flying at save time while you reappeared somewhere else entirely. Saves made mid-flight now capture that the ship was actually airborne (plus its real altitude, not just x/z), and reloading drops you straight back into flying it — in open space at its exact last position, or still gliding over the exact same world, with the same on-the-ground parked-ship restore untouched for genuinely parked saves"]},{"v":"1.47.5","d":"Throttle now actually works over a world, Q/E altitude keeps up with the ship","items":["The throttle/speed gauges showed up while flying low over a world (fixed last patch) but were only ever wired to space flight — down on a world, the up/down arrows and scroll wheel that are supposed to drive them did nothing, since low-altitude flight was still on the old direct instant W/S control from before the throttle system existed. It now shares the exact same throttle lever as space (only up/down arrows or the wheel move it, W/S don't, no auto-decay)","Q/E altitude was a flat 9 units/sec no matter the ship — next to how much faster ships now cover ground horizontally, 9 read as barely doing anything at all: crossing the ~45-unit altitude band took 5 real seconds. It now scales with the ship's own surface speed, so climbing/descending actually keeps pace with everything else instead of feeling like it's not responding"]},{"v":"1.47.4","d":"Fixed Q/E's fake left/right tilt, throttle/speed gauges now show everywhere","items":["Q/E pitching the nose up/down looked fine right after launch but started visibly tilting left/right the moment you'd turned at all — in both space and low-altitude flight over a world. That wasn't roll (nothing was ever setting one), it was a rotation-order artifact: pitch and yaw were composing in an order where pitching the nose no longer happened purely around the ship's own local axis once yaw had changed. Reordered to yaw-then-pitch-then-roll, the standard order for exactly this kind of flight control, which eliminates the cross-coupling entirely — confirmed directly: with 90° of yaw and a hard pitch both applied, the ship's local \"right\" direction stays dead level (effectively exactly 0 tilt) instead of skewing","The throttle and speed gauges only ever showed up during main space cruise — they vanished the instant you dropped into low-altitude flight over a world, even though you're just as much flying. They now show in every flying context, space or surface, and the speed gauge reads real current speed relative to whatever the actual top speed is in that context instead of freezing at a stale value"]},{"v":"1.47.3","d":"Throttle is now a real lever, not a hold-to-go key","items":["W/S used to also move the throttle (it decayed back to 0 on its own whenever neither was held), which fights the entire point of a throttle you can set once and leave — the ship never actually held a steady cruise speed on its own. W/S no longer touch the throttle at all: only the up/down arrows and the scroll wheel move it now, and it holds exactly wherever you leave it with no automatic decay. Set it to 30% with the down arrow and the ship settles there and stays there — actual capped cruise control instead of something that quietly crept back toward zero the moment you let go"]},{"v":"1.47.2","d":"Throttle vs. speed are now actually different things, with a real speed gauge","items":["Throttle is how much power you're commanding, not how fast you're going — but the ship's velocity used to snap to match the throttle setting instantly every single frame, so there was never any real difference between the two. The ship now actually has to accelerate: pin the throttle to 100% and speed genuinely ramps up toward it over about a second and a half instead of teleporting there, and easing off the throttle bleeds speed back down gradually instead of cutting it immediately","Added a second gauge (SPD, in blue) next to the throttle gauge (THR, now amber) on the right edge of the screen, so you can actually see the ship's real speed chasing whatever you've set the throttle to instead of just watching one number that was secretly both things at once"]},{"v":"1.47.1","d":"Fixed landing on home being impossible after the last altitude fix","items":["The altitude ceiling added last patch to stop combat from accidentally triggering a landing used a flat 65% of a world's radius — for home specifically (radius 3150) that ceiling sat at 2047, which is BELOW the 3180-altitude line where the ship's own solid-planet collision physically shoves it back out. There was no legal altitude left where the ship could be both close enough to lock on and not colliding with home's own surface, so flying home and landing normally stopped working entirely. The ceiling is now anchored to that same solid-collision boundary plus a real descent buffer instead of an independent fraction of the radius, so the two can't drift apart like that again — confirmed combat altitude is still excluded and a normal landing approach works again"]},{"v":"1.47.0","d":"Collision matches the actual models, a visible throttle gauge, no more accidental home-landing mid-fight","items":["Found a serious one: home-defense combat happens at a fixed altitude almost directly above the colony, and last patch's proximity-only landing lock-on checked distance in a flat plane with no altitude limit at all — so simply fighting a wave kept the lock-on timer filling the entire time, and would silently force-land you out of the fight roughly every 1.3 seconds. Landing near a world now also requires being reasonably low relative to that world's own size (generous enough that normal approach flying is unaffected — this isn't the old 6-unit hard floor), which keeps combat altitude clearly out of range everywhere","Foundation tiles and the Landing Pad were flagged solid along with everything else, which is exactly backwards — a Foundation is the flat floor you're meant to stand on, and the Landing Pad is a raised deck on thin legs, not a solid drum. Both are walk/fly-through again","The ship itself had no building collision at all and flew straight through everything at low altitude, including the Landing Pad it's supposed to be landing on. Low-altitude surface flight now collides with buildings the same way the on-foot player does","Added a throttle gauge docked to the right edge of the screen while flying in space, filling live as you adjust speed","S and the down arrow can no longer push the throttle negative — it stops dead at 0 instead of reversing","Engine effects got a real pass: a small white-hot core spark right at each nozzle, and an actual point light so the engine now visibly lights up the hull and anything nearby instead of just being a bright texture that illuminates nothing — boosting pushes both much further than before"]},{"v":"1.46.1","d":"Fixed the throttle never letting the ship coast to a stop","items":["The new throttle held its value completely whenever neither W/S nor the arrow keys were being pressed — which sounds fine for a real throttle lever, but the ship launches at 0.75 throttle and, since dogfighting is mostly turning and dodging rather than constantly holding forward, it would just keep cruising at that speed with nothing to stop it, flying itself tens of thousands of units off into empty space within seconds of a normal fight. The throttle now decays back toward zero on its own whenever you're not actively holding a throttle key, so letting go actually brings the ship to a stop again like it always used to, while still keeping the smooth ramp instead of an instant snap while you are holding a key"]},{"v":"1.46.0","d":"Throttle control, real engine ignition, solid buildings and planets","items":["The bright blue nozzle balls used to sit permanently lit no matter what the ship was doing — they're a plain grey inert part at rest now, and only light up (and the flame blasts out through them) once the ship actually starts moving, scaling continuously with how fast it's actually going","Boosting now blasts a visibly different engine: the flame stretches and widens further than before and switches to a hot magenta instead of just a brighter version of the normal cyan, so it reads as a distinct mode rather than the same flame turned up","W/S (and the up/down arrows) used to snap speed straight to full forward or full reverse the instant a key went down. They now ramp a persistent throttle up or down instead — the scroll wheel nudges the same throttle directly too — so you can settle at any cruising speed, including easing into reverse, instead of only ever having full-send or a dead stop","Buildings had no collision at all — you could walk straight through a Shipyard or a Refinery. Free-standing buildings (not wall/window/door panels, not belts/cables) are now solid and block the player","Planets had no collision either — the ship could fly straight into the middle of a world's rendered sphere. Planets are now solid too; flying into one bumps the ship back out and bleeds off its speed instead of clipping through the mesh"]},{"v":"1.45.4","d":"Fixed Q/E pitch direction in space, proximity lock-on landing, diagonal arrival glide","items":["In space, E (climb) was pitching the nose down while the ship actually rose, and Q (descend) pitched it up — exactly backwards, and disorienting since the tilt never matched which way you were actually moving. Fixed the sign so climbing noses up and descending noses down, matching low-altitude flight's convention","Landing on a world required flying near it AND already being at near-zero altitude AND under 3 units/sec, all at once — since the lock-on ring itself only appeared deep inside that same near range, in practice you had to basically fly into the planet just to get the prompt to start counting down. Landing now locks on from proximity alone: get within range at any altitude or speed and it locks on and takes you down, no more fiddly last-second altitude/speed threading","The glide down to a world's surface only ever moved the ship straight down — x/z never changed, so despite spawning hundreds of units up it was a dead-vertical drop with zero forward motion, reading as plummeting rather than flying in even though the ship wasn't tilted at all. It now spawns behind its own facing and eases in on a real diagonal path, and since forward motion sells the descent by itself now, the drop height came down a lot too"]},{"v":"1.45.3","d":"Real engine flames while flying, dramatically hotter effects on boost","items":["The only sign the engines were doing anything used to be a small idle glow ball plus occasional random particle puffs — between puffs it read as static. Every ship now has an actual flame at each nozzle (a soft outer flame plus a hot inner core) that eases up to full length the moment you're moving and settles back to a dim flicker at idle","Boosting now visibly transforms the engines instead of just moving faster: the flames stretch further, flare wider, and shift from cool blue-white to a hot orange-gold, the particle trail spawns twice as many streaks, and the camera's field of view widens for a few seconds — the classic everything-rushing-past push that sells speed better than a bigger trail alone"]},{"v":"1.45.2","d":"Smoother planet arrival, faster surface flight, landing prompt reaches you sooner","items":["Spawning in over a world dropped you 900 units up then eased out fast — full speed the instant you appeared, only slowing down near the ground — which read as spawning in the air and immediately plummeting. Switched to the same smooth ease-in/ease-out curve the actual touchdown already used, so the descent ramps up gently instead of starting at full plummet","Low-altitude flight over a landed world's terrain was capped at 26-46 units/sec regardless of ship — way too slow next to how far you can see across a world. Roughly doubled every ship's surface speed","The ring/name prompt that shows you're near a landable world only appeared once you were within 500 units of its surface — for a 5000+ unit-radius world that's barely outside the visible sphere, so you'd fly straight into the planet with zero warning it was even there. It now scales off each world's own radius and reaches you well before you're in landing range"]},{"v":"1.45.1","d":"Fixed the flight camera falling behind for good, stronger Q/E tilt","items":["Every earlier fix for the ship-chase camera lagging behind and zooming way out just bumped its catch-up rate — which works right up until ship speed gets pushed higher again in a later update, then the lag creeps back in, worse than ever with the spacebar boost active. The camera now hard-clamps itself to a fixed maximum distance from the ship on top of the catch-up lerp, so it physically cannot drift far behind no matter how fast the ship ends up flying in the future","Q/E ship tilt was too subtle to read — nearly doubled the tilt angle in space flight and low-altitude surface flight alike so banking with Q/E actually looks dramatic"]},{"v":"1.45.0","d":"Landing Pad and Splitter/Merger rebuilt with a lot more detail","items":["Landing Pad was just a flat disc, a lit ring, and four ball lamps — rebuilt as a real raised deck standing clear of the ground on six visible support legs, with a hazard-striped rim, a painted center marker, four corner beacon masts, and a small control shack with a window and an antenna","Splitter and Merger were a base disc, one plain box, and a flat trim band — added corner gussets so the cabinet reads as actually welded together, a vented top hatch instead of a flat cap, exposed pipe stubs, and panel bolt rows"]},{"v":"1.44.4","d":"Polish pass: smoother turning for enemies and turrets, a livelier shipyard","items":["Enemy ships in the home-defense fight used to snap their facing instantly to your exact bearing every single frame — they now turn smoothly toward you like something actually flying instead of twitching to track you","Alien turrets aimed the same instant-snap way — they now traverse smoothly onto target","The little docked-ship preview sitting on the Shipyard used to just sit there completely still — it now idles with a slow spin and a gentle bob while it waits to launch"]},{"v":"1.44.3","d":"Found the real cause of the dark band at the bottom of the screen","items":["That solid dark band sitting at the bottom of the screen the whole time wasn't a UI bug — the camera's near clipping plane got raised to 10 units a few updates back to stop the space scene's planets from z-fighting, but that's one shared camera used for on-foot walking too. Standing anywhere near a building, a slope, or just normal ground put real geometry closer than 10 units to the camera, which got clipped away entirely, showing raw empty background through the gap instead. The near plane now switches per scene (10 in space, 0.3 on foot) instead of one fixed value forced on both"]},{"v":"1.44.2","d":"Fixed the flight camera, Q/E tilt swap, and how far the landing circle reaches","items":["Q and E were swapped — climbing tilted the nose the way descending should, and descending tilted it the way climbing should","The landing-approach radius was a flat number added to a world's radius, so a bigger world (Ember Reach especially) could dwarf the pad and still make you fly almost onto the surface before \"near\" registered. It's now a straight multiple of that world's own radius, so it scales automatically no matter how big a given world is","Found why the flight camera clipped into and cut off the ship, and why the glide-down entrance didn't read as gradual: three separate camera-follow spots (world arrival, final touchdown, low-altitude flight) were still using an old, small 11-13 unit follow distance and a slow catch-up rate — nowhere near enough clearance for a ship that can fall 900 units in the first moment of the entrance glide. All five ship-following camera spots now use the same much larger distance and fast catch-up rate the interplanetary cruise camera already got fixed to"]},{"v":"1.44.1","d":"Fixed parked ship teleporting on reload, real drag-and-drop Storage, hid world edges","items":["Your parked ship's position was never actually saved — reloading fell back to wherever the player was last standing, so walking away from a landed ship before saving would make it \"teleport\" to your new spot on reload. Now saves and restores the ship's own resting position","Found why dragging into Storage never worked: the panel was quietly rebuilding its whole grid every 0.4 seconds while open, which destroys the exact element you're mid-drag on and silently kills the drop. Drag is now suppressed while an item's actually being dragged","Storage panel is bigger and fuller, with real empty slots shown on both sides (not just what's non-zero) so there's always a visible tile to drop onto","Extended each world's ground plate all the way out to its own fog distance, so the plate's actual edge is always fully fogged out before it comes into view instead of showing as a hard line against open space in the distance"]},{"v":"1.44.0","d":"Weapon fire is finally visible, flight fixes, more room to approach a planet","items":["Found the real reason weapon fire (Fighter included) looked broken: pBolts/eBolts were pure invisible data with no mesh ever created for them, on any ship, the entire time — added an actual glowing bolt mesh for both player and enemy fire, and widened the hit-detection radius so a bolt moving thousands of units/sec can't tunnel straight through a target between frames","Q/E vertical thrust in space was accidentally set to 0.6x cruise speed — for a 5000+ speed ship that's 3000+ units/sec of vertical motion in one key press, which is what was actually behind the camera going crazy and the ship's motion not matching its visible tilt. Cut down to a much saner 0.05x","The landing approach radius only extended ~800 units past a planet's surface at a 5000+ unit radius, so you had to fly almost inside a planet before \"near\" ever registered — pushed out to 4500 so you can start your final approach from well outside the visible surface","Arriving at a world (or gliding home) now starts the descent from much higher up (900 units instead of 58), so it actually reads as a real approach at this scale instead of a short hop","touchDown() was hardcoding the ship's landing rotation to a fixed tilt and yaw of exactly 0, ignoring whichever way you actually flew in — ships now land flat, facing the direction you brought them in on","A save made mid-flight (surfaceFlying true — mid-glide-down, or just after boarding to take off) restores as if already landed, but never turned the player's model back on — fixed, so you don't come back invisible","Enemy ships were still built at their original pre-3x scale (a speck next to a real ship) — scaled up 5x for visibility and fairness","Beefed up the engine thrust particle trail (bigger, farther-flung, more frequent) — the old few-unit, sub-half-scale sparks were invisible next to the current ship scale and speed","Worlds spread another ~1.8x farther apart on top of the last pass, with ship speed/accel/bolt speed scaled to match"]},{"v":"1.43.0","d":"Unique alien planets, redesigned home station, drag-and-drop Storage","items":["Worlds are spread another ~3.5x farther apart, with ship speed/accel/bolt speed scaled to match so travel time doesn't balloon","Every world used to be the same sphere-plus-clouds template just recolored — each now has its own real design: Vesta has jagged ice crystals jutting off the surface, Ember Reach is a cracked lava world with glowing fissures and eruption plumes instead of clouds, and Verdant Hollow has a hazy spore-cloud shell with a lumpy organic ring of growths instead of a clean planetary ring. All three got more vivid, alien colors too","Replaced the home-defense objective — the black faceted icosahedron with a green ring that read as a strange second planet — with an actual station: a hull, two solar-panel wings, a dish, and a status beacon","Rebuilt the Storage panel: it's now a Backpack grid and a Container grid side by side, both using the same icon-tile look as the Backpack panel, with drag-and-drop (or click) to move stuff between them — the old scrolling text-list \"Your Stock\" section is gone"]},{"v":"1.42.0","d":"Fixed lighting regression, Shipment Hub belts, ghost sizing, grid button, Q/E","items":["The logarithmic depth buffer added last update to fix planet flicker was itself causing lighting glitches on some hardware — reverted it and fixed the flicker a safer way (a much higher camera near plane), so the lighting should be back to normal","Fixed Shipment Hub belts not moving anything: connecting a belt always tried to pull whichever resource came first in the hub's full accepted-cargo list, even if that specific one had never actually arrived — it now connects to whatever's actually sitting in the hub","Fixed the placement hologram being smaller than the building it actually places — it was missing the same 1.45x scale-up every real building gets","Added a visible \"Grid\" button next to Remove in the Build panel to change placement snap size — it was only ever reachable through the (undiscoverable) G key on desktop","Q/E in space barely moved you at the current ship speeds (tied to a flat old-scale number) — rate now scales with the ship's own speed, and Q/E now visibly tilt the nose down/up while climbing or descending instead of the ship staying dead level","Conveyor belts now have scrolling chevron tread marks (Satisfactory-style) that animate along the belt while it's actually carrying something, instead of a static painted panel texture","Tested Fighter weapon fire directly — it does fire bolts correctly on right-click, same as every other non-Bomber ship; if it still looks like nothing's happening, that's likely the very fast bolt speed being hard to spot rather than it not firing"]},{"v":"1.41.0","d":"Fixed space camera lag and planet flicker, planets 5x bigger again","items":["The chase camera used to trail the ship by a fixed lerp rate that was tuned for the old, much slower cruise speed — at the current speeds that meant the camera fell hundreds of units behind during sustained flight, reading as it suddenly zooming way out. It now catches up fast enough to stay tight at any speed","Fixed the planets' surface flickering/glitching in space — a 1-unit-near, huge-far camera needed to see both nearby ships and the far starfield at this scale was overwhelming the standard depth buffer, so the planet's core/cloud/atmosphere layers were fighting over which one was actually in front each frame. Turned on a logarithmic depth buffer, which is built for exactly this","Every world (and home) got another 5x bigger on top of the last two scale-ups. Bumped the planet texture resolution too so it doesn't look blurry stretched over one this size, and grew the flyable space area and the home-defense fight's altitude to match so nothing clips into the now-even-bigger home planet"]},{"v":"1.40.1","d":"Rebalanced ship speed for combat and low-altitude flight","items":["The last update's big ship-speed boost was meant for crossing the huge new distances between planets — but it also carried into the home-defense dogfight and into flying low over a landed world's own terrain, both of which are still the same compact scale as before. A fast ship in either one meant crossing the whole fight, or the whole world, in under a second","The home-defense wave fight (enemy spawn distance, movement/bolt speed, attack range) was scaled up to match the new arena size instead of the old one","Flying low over a landed world now uses its own fixed speed per ship instead of a cut of the interplanetary cruise speed, so picking a landing spot or lining up a bombing run is controllable again"]},{"v":"1.40.0","d":"Colony planet recolor, real per-world power, fly-home landing, 9x bigger worlds","items":["Your home planet was a plain blue-and-white marble in space view — recolored to the same warm brown/purple/orange alien palette as everywhere else","The Build menu's close X is now much bigger and easier to hit","The PWR readout used to add up every power network on every world you'd ever built on into one number — it now only counts whichever world (or home) you're actually standing on","Coming back from space used to just fade-cut you standing at the Hub with no ship, which never made sense — you now glide back down and land at the colony the exact same way you land on any other world, ship and all, instead of teleporting home","Every world (and home) got another 9x bigger and spread another 9x farther apart on top of the earlier scale-up. Ship speed, accel, and weapon bolt speed were all boosted to match, and the home-defense wave fight was moved up to a fixed high altitude so it no longer takes place inside the now-enormous home planet's own sphere"]},{"v":"1.39.0","d":"Fixed the whole game being way too dark — this is why everything looked bad","items":["The surface scene's lighting was tuned so low that almost nothing actually read on screen — every building's detail (bolt rows, pipes, gauges, hazard stripes) was there all along, just invisible in the dark. Ambient, sun, and fill lighting were all boosted a lot, plus a proper filmic tone-map on the renderer so buildings, terrain, and (this was very likely also why the other planets' ore looked missing) ore nodes are all actually visible now","Every world's own sun brightness was scaled up to match, so landing elsewhere doesn't suddenly go dim again"]},{"v":"1.38.1","d":"Storage now only lists what's actually in it","items":["The Storage panel's container list used to show every resource type it could ever accept, most of them sitting at 0 — it now only lists what's actually stored, with a small note when it's empty"]},{"v":"1.38.0","d":"Planets are actually huge now, fast ship, fixed a takeoff bug","items":["Every planet (and home) got scaled way up and spread much farther apart — flying out now feels like crossing real distance to an actual huge world instead of a short hop to an oversized beach ball. Ship speed was boosted a lot to match, so the extra distance means a fast, dramatic flight instead of a long boring one","Fixed a bug where boarding your landed ship and trying to take off would get force-landed again within about a second — the auto-land check was reading \"still near the ground and slow, right after boarding\" as \"landed\" and slamming you back down before you could actually climb out","Fixed old colonies never getting ore on the other planets — worlds that existed before per-world ore scattering was added (or any save from before this) now get their ore backfilled in automatically on load","The flyable space area, camera draw distance, and the home defense station's position were all resized/repositioned to match the much bigger planets"]},{"v":"1.37.0","d":"Per-part building paint — a real detail editor for presets","items":["The Colors panel got a new \"Paint Parts...\" button that opens a spinnable 3D preview of any building in the game — click individual panels/frames/pieces to recolor just that part instead of the whole building at once","Every building type in the game is available to detail-paint, and your per-part choices are saved into the active preset — using that preset (with the Paint Gun or Apply to All) now paints each building with your exact part-by-part scheme instead of a single flat color","Added a Reset This Part button per selected piece to drop it back to the preset's base color"]},{"v":"1.36.0","d":"All 4 ships completely redesigned and 3x bigger","items":["Every ship (Scout, Fighter, Frigate, Bomber) got a full model rework, not just a resize — panel bolt lines, engine nozzle rings, wing hazard stripes, a cockpit frame ring, and landing pods on the Scout; a sensor dish, antenna, hull nose ram, extra point-defense guns, a 4th engine pod, and dorsal hazard striping on the Frigate; a chin sensor blister, bomb-bay struts, underwing bomb pods, and twin tail finlets on the Bomber; and wingtip gun barrels plus an engine ring/nose-cap on the Fighter","Scaled all 4 up to a full 3x their original size (up from the earlier 1.4x pass) — ground clearance was rescaled to match so the bigger hulls still land sitting on the ground instead of sinking into it"]},{"v":"1.35.1","d":"Fixed the planet/station overlap, bigger Remove button","items":["Fixed a bug where the home base's orbital defense station (the icosahedron + green ring you defend during space combat waves) was positioned right on top of your home planet's sphere, making it look like a strange growth sticking out of the planet — it's now clearly separated as its own station orbiting above the colony","The Build menu's Remove tool button was a tiny unlabeled trash icon that was easy to miss or mis-click — it's now a full-size labeled Remove button"]},{"v":"1.35.0","d":"Building paint — presets, a paint gun, and Apply to All","items":["Added a new Colors button to the HUD: create named color presets (each with a full color picker plus quick swatches), then either grab the Paint Gun and click individual buildings to recolor them, or hit Apply to All to instantly repaint every building on your colony","You can keep as many presets as you want, switch between them, and delete the ones you don't need — painting leaves each building's glowing bits (engine trails, status lights, hazard stripes' glow) alone and only recolors the plain structural panels/frames","Painted colors save and reload with your colony"]},{"v":"1.34.0","d":"All 4 ships are bigger with hazard-stripe detailing","items":["Every ship (Scout, Fighter, Frigate, Bomber) is now noticeably bigger — they used to read smaller than the player standing next to them, which looked wrong. Ground clearance was scaled to match so the bigger hulls still land sitting on the ground instead of sinking into it","Added a hazard-stripe accent band to each ship's hull for a bit more industrial/military detail"]},{"v":"1.33.0","d":"Fixed belt-upgrade highlight, rounder belt bends, and Storage redesigned as a real cargo crate","items":["Fixed the Conveyor Belt Mk.2 hover highlight — aiming at an upgradable Mk.1 belt now actually makes it glow cyan (its rail material pulses), instead of only showing a text tooltip with nothing visibly different about the belt itself","Belt bends now use a rounded swivel-hub joint instead of a flat-sided box, so turns read as a real curved corner instead of a sharp mechanical angle","Storage is now a real cargo crate — a ribbed steel container on skid feet with a hinged hatch and a pressure gauge — instead of a tapered fuel-tank silo, and its default color is grey/steel instead of brown"]},{"v":"1.32.0","d":"Wardrobe — recolor your character from the Command Hub","items":["Added a Wardrobe, opened from the Command Hub: pick a color for your Body, Arms, Legs, Head, and Eyes independently, either from quick presets or a full color picker. Changes apply instantly and save with your colony","Body/arms/legs used to share one material, so recoloring one recolored all three — they're now separate materials so each part actually customizes on its own","Added actual eyes to the player model (previously just a glowing visor with no distinct eyes) — their own color, and they blink periodically instead of sitting static","Gave the player model's materials a bit more metalness so they catch light instead of reading completely flat"]},{"v":"1.31.0","d":"Sci-fi blast doors, alien plants, new lights, a fixed gun, and a no-scroll Build menu","items":["The Pulse Blaster now actually shows something when you fire it — a visible bolt from the muzzle to whatever it hits (or the aim point, if it misses), plus muzzle flash and impact sparks. It was only ever applying damage silently before, which read as \"the gun doesn't work\"","Reworked the Door into a real sliding sci-fi blast door — two armored leaves that slide open as you approach and seal shut behind you. Interact with it to lock it permanently sealed instead of automatic","Backpack: the player preview is framed lower (less headroom wasted, more of the character visible), the Equipped boxes are smaller, and clicking an equipped item now unequips it directly instead of only being able to swap it from the Workshop","Added three new decorations: Alien Plant (five different random looks — some glow, sway, or slowly spin), Light Post, and Floor Light","Build menu: cost-to-build, the Remove tool, and the confirm button now live in an always-visible detail pane next to the grid instead of stacked below it, so browsing buildings doesn't need to scroll","Fixed a bug in the door's own open/close animation math that would have made it silently never animate (a self-referencing calculation that produced NaN on the very first frame)"]},{"v":"1.30.0","d":"Every panel gets a fixed close X, bigger models everywhere, a sidebar Build menu, and a Backpack that's an actual grid","items":["Every panel's Close button (which scrolled out of reach at the bottom of a long list) is gone — every panel now has a fixed × in the top-right corner that stays put no matter how far you scroll","Build menu categories moved from a row across the top to a sidebar on the left, and the building models in the grid are noticeably bigger and easier to make out","The Backpack's Carrying list is no longer a tall scrolling list — it's a grid of squares like the Build menu, so you can see your whole inventory at a glance. The player model preview and item icons are bigger too","The Conveyor Belt tile in the Build menu now glows and gets an upgrade badge once Belt Mk.2 is unlocked, so it's obvious there's a better tier available instead of the two tiles looking identical"]},{"v":"1.29.0","d":"Build menu moved to the left and made more transparent, a live title screen diorama, cooler buttons","items":["The Build menu now opens as a side panel on the left instead of a centered blocking modal, and it's noticeably more see-through so you can still make out the placement ghost and terrain behind it while browsing","The title screen had a small always-animating diorama added behind it — a Miner drilling, a Smelter forging (glowing, its fan actually spinning), and a lit conveyor belt actually carrying ore between them, built from the same models the real game uses. The title card is now more transparent so it's actually visible","Redesigned every button (title screen, pause menu, etc.) with a subtle gradient, a soft shadow, and a hover chevron instead of the flat single-color rectangle they were before"]},{"v":"1.28.0","d":"Every UI panel redesigned — same theme, but each one now has its own color and identity","items":["Every menu (Build, Machine, Storage, Shipyard, Workshop, Command Hub, Backpack, Shipment Launcher/Hub, Galaxy Map, Pause, Changelog) used to be the exact same gray card with a plain centered title — impossible to tell apart at a glance and all a little generic","Each panel now carries its own accent color and a small category tag under its title, grouped by what it actually is: build/unlock screens in amber, ships in blue, production machines in red, storage/inventory in green, the galaxy map in cyan, the Workshop in purple — same card layout and fonts everywhere, just color-coded so you instantly know what kind of panel you're looking at","Tightened padding and spacing across every panel (build grid, recipe cards, upgrade lists) so more fits on screen without scrolling as much"]},{"v":"1.27.1","d":"Low flight over a world now flies like flight — banking, pitch, and an engine trail","items":["Piloting the ship low over a world used to sit dead level no matter what you did — no bank into turns, no engine trail, nothing space flight didn't already have. It now banks into turns, pitches slightly with your throttle/altitude input, and trails glowing thrust particles while moving, matching what space flight already looked like"]},{"v":"1.27.0","d":"Ships can now actually be shot down, cooler bombs/explosions, and a fixed 'mystery planet'","items":["Getting shot down by an alien turret while flying over a world is now a real crash instead of the space-combat eject (which never had anywhere to send a rescue ship out there) — your ship tumbles, catches fire, trails smoke, and slams into the ground as a wreck","A wrecked ship can't be boarded or flown again. Walk up and press E to abandon it and get rescued back to your colony (you'll need to build a new ship at the Shipyard), or use the delete tool on it directly","Redesigned the bomb model — a proper finned bomb with a glowing nose instead of a plain sphere — and gave both space and surface bomb blasts a much bigger explosion: a bright flash, a wide spray of fire, and a few lingering smoke puffs","Removed the manual Return button from the space HUD — flying home now works the same way landing on any other world does, so the button was redundant","Fixed Vesta Outpost's space-view color being desert orange/tan while the ground you actually land on (and its Frost Crystal/Ice Core ore) is an ice world — it's now the icy blue-white it actually is","Fixed the 'unnamed planet you can't land on' — it was your own colony's decorative space backdrop, positioned 70 units away from the actual point flying-close-and-slow checks against, so it looked like a separate landable world that could never be landed on. It now sits right where your colony actually is"]},{"v":"1.26.0","d":"Command Hub, Refinery, and Conveyor Belt Mk.2 — a real upgrade tree","items":["Added the Command Hub — a new one-per-colony building. Build it and that's your new respawn/return point, and interacting with it opens an upgrade console","The Hub console lets you permanently unlock two things with Parts and refined metal: the Refinery (a dedicated exotic-ore processing building with better yield than the Constructor) and Conveyor Belt Mk.2 (double throughput)","Added a new Constructor recipe, Alloy Composite Parts, combining one of every ore type (including all five exotic ores) for a big parts payout","Belt Mk.2 can upgrade an existing Mk.1 belt in place — aim at it with Mk.2 selected and click, no need to remove it first","Fixed several buildings (Landing Pad, Workshop, Shipment Launcher, Shipment Hub) missing a build-menu icon entry"]},{"v":"1.25.0","d":"Sci-fi hazard-stripe detailing on buildings, 2x/4x/8x/16x grid snap, and zoop placement","items":["Added hazard-striped (black/yellow) accent bands to the Smelter, Constructor, Storage, Miner, Miner Mk2, Shipyard, and Walls — the classic industrial-factory visual cue the buildings were missing","Grid snap now has 5 steps — 1x/2x/4x/8x/16x the base tile size — cycle through them with G while placing (or the GRID button on mobile), so laying out a big base doesn't mean clicking on every single tile boundary","Placement now \"zoops\": every building keeps the tool equipped after you place it (not just walls/foundations like before), so you can drop copy after copy without reopening the build menu. Only one-per-colony buildings like the Shipyard still clear the tool automatically"]},{"v":"1.24.0","d":"Landing/arrival animations, a fixed placement hologram on other worlds, no more ship-in-the-ground, and a much bigger-looking space","items":["Fixed the building placement hologram (and mouse placement itself) doing nothing on Vesta/Ember/Verdance — the invisible plane used to figure out where your cursor is pointing only ever covered home's own footprint, so on any other world every click and every ghost preview silently missed","Your ship no longer sinks partway into the ground when it lands — it now settles at a height that actually clears its hull, tuned per ship model","Arriving at a new world is now a real descent: your ship drops in from high altitude with an engine trail instead of snapping straight to hover height","Touching down is no longer instant either — once you're low and slow enough, the ship glides the rest of the way down and settles before you're back on foot","Every planet in space now has an actual textured surface (built from its own ground palette) plus a drifting cloud layer, instead of a flat-shaded ball. Vesta got a proper double ring; Ember and Verdance don't, so the three actually look different from each other","Space itself got a pass: more stars with a brighter sparse layer for contrast, richer multi-coloured nebula clouds, and several distant galaxies visible in the deep background","Ice Core no longer looks almost identical to Frost Crystal — it's now a distinct blocky/faceted shape with its own icon, so the two are easy to tell apart at a glance"]},{"v":"1.23.2","d":"Fixed home's landing circle, invisible player after returning home, unbuildable ore on old saves, and new ore recipes","items":["The targeting circle near your own colony was never actually working — it showed, but the ring could never fill and nothing happened when it did, so flying home always felt broken. Home now uses the exact same fly-low-and-slow landing flow as every other world, and finishing it flies you straight back to walking around your colony","Fixed your player model staying invisible forever after returning home from space — the code that hides you while piloting the ship never had a matching \"show yourself again\" once you actually landed back home","Fixed Miners and buildings still rejecting placement on Vesta/Ember/Verdance if your save predated the last ore-scatter fix — old saves now pull any deposit sitting outside the buildable radius back inside it on load, instead of only fixing brand-new colonies","Frost Crystal, Magma Shard, Spore Pod, Ice Core, and Sulfur Vein now have real Constructor recipes — combine one with a bit of Iron or Copper for a batch of parts worth more than the base recipe"]},{"v":"1.23.1","d":"Fixed E opening the Shipyard mid-flight, an exit-to-space bug, and unminable ore","items":["Fixed pressing E while flying over a world re-triggering building-interact logic against your stale on-foot position (usually right next to home's Shipyard) and popping that panel open — E now does nothing while you're actually piloting the ship, same as it should","Fixed breaking for space sending your ship back toward the planet instead of away from it — the exit velocity and the exit position were pointing in opposite directions, so the ship would drift straight back into landing range and immediately re-land, which is likely what looked like \"spawning inside the planet\"","Fixed most scattered ore deposits sitting outside a world's actual buildable radius — since only the inner region allows placing buildings, a Miner could never actually be placed on them even though you could see and hand-gather them. Deposits now stay within the buildable area","The targeting circle now shows from noticeably farther away again"]},{"v":"1.23.0","d":"Bigger worlds, alien turrets, and a real fly-it-yourself departure","items":["Every world's baseplate is 4x bigger, with ore deposits scattered generously across the whole thing instead of clustered near the middle","Vesta Outpost and Ember Reach each got a second native resource — Ice Core and Sulfur Vein — on top of their original ore","Added alien flak turrets that spawn on every world and shoot at your ship while you fly low over their territory — a Bomber can blow them up from the air the same way it deals with the Hive","Fixed the space targeting circle not working for your own colony — it had a shrunken show radius meant to stop it from hogging priority over the landable worlds, which instead made it barely show at all; targeting is back to honest nearest-wins, home included","Fixed a menu (like the Shipyard) sometimes staying stuck open on screen after landing on a world","Fixed the player spawning inside the terrain on load or on touchdown — position now always matches the actual ground height instead of assuming flat ground","Reworked leaving a world: walk up to your parked ship and press E to board it, then fly it up yourself — climbing high enough breaks you back into space. Replaces the old instant Board Ship & Launch button"]},{"v":"1.22.2","d":"Worlds separated by real distance, not walls","items":["Each landable world's ground now sits tens of thousands of units from home and from every other world — genuinely far beyond fog range and the camera's own render distance, not just a short hop away with a wall blocking the view","Removed the perimeter walls — with that much real distance between worlds there's nothing nearby left to hide, so they weren't doing anything walls should be responsible for","The in-space \"fly to a planet\" view is unchanged — that's a deliberately compact system map, separate from the actual ground you land on"]},{"v":"1.22.1","d":"Every world is now a walled square baseplate, not a circle","items":["Rebuilt each landable world's terrain from a circular disc into a proper square baseplate, built the same way home's map is — its own height-mapped, vertex-colored terrain mesh with real bumps and rises, not a flat tinted patch","Boxed every world in with a solid perimeter wall on all four sides, so it unmistakably reads as its own bounded map rather than an island fading into open space","Movement (both flying and on foot) now clamps to that square footprint instead of a circular radius"]},{"v":"1.22.0","d":"Every world is now a truly separate floating platform","items":["Rebuilt each landable world as its own physically separate disc-shaped platform with a raised rim wall, instead of a tinted zone painted onto the same shared ground plane as home — flying between worlds now actually takes you somewhere else, not to a different-colored patch of the same map","Fixed a bug where the space targeting circle could get stuck on screen — approaching a world and then heading home or opening a menu (like the Shipyard) could leave the circle and its label frozen on top of the UI. It's now force-hidden the instant you're not actively flying in open space","Fixed your character model staying visible (and stuck at an old position) while you were piloting the ship over a world's surface — you're properly hidden while flying and only reappear when you actually touch down","Flying or walking now keeps you within your current world's platform — you can't drift or wander off its edge into nothing"]},{"v":"1.21.3","d":"Landing Pad is now buildable, and the targeting circle is reliable everywhere","items":["The Landing Pad is no longer fixed scenery — it's a real placeable building (under Ship Production in the Build menu), so you can put one wherever you actually want to dock, on any world","Fixed the targeting circle favoring home over whichever world you were actually flying toward — home used the same huge show radius as the landable worlds, so it kept winning \"nearest\" for most of a long approach. Home's radius is now much smaller, so the world you're flying to takes over reliably"]},{"v":"1.21.2","d":"Fly-in landings, Bomber runs over the Hive, and truly separate worlds","items":["Arriving at a world no longer cuts to a cutscene — you fly your ship in low over its actual terrain and stay in control, choosing to touch down on open ground or at the landing pad in the middle of each world's clearing","A Bomber can drop bombs while flying over a world, not just in space — line up over the Hive on Verdant Hollow and blow it up from the air","The targeting circle now appears from much farther out, so you can see it well before you're already on top of a world","Each world now runs its own tight fog range while you're on it, so home and the other worlds are no longer visible in the distance — you're genuinely somewhere else, not a colored patch on the home map"]},{"v":"1.21.1","d":"Real freeform landing, and bigger, fully separate landscapes","items":["Landing no longer needs you to aim and hold a lock — fly low, slow down near a world, and you settle wherever you actually came to a stop, so you control exactly where you touch down","Flying fast and low near a world (a bombing run, for instance) no longer forces a landing — only stopping does, so you can freely buzz a world without getting yanked out of the sky","Fixed the targeting circle not reliably showing up near Ember Reach and Verdant Hollow — it was tied to an aiming requirement that's gone now, so all three worlds (and home) show it the same way","Every world's walkable footprint is much bigger, and no longer fades back toward home's colors until well past the edge of where you can actually walk — each one reads as its own separate landscape, not a tinted patch on the home map"]},{"v":"1.21.0","d":"Three worlds to explore, fly-in landing, a Bomber, and a hostile Hive","items":["Two brand new landable worlds join Vesta Outpost: Ember Reach, a volcanic world with molten-glass spires and native Magma Shard deposits, and Verdant Hollow, a jungle world with glowing fern growths and native Spore Pod deposits","Every world now has its own sky, lighting, ground palette and decorations, so each one feels like a genuinely different place the moment you land — not a recolored copy of home","Your home colony is now a visible planet in space too, not just an abstract defended structure","The targeting circle now names whichever world you're approaching, home included — worlds you haven't landed on yet before show as UNDISCOVERED WORLD until you actually touch down there","Landing is no longer instant: fly in, hold the lock, and use Q/E to lower or raise your altitude — you only touch down once you're locked on AND low enough, so it actually feels like flying in for a landing","New ship: the Bomber. It drops heavy, high-damage bombs instead of firing bolts — press fire while flying to release one, arcing down and exploding for area damage","Verdant Hollow has a hostile Hive that periodically spawns monsters — they'll come after you if you get close, so gathering there is riskier than the other two worlds","New equipment slot: Weapon. Craft the Pulse Blaster at the Workshop and left-click while on foot to fire at the nearest monster (or the Hive itself) in front of you","Fixed a rare soft-lock where the Launch button could stop responding after landing"]},{"v":"1.20.0","d":"Touch down on a real frozen outpost, with a brand new resource","items":["Landing now automatically teleports you onto an actual walkable planet surface — no menus, no waiting, you just touch down and you're standing there","This second world looks and feels distinct from home: a pale icy ground instead of home's warm alien rust, with frost spikes and frost-crusted boulders instead of purple crystal and mushrooms","New resource: Frost Crystal, native to this outpost — gather it alongside the same Iron Ore and Copper Ore you already know, so it's a mix of old and new materials to build with","Frost Crystal works everywhere the other raw materials do — Storage, Splitters, Mergers, and both Shipment buildings all accept it","Your ship is parked right at your landing spot, tilted back on its tail like it's mid-countdown — press Board Ship & Launch when you're ready to fly, and it takes off for real","Saving and reloading while you're out on the outpost puts you right back there, ship and all"]},{"v":"1.19.2","d":"Landing actually looks like landing now","items":["Your ship now visibly tilts to rest flush against the planet's curved surface instead of just floating upright next to it","The camera switches to a close, grounded angle while landed so the horizon and surface read clearly, instead of the same free-flight chase cam","Added a dust-burst effect the moment you touch down","Launching cleanly resets the ship back to level flight, no leftover tilt"]},{"v":"1.19.1","d":"Simplified planet landing — no more disembark/board-ship flow","items":["Removed the disembark-and-walk-around-on-Vesta mechanic entirely — it added a confusing extra scene-switch that didn't reliably work","Landing now does exactly one thing: fly to the planet, hold the lock until the circle fills, and your ship touches down and parks right there — a single Launch button appears while you're landed","Pressing Launch plays the same liftoff animation as before and drops you back into free flight near the planet","You never leave your ship or the space scene anymore — it's a clean, reliable land-and-launch loop instead of a multi-step handoff"]},{"v":"1.19.0","d":"Real launch/landing cinematics, bigger ships and shipping buildings","items":["Landing on Vesta Outpost now happens automatically the moment the lock-on circle fills — no more confirmation panel, the screen just fades and you're standing on the surface","Boarding your ship at Vesta now launches you straight back into space in one action — press Board Ship & Launch and watch it happen, no extra menu","Every launch (from the home Shipyard or from Vesta) now plays a real liftoff: the ship visibly rises and accelerates, engines flare and trail sparks, and the camera pulls back and chases behind it","Rebuilt all three ship models (Scout, Fighter, Frigate) from scratch with far more detail — proper hulls, cockpit canopies, wings, engine nacelles, and blinking red/green nav lights — and made them noticeably bigger","Flying feels more alive: the chase camera now smoothly follows instead of snapping, your ship banks into turns, and a thrust particle trail streams out the engines while flying or boosting","The Shipment Launcher and Shipment Hub are both dramatically bigger and easier to tell apart, and each now has a permanent glowing marker baked into the model — green intake funnel on the Launcher's input side, red spout on the Hub's output side — visible all the time, not just while placing a belt","Shrank the Backpack's character preview further and enlarged every item icon again for easier reading"]},{"v":"1.18.0","d":"Land on Vesta Outpost, and ship goods between planets","items":["You can now disembark from your ship after landing on Vesta Outpost — step out onto its surface, gather from resource deposits scattered around the landing site, and place buildings there exactly like at home. Board Ship & Launch returns you to space when you're ready","New building: the Shipment Launcher. Belt materials into it, pick a Shipment Hub as its destination, and every 14 seconds it launches its whole buffered load over — open it (E) to see what's loaded and choose or change the target Hub","New building: the Shipment Hub. Receives everything launched at it from any Shipment Launcher aimed its way. Open it (E) to see what's arrived, rename it, collect its contents straight to your backpack, or belt its output into your factory","Both new buildings are under a new Shipping category in the Build menu"]},{"v":"1.17.3","d":"Belt arrows only while placing belts, chain-place structural pieces","items":["The green/red input/output arrows on every building now only show up while the Conveyor Belt tool is actually selected, instead of being visible all the time","Placing a Foundation, Wall, Window, Door or Railing now keeps that tool equipped afterward so you can keep placing more without reopening the Build menu each time — machines and ships still clear back to nothing after one placement, since those are normally a one-off decision"]},{"v":"1.17.2","d":"The game no longer pauses when a menu is open, and better item sizing","items":["Opening any menu used to stop the whole world, not just your own movement - clouds, spores and pools stopped animating, and hand-gathering stopped even while standing right on a deposit. The camera, world animation, hand-gathering and building/jump simulation now all keep running the moment a panel is open; only your own WASD movement is still locked while a fullscreen panel like Build or Workshop is up, so you can't wander around blind underneath it","Shrank the character preview in the Backpack and enlarged every item icon (Carrying, Equipped slots, and the Workshop's craft list) so the actual items read clearly instead of the character render dominating the panel"]},{"v":"1.17.1","d":"Real gear slots, 3D item models, a character preview, and a real jump-freeze fix","items":["FIXED: jumping and opening any menu (Build, Backpack, anything) used to freeze you suspended in mid-air until you closed it — gravity only ran in the surface update loop, which stops while a panel is open. Jump physics now live in the loop that always runs, so you keep falling normally with a menu open","Every resource and every craftable now renders as an actual small 3D model (the same ore-chunk/ingot/gear shapes used elsewhere in the game) instead of a flat icon, in both the Backpack and the Workshop","Gear is now three real equipment slots — Head, Hands, Legs — instead of a flat list. Running Boots go on Legs, Cargo Gloves on Hands, and the old \"Haul Pack\" is now the Scanner Visor, worn on the Head","The Backpack now shows a live rendered snapshot of your actual character at the top, gear and all","Equipping something now visibly changes your character model on the spot — boots add a glowing anklet, gloves a wristband, the visor a glowing browband — and it updates immediately, no menu reopen needed","Note: save files from 1.17.0's boots/gloves/pack format won't carry over to the new head/hands/legs slots - that version shipped for less than a day, so this is a one-time reset rather than an ongoing migration concern"]},{"v":"1.17.0","d":"Jumping, a Workshop, craftable gear, and a Backpack","items":["You can jump now (Space) — real gravity-driven physics, not a hop animation","New building: the Workshop, a lean-to garage with a real bench, vice and a spinning grinder wheel. Interact with it (E) to craft permanent gear","Three craftable upgrades so far: Running Boots (+35% move speed, +50% jump height), Cargo Gloves (+80% hand-gathering rate), and a Haul Pack (+50% hand-gathering range) — each a one-time craft from your stockpile that's worn the moment it's made","New Backpack panel (press B, or the HUD button) — pops up on the left side of the screen without blocking the rest of the view, showing what you're wearing and your full carried stock. Production keeps running while it's open, same as every other panel","Equipped gear is saved and restored with your colony","Gave the player a real headlamp point light (not just an emissive mesh) so the wild terrain out past the buildable clearing reads better under the new dusk lighting"]},{"v":"1.16.0","d":"The colony now sits on an actual alien world","items":["New sky: a violet-to-rust gradient with faint nebula bands, a ringed gas giant and a cratered moon hanging near the horizon, and 1600 colour-varied stars instead of a flat white scatter","New lighting: the single warm sun is now joined by a cool magenta rim light standing in for a second, more distant star, so shadowed sides of buildings and terrain read as alien dusk instead of flat brown-on-brown","The wild terrain beyond the buildable clearing got a full pass: six fixed crater bowls, tall angular cliff stacks, ordinary boulders now veined with glowing purple/cyan crystal, 24 freestanding crystal clusters, giant bioluminescent mushrooms with spotted caps and glowing spore pods, scattered wreck debris, and seven ruined arches/obelisks marked with a faint glowing rune","Six pools of glowing alien liquid with a slow shimmering pulse, and ~70 drifting bio-spore motes across the whole map for ambience","Ground colours shifted to an alien palette (rust regolith, teal lichen, violet mineral crust) while keeping the buildable clearing itself readable against buildings","None of this touches the flat buildable clearing or its footprint - every new crater, cliff, ruin and pool is confined to r > 0.7x the clearing radius, so existing colonies, belts and sightlines are unaffected"]},{"v":"1.15.0","d":"Rebuilt Smelter, Constructor and Storage from scratch","items":["The Smelter is now an asymmetric blast furnace: a tapered tank leaning under an offset main chimney, a smaller secondary flue, an angled ore hopper on one side, and a slow-spinning induction fan on its flank that keeps turning whenever it's powered","The Constructor is now a cantilevered stamping rig: an angled discharge chute juts out to one side instead of a centered slot, and the stamp head is a real piston that physically slides down and back up each time it actually completes a part - not just a spinning cube","Storage is now a squat fuel-tank silo with corrugated rib bands, a domed cap, a working pressure gauge, a side ladder, and an offset sliding hatch instead of a plain crate with a centered door","FIXED: the Constructor's own recipe needs Iron and Copper at the same time, but 1.14.0 only gave it one belt input port - it could never actually receive both at once. It now has two input ports on its input side, matching the Merger's layout","Miner, Miner Mk.2, Splitter, Merger, Pylon, Solar and the Shipyard already used non-boxy silhouettes before this pass and are unchanged here - next up if you want the same full rebuild treatment applied to those too"]},{"v":"1.14.1","d":"Bigger buildings, port direction arrows","items":["Buildings are noticeably bigger again (about 23% more on top of 1.14.0's bump) - more room to actually see the detail already on each model","Every belt port on every building, Splitter and Merger now shows a small floating arrow: green pointing in for inputs, red pointing out for outputs, right at the exact spot a belt will connect to","Fixed a scale mismatch this uncovered: belts, cables and smoke were still anchoring to buildings' pre-1.14.0 (smaller) size, so the connection point silently drifted from the bigger model - all three now scale with the building"]},{"v":"1.14.0","d":"Fixed belt ports, bigger buildings, smelter smoke","items":["Conveyor belts now use fixed input/output ports per machine, Satisfactory-style, instead of aiming straight at whatever's on the other end — a belt always leaves and enters a building perpendicular to its assigned side, then bends to reach the other machine at any angle, so it never cuts into a wall on a diagonal","Splitters and Mergers now actually enforce their advertised port counts (1 in/2 out and 2 in/1 out) — connecting a third belt to an already-full side is rejected with a clear message instead of silently overlapping","Every building is now about 18% larger (structural pieces like foundations and walls are untouched so grid tiling stays exact)","The Smelter now puffs real smoke from its chimney while actively smelting","Right-click suppression hardened further with a third, independent layer (an inline handler directly on <body>) on top of the two script-based listeners from 1.13.1","Re-confirmed production keeps ticking (resources, belt flow, and animations) while the Build, Machine, and Storage panels are open — tested through several real seconds of wall-clock time with each panel open, not just a single tick"]},{"v":"1.13.1","d":"Textured buildings, hardened right-click, verified belt fix","items":["Every building, prop and ship now has a subtle scuffed-metal surface instead of a flat colour fill","The browser's native right-click menu is now blocked by a second listener registered before any other script on the page runs, so an unrelated error elsewhere can no longer leave it able to leak through","Re-verified the belt-to-building connection fix from 1.13.0 end-to-end through a real Miner → Smelter → power setup - ore visibly flows and the smelter produces iron with no gap in the belt mesh"]},{"v":"1.13.0","d":"Real flight controls, a landable planet, belts that connect, and a real ground","items":["Conveyor belts now visually plug into the machines they connect instead of stopping short with a gap","Fixed a lingering movement/camera-drag glitch that could get stuck on after holding Shift and right-clicking at the same time","Reworked PC ship flight: A/D turn the ship, W/S thrust forward and back, and right-click fires — no more mouse-look","Added a real objective in space: fly toward Vesta Outpost, keep it centered to fill the landing ring, and touch down. Your ship stays parked there until you launch back","Replaced the bright grid overlay on the ground with an actual tiled terrain texture"]},{"v":"1.12.0","d":"Movement/input fixes, Remove tool feedback, hardened saves","items":["Fixed right-click opening the browser's native context menu — it now only ever cancels placement/tools","Fixed the Build menu flickering while hovering items — icons and tabs now update in place instead of being torn down and rebuilt on every timer tick","The Remove tool now shows the targeted building's name and exactly what materials it will refund before you click","Saves now remember exactly where you were standing and facing, keep a backup copy so a corrupted save can't wipe your colony, and show a small Saving.../Saved indicator","Starting a new colony over an existing save now asks for confirmation first","The Shipyard now waits for power before starting construction, shows build progress as a percentage, and displays the finished ship docked in place until you launch it"]},{"v":"1.11.2","d":"Splitters, mergers, Miner Mk.2, and better logistics","items":["Added a Splitter (one belt in, up to two out) and a Merger (up to two belts in, one out) as real industrial Y-junction devices","Added Miner Mk.2 — a much larger, multi-drill rig with 4x the output of Mk.1 at triple the power cost. Rebalanced Mk.1 to its intended 30 Iron Ore/min at 20 MW","Belt items now have real shapes instead of a plain cube: jagged rock chunks for raw ore, smooth bars for ingots","Selecting a belt or cable connection target now highlights it green (valid) or red (invalid) before you click, and aiming at machines is noticeably more accurate","You can now take an item off a belt (E) or place one from your stock onto a belt (F / the mobile DROP button)"]},{"v":"1.10.0","d":"Icon-based machine and storage panels","items":["Recipe selection in the Smelter/Constructor now uses icon cards instead of a text list, with icon-based input/output slots showing exactly what's flowing where","Production targets got a real +/- stepper (with -10/-1/+1/+10 and direct entry) instead of a plain number box, and changing the target no longer wipes your progress","The Storage panel now shows every resource with a proper item icon instead of a plain color dot"]},{"v":"1.9.0","d":"Fixed stuck movement, redesigned Build menu","items":["Fixed a real bug where the player could keep moving on their own after releasing every key — a missed keyup (losing window focus, switching tabs) could leave a direction stuck on. Movement now resets on blur, tab-hide, and whenever a menu opens","Completely redesigned the Build menu: a compact icon grid with category tabs (Structure/Power/Production/Logistics/Ship) instead of one long scrolling list","Selecting an item shows a detail panel with description, power info, and a cost breakdown that turns red on anything you're short on","The Build button now reads INSUFFICIENT MATERIALS when you can't afford something, instead of just going gray"]},{"v":"1.8.0","d":"Walls, windows, doors, and railings","items":["Added Wall, Window Wall, Door, and Railing — the first structural pieces beyond Foundation","They snap to the edge of a grid cell instead of the center, with automatic orientation based on where you aim","The Door has a real open-frame look with a swung-open leaf; the Window has a glass viewport you can see through"]},{"v":"1.7.0","d":"Simpler building, menus don't freeze the factory","items":["Removed the fabrication queue — buildings, belts, and cables are placed directly again, paying their cost immediately instead of waiting on a timer","Removing a building now refunds half its materials straight to your stock","Opening the Build, Machine, Storage, or Shipyard menu no longer freezes the factory — power, belts, and production keep running behind the panel"]},{"v":"1.6.0","d":"Real storage","items":["Storage no longer silently drains into your stock — it holds real contents up to its capacity","Walk up and interact with a Storage Container to open it: see your stock and its contents side by side, with Take/Store per resource or Take All/Store All","The storage door physically swings open and shut when you open and close the panel"]},{"v":"1.5.0","d":"Crafted economy & landing site","items":["Buildings, belts, and cables are now real inventory items — fabricate them from raw resources over time, then place them from what you own","Added a Remove Building tool that deconstructs a placed building and returns it (and any attached belts/cables) to your inventory","Smelter and Constructor can now target a specific production quantity with a progress/ETA readout, or run continuously","Added a landing pod, scorch marks, supply crates, and abandoned equipment around the starting area"]},{"v":"1.4.0","d":"Menu overhaul","items":["Reworked the pause screen into a real menu, including a Return to Title option that was missing entirely","Added this changelog, readable from the title screen or the pause menu","Added a link back to the Meat Lad site from the title screen"]},{"v":"1.3.0","d":"Recipes & machine status","items":["The Smelter and Constructor now require you to pick a recipe by hand — nothing runs automatically anymore","Added a machine panel showing exact input/output rates in units per minute, power draw, a live status light, and cycle progress","Added Foundation floor tiles that snap edge-to-edge into a factory floor"]},{"v":"1.2.0","d":"Power cables","items":["Removed automatic range-based power — a machine only gets power if you physically cable it to a generator","Cables sag like real power lines and glow blue when powered, red when the grid is overloaded"]},{"v":"1.1.0","d":"Bigger, better factory","items":["Doubled the build grid and rebuilt every machine bigger with more mechanical detail","Resource deposits are now large, distinct mineral formations instead of tiny rocks, and the Miner visibly drills into them","Conveyor belts got real frames with rollers and support legs","Rebuilt the terrain, skybox, lighting, and the player's spacesuit and animations"]},{"v":"1.0.0","d":"3D rework","items":["Rebuilt the game in 3D from the ground up","Land on your planet, gather resources by hand, build a factory, then launch a ship into space combat"]}]},"meatlad":{"nm":"Meat Lad","href":"meatlad.html","entries":[{"v":"4.1.0","d":"latest · Oct 28, 2026","items":["ENHANCED GRAPHICS GOES FURTHER. New models for everything: bolted metal spikes with a glint down each edge, real circular saws with hooked teeth and a bolted hub, thick spinning coins with a raised star, laser housings whose lens warms up before they fire, and crumbling blocks whose cracks glow before they give","SHE'S DRAWN PROPERLY NOW - same squishy body as you, with blinks, a shadow, and she bounces and waves when you get close. In the last fight she's sealed in a proper block of ice","THE BOSSES GOT REMODELLED. The Carver is a giant saw with a furious face throwing sparks, The Butcher is a slab of meat in a stained apron who raises a cleaver before he drops, and The Suit has shaded armour and a visor glint","THE LAD stretches when he flies, his eyes follow where he's going, and his feet go red after he's walked through blood","BLOOD IN ENHANCED: the wound keeps pulsing out jets for a moment, hard drops kick up little crown splashes, blood gathers into beads on ceilings and drips back down, fresh pools are wet and shiny and dry out, stains get streaks in the direction they flew, and chunks are marbled with the odd bit of bone","THE LEVEL MAP IN ENHANCED has a skyline for each world - smoking chimneys, glowing rot hills, snowy peaks under an aurora - a road between levels, and raised badges with stars for cleared levels, padlocks for locked ones and a pulsing skull for bosses","Respawning in Enhanced no longer blacks out the whole screen for a moment"]},{"v":"4.0.0","d":"Oct 28, 2026","items":["CHAPTER 3: THE DEEP FREEZE. The Butcher is down but the suit still has her, and he's dragged her into cold storage. Twenty new levels with a new frozen look, falling snow and its own music","FOUR NEW MECHANICS. Ice you slide on and can't grip as a wall, conveyor belts that carry you, springs that launch you twice a jump's height, and icicles that shake and drop when you run underneath","NEW FINAL BOSS - THE SUIT. He hovers on a jetpack dropping ice shards, then dives and sends a frost wave along the floor. Jump the wave and stomp his head while he's dazed. Beat him and the story gets its ending","ENHANCED GRAPHICS (Settings). A whole new skin: painted backdrops for every world, detailed tiles (moss, goo, snow and frost), glowing lights, drifting dust, spores and snow, a shadow, lean and blinks for the lad, motion-blurred saws, death shockwaves, an iris wipe between lives, colour grading and a restyled menu. Classic is still there","TRAILS - a new wardrobe category. Snowfall, Sparkle, Frost, Embers, Hearts, Rainbow and Bubbles follow you around as you move","NEW COSMETICS. Frostbite, Aurora and Chrome meat, Ushanka, Earmuffs, Snow Pile, Ice Crown and the Suit Helmet, Ice Skates, Snow Boots and Moon Boots, plus Frozen eyes","A REAL JUMP BUTTON ON MOBILE. The faint red half-screen box is gone - there's a proper round button with an arrow that presses in when you hold it (a tap anywhere on that side still works as a backup)","Boss health bars across the top of the screen, your best time for each level on the map, and NEW BEST on the clear screen","The level editor has the new pieces too: Ice, both Belts, Spring and Icicle, and its Check button understands them","Fixed an error that fired every frame on the menu before you'd played a level, and dust from crumbling floors that was never actually visible"]},{"v":"3.3.0","d":"Oct 11, 2026, 11:14 AM ET","items":["FRIENDS LIST ON EVERY MULTIPLAYER GAME. The main menu now has a friends panel down the side with an Add friend button - add someone by the username they picked at sign-up. It's one list shared by Meat Lad, Beetdown, Alien Attack and Starforge, so a friend you add once shows up everywhere","INVITE FRIENDS TO YOUR PARTY. While you have an online party open, a mini friends list sits at the side of the menus with a green dot for whoever is online and what they're playing, plus an Invite button","INVITES POP UP WHEREVER YOUR FRIEND IS - in this game, another game or the main menu - and Join takes them straight into your party, opening Meat Lad for them if they were somewhere else"]},{"v":"3.2.3","items":["FIXED CLOUD SAVES SILENTLY NEVER LOADING. The code asked Firestore whether a document existed by calling exists() as a function, but in this version of the SDK it is a plain property - so the call threw, got swallowed by the error handler, and your online save was quietly ignored every single time. Progress made on another device will come back now"]},{"v":"3.2.2","items":["MENU MUSIC REWORKED. It was just floaty sustained chords with barely any rhythm - added a soft heartbeat-like pulse, a plucked bass instead of one long drone, a light shaker texture, and an actual melodic hook so the theme has real identity instead of drifting"]},{"v":"3.2.1","items":["IN-GAME MUSIC IS MORE INTENSE. Faster tempo in both worlds, a pounding double-hit kick pattern, an added snare/clap, denser hats and a relentless arpeggio - matches the game a lot better than the earlier, calmer pass"]},{"v":"3.2.0","items":["ORIGINAL MUSIC. Separate menu, World 1 and World 2 themes now play automatically as you move between them - a lighter menu theme, driving industrial percussion in the Meat Works, and darker, more dissonant tones in the Rot. All written from scratch with the Web Audio API, no samples"]},{"v":"3.1.0","items":["NEW UI, MORE MOBILE FRIENDLY. Fixed double-tap accidentally zooming or selecting text, and dragging on menus no longer triggers the browser's pinch-zoom - both were mobile browser default behaviours that had never been switched off","PC OR MOBILE - pick on the main game-select page (or change it anytime from the button there). This overrides the automatic touch-vs-mouse detection for anyone it was guessing wrong for, like touchscreen laptops"]},{"v":"3.0.6","items":["THE MAIN GAME-SELECT PAGE NOW RUNS ON THE SAME FIREBASE ACCOUNT AS BOTH GAMES. It was quietly running its own separate, older login system this whole time - signing in there did nothing for Beetdown or Meat Lad. Sign in once on the main page now and both games recognise you immediately, no second sign-in anywhere","SETTINGS NO LONGER OFFERS A FAKE SIGN-IN. When you're not signed in it just says so and points you to the main page, instead of showing a Sign In button with nowhere to go"]},{"v":"3.0.5","items":["SIGN-IN MOVED TO FIREBASE, and the login screen is gone entirely. Sign in once on the main game-select page and Meat Lad now recognises that same account automatically the moment you open it - no separate sign-in step, no login screen blocking the way in","SAVES AND FRIENDS NOW GENUINELY SHARE ONE ACCOUNT DOCUMENT WITH BEETDOWN instead of a separate, unrelated backend. Progress made here shows up under the same account there, and the friends list is one list, not two","FIXED A BROKEN SCRIPT. A batch of curly/smart quotes had snuck into the changelog and error text and were breaking the page's JavaScript outright - that's why buttons and sign-in looked completely dead. All fixed","The home button now reads \"Back to Main Menu\" and points right, since Meat Lad has nothing after it to go \"forward\" to"]},{"v":"3.0.4","items":["FIXED SIGN-IN. \"Can't reach the account server\" was real - the account index had been silently deleted by the free host it lives on (it enforces a strict 24-hour lifetime with no way to renew it, which nobody realised until it actually expired). A fresh index is in place and sign-in works again. Any account made before this fix is gone - sorry, there was no way to recover it. This backend is not durable long-term and needs a proper home","COMPACT HOME BUTTON. Back button is now a small styled pill in the top-right corner with a right-pointing arrow, matching Meat Lad's colour scheme with a flip animation on hover. Takes up way less screen space while staying easy to reach"]},{"v":"3.0.3","items":["Friends are now genuinely one list with Beetdown - add a friend there and they show up here too, and back. Fixed a real risk this uncovered: saving progress here used to overwrite the WHOLE shared account, which could have silently wiped a signed-in player's Beetdown progress. It merges instead of overwriting now","Signing in now works even if the account was first created in Beetdown - it used to refuse with \"couldn't read the save\" if Meat Lad had never been played on that account yet"]},{"v":"3.0.2","items":["ONE SIGN-IN COVERS BOTH GAMES NOW. Sign in on the game-select screen, or in either game, and the others recognise the same session without asking again - nothing about signing in or out here changed, this only widens what counts as \"already signed in\""]},{"v":"3.0.1","items":["THE GIRL IS GONE FROM PASS THE BOMB. The arenas still had an exit marker in them, so running into her handed the bomb over and muddled the round. Every bomb arena is now just an arena - there is nothing to reach, only the fuse","DYING WITH THE BOMB KEEPS IT. It used to jump to the other player the instant you died, which meant blowing up could hand your opponent the problem. Now it stays with whoever went down, and they lose the round"]},{"v":"3.0.0","items":["WORLD 2 IS NOW 20 LEVELS with THE BUTCHER waiting at 20, same shape as World 1. The ten new ones are all different from each other - a crawl under a low ceiling, a drop through holes in the floor, tower-to-tower hops, a laser corridor, crumbling catwalks and a final climb using the lot","Found and fixed an old level while I was at it: STROBE was impossible. Its three laser emitters had nothing to stop their beams, so each one covered its entire row and there was no gap to walk through. Rebuilt with the beams capped short","Every one of the 40 levels is now checked by a simulator that plays them with the saws moving, the lasers blinking on their real timers, and the crumbling floors tested both intact and already fallen. A level only ships if it can be finished in both states","PASS THE BOMB HAS ITS OWN FIVE ARENAS - The Pit, Tower Block, Crossroads, The Rings and Hot Potato. Open and loopy so you can actually circle each other, instead of borrowing a campaign level built for running one way","Set up a match before you start: 1 round, best of 3 or best of 5, and either pick the arena or have it change every round. Online, the host's setup is sent to everyone so both sides play the same match on the same arena","Five new cosmetics for playing it - Lit Fuse, Blast Boots, Blast Eyes, Bomb Hat and the TNT meat colour, earned by playing, passing and winning","PARTY DEATHS NOW COUNT FROM WHEN THE PARTY STARTED. It was adding up everyone's lifetime totals, so it read in the hundreds the moment you joined. Everyone's total is noted as they arrive and only what happens after that counts"]},{"v":"2.9.3","items":["BLOOD IS NO LONGER A LIQUID SIM. Standing in a puddle made it multiply and creep outwards - puddles were a simulated volume that levelled out sideways and poured off ledges, and the spill spawned a drip that settled and added the same volume straight back. It recirculated forever. Gone","Blood now splatters and stays put. It sprays wide on death, marks whatever it hits, and never moves again once it lands","Blood that hits a wall clings to the face and runs down it, and where a run reaches the ground it leaves a pool on the floor","Pools are painted once as an irregular blobby mark instead of an animated oval, and are capped per level so a long session can't pile up"]},{"v":"2.9.2","items":["FOUND THE REAL FREEZE - the one where you're stuck in midair right before the blood shows up. It was never an error, which is why it kept surviving my fixes. The game measures how long each frame took, and on some devices that measurement can come back as a negative number. That negative number was being subtracted from the hit-pause timer, so instead of counting down to zero the pause grew, and grew, forever. The game kept drawing but stopped updating: you hang in the air, the blood never moves","The frame timer is now clamped so a bad or backwards clock reading can't stall the game, and the leftover-time counter can't run away either","Added a watchdog: if you're ever dead for more than 3 seconds without respawning, the level resets itself. Dying can't be a dead end again, whatever goes wrong","One bad frame can no longer take the whole game down - errors in the loop are caught, cleaned up and skipped instead of stopping everything"]},{"v":"2.9.1","items":["FIXED THE FREEZE. My blood rewrite accidentally deleted the line defining the blood colours, so every single death threw an error and killed the game loop the instant you died. Restored","Blood drawing is batched into one clipped pass instead of one per particle, and the death spray uses fewer, slightly larger droplets - a death now costs well under a millisecond a frame"]},{"v":"2.9.0","items":["BLOOD IS NOW A FLUID. It was never actually flowing - it stamped oval shapes wherever a droplet landed and left them there. Puddles are now a real volume that collects on floors, levels out sideways, and pours off ledges to the floor below","Found the real reason it went through walls: the flying droplets and gibs were drawn with NO clipping at all. My last fix only covered the baked marks. Every layer is now clipped to open space","Blood runs down walls properly. A droplet that hits a wall grabs on, slides down the face leaving a streak, lets go when the wall ends, and pools where it lands","Die up high and the blood makes its way down as far as it can reach, pooling on each surface and spilling over the edges","Puddles draw as one connected body with a rippling surface and wet highlight instead of a heap of separate ovals","The saw that kills you now has blood caught in its teeth, smeared round the hub and a drip swinging off the bottom, instead of just being tinted red","Blood volume is conserved when it moves and capped per level, so it cannot multiply forever"]},{"v":"2.8.0","items":["BLOOD REBUILT. It was clipped to a small window around each splat, so anything landing further out painted straight over the walls. There is now one clip region per level covering only open space - blood physically cannot touch a solid block any more","Puddles merge instead of stacking. Blood landing near an existing puddle grows it and drifts it, rather than dropping another oval on top - that is what caused the pile of ovals at the bottom","Blood no longer hangs in mid-air. If it lands with nothing underneath, runners break off and trickle down to the first floor below and start a puddle there","Spikes now draw ON TOP of blood instead of under it, so blood sits behind them and you can see both - no cut-out holes around hazards","The exact spike or saw that killed you keeps wet blood on it, so when you respawn you can see where you died"]},{"v":"2.7.1","items":["FIXED: damage zones did nothing. The damage only counted if the boss smashed a solid block in that cell - and a saw is not solid, so a zone placed on a saw could never fire","Zones now hurt the boss the moment it touches one, whether that cell holds a block, a saw, or nothing at all. Put one over a saw and the saw appears to do the killing","One hit per zone with a short cooldown, so a single zone cannot drain the whole health bar"]},{"v":"2.7.0","items":["FIXED the friends system - accounts made before friends existed were never registered, so nobody could be found by name. Signing in now registers you automatically","Invite buttons moved into the host's party screen, next to the mode and level picker, where you actually need them","The full cutscene now only plays after the World 1 boss. Every other level ends with him stomping in and carrying her off inside the level itself, Meat Boy style","Damage zones are now an invisible overlay instead of a block - red dashed outline in the editor, nothing at all in game. You can paint one straight on top of a saw so it looks like the saw is what kills the boss","When the editor is unlocked, Edit this level appears in the pause menu too","The level map now reopens on the level you last played instead of jumping to the newest one"]},{"v":"2.6.0","items":["Damage zones - invisible markers you paint in the editor (red dashed outline there, nothing in-game). The World 1 boss only loses health when it smashes into one, and you can put them on top of a saw so it looks like the saw kills it","Coins - 27 of them hidden across 9 levels, with a counter in the corner while you play","4 secret items that only appear when you find coins: 1 coin gives the Coin Crown, 3 gives Star Eyes, 5 gives the Gold Halo, 8 gives Treasure meat","Cutscene - a figure in a robot suit takes the girl. Full dramatic version after the World 1 boss, and a shorter swipe at the end of every World 2 level. Tap to skip","Both bosses redrawn - the blade has proper teeth and a glowing core, the Butcher has a visor, chest light and antenna","Wardrobe: added noses, mouths and ears (6 / 8 / 6 of them), two new eye styles, and 21 colours for every single part","The level editor is now hidden. Press 1, 0 and L together, then enter the password","Friends - add people by username in the Multiplayer menu, then invite them straight into your party","Usernames are now unique, so two people can't take the same name","You get a popup when a friend invites you, but only while you're in a menu or paused - never mid-jump"]},{"v":"2.5.0","items":["LEVEL EDITOR - you can now build the levels yourself. Open Singleplayer, pick a level, and hit Edit","Place blocks, all four spike directions, patrolling saws, vertical saws, spinners, crumbling floors, lasers, the spawn and the exit. Tap or drag to paint","Your version replaces the built-in level for real - it is what you play, and the map marks it 'your version'","CHECK button actually plays the level for you and tells you if it can be finished, saws and all. If you wall off the exit it will say so","TEST drops you straight into your level without saving, so you can try ideas","UNDO ALL puts the original level back whenever you want","Your levels save with your account, so they follow you to other devices"]},{"v":"2.4.0","items":["FIXED: in online play your partner was drawn as a faded ghost - they now look like a solid, real player","All 19 World 1 levels rebuilt again. Every one now has spikes, a saw or a spinner, and they are ordered easiest to hardest by an actual measurement instead of guesswork","The level checker now simulates the saws moving too, not just the walls and gaps. It caught a level that genuinely could not be finished because a saw blocked the only route - that is fixed","Eyes are now a thing: Normal, Happy, Angry, Sad, Shocked, Sleepy and X Eyes, and you can pick the pupil colour","Every hat and every pair of shoes can be recoloured - 13 colours each, or leave it original","6 new hats (Ball Cap, Bow, Antenna, Bucket, Beret, Hard Hat) and 3 new shoes (Wellies, Sandals, Tall Boots)","Wardrobe rebuilt again - colour pickers sit under your character where the empty space was, and the item icons show the piece in the colour you chose"]},{"v":"2.3.0","items":["New level select - a map like Mario, where levels sit on a winding path, light up as you beat them, and your character walks over to whichever one you pick. World 1 and World 2 are separate pages with their own look and a slide between them","Main menu rebuilt: Singleplayer and Multiplayer up top, then Changelog, Settings and Wardrobe. Multiplayer asks Couch or Online. Settings holds your account, sound and stats","All 19 World 1 levels redesigned with their own layouts - gaps, shafts, spike floors, ledges, saw corridors - and they ramp up gently instead of all feeling the same","FIXED: boss levels were zoomed way out. The camera now follows you at a normal size and scrolls along the corridor","FIXED: your partner's outfit never updated online - if they changed hats mid-game you kept seeing the old one","The host's party screen is much more compact, so Start is reachable without scrolling","Every level is still run through the physics simulator - all 28 non-boss levels are proven completable"]},{"v":"2.2.0","items":["Accounts now work on any device. Sign in with the same username and password on another phone or computer and your progress comes with you","Wardrobe rebuilt - your character stands on the left and updates instantly, and each icon now shows just the hat or just the shoes instead of a tiny copy of you wearing it","Changing your outfit mid-game now updates straight away, and your partner sees it change too","The menu has a lad hopping between platforms while a saw rolls past - and he wears whatever you've got equipped","In multiplayer the two players no longer spawn on top of each other","The host can only pick levels they've already beaten in solo","Buttons, chips and items now react when you hover and press them"]},{"v":"2.1.0","items":["FIXED: your progress was never actually saving. The game was looking for a save system that doesn't exist on a normal website, so every level you cleared was lost on refresh. It now saves properly in your browser","New sign-in screen. Make a username and password and your progress is kept for you, or tap 'Play without saving' to skip it","Only the host can press Next Level or Restart now, so players can't drift onto different levels","The host can pick which level the party starts on, in both Couch and Online","Leaving to the main menu now takes you out of the party","You can't start a solo game while you're still in a party - it tells you to leave first","If the host closes the game, everyone else is told and sent back to the menu instead of being left stuck"]},{"v":"2.0.0","items":["The game is now split into two worlds. World 1 (The Meat Works) has 19 levels plus a boss, World 2 (The Rot) has 9 levels plus a boss, with a new purple theme","New boss - THE CARVER. A giant blade chases you down a long corridor and smashes itself apart on the pillars until it dies","New boss - THE BUTCHER. It hovers, picks a spot, and slams down. Bait it onto the spikes three times. The exit stays locked until it goes down","New obstacle - crumbling floors. Stand on one and it shakes, drops away, then comes back","New obstacle - laser turrets. They fire a beam across the room on a timer, with a warning flicker before each shot","Every single level was rebuilt and then run through a physics simulation that plays them automatically. All 28 non-boss levels are now mathematically proven to be completable - no more impossible jumps","Your online partner now animates properly instead of gliding around frozen, and shows the actual outfit they picked","You can see your partner's blood and hear their death sound at half volume when they die"]},{"v":"1.6.0","items":["Online play completely rebuilt. It used to save your position to a shared file on a server and re-download it over and over - that server was rate-limiting us, which is why it was so delayed and kept dropping movements","Phones and computers now connect directly to each other instead. Start signals arrive in about 5 milliseconds instead of 4+ seconds, and positions stream 15 times a second","Room codes are now 5 characters like K3F9Q instead of a huge string you had to copy and paste","Your partner is drawn at their real position instead of replaying an old loop of movement, so they no longer teleport or freeze","Level 6 rebuilt again as Ice Box - real ledges to drop down instead of one saw floating in an empty room"]},{"v":"1.5.0","items":["All multiplayer modes now need BOTH players to reach the exit, including Time Trial","Whoever finishes first becomes invincible and waits at the exit while their partner catches up","Two dots above the exit show who has made it - grey until a player arrives, then green","Little celebration burst when you reach the exit","Levels 6, 11 and 15 rebuilt: Ice Box, Tight Squeeze and Off The Rails","10 tougher levels added before the boss: Sharp Turn, Overhead, Crosscut, Narrow Margin, Chain Reaction, False Floor, Grind House, Hangnail, The Wringer and Last Rites","The Carver boss fight moved to level 30","Only the party host picks the mode and starts the game for everyone","Fixed levels 13, 14, 16 and 18 - their exits were walled off or floating out of reach, making them impossible"]},{"v":"1.4.0","items":["Chained and Pass the Bomb can now be played Online (position syncs every few seconds, so expect some lag)","10 new solo levels: Drip Ceiling, Side Step, Tick Tock, Spin Cycle, Ladder of Regret, Double Trouble, Pit Stop, Ceiling Fan, Gauntlet II","The Carver boss fight is now level 20","UI polish - screens fill wider on desktop, wardrobe icons are bigger, added this changelog"]},{"v":"1.3.0","items":["New Multiplayer menu: Couch (same device) and Online (room code) as separate flows","Online party: pick a mode, wait for friends to join, then Start syncs everyone into the level together","New Casual mode - normal levels, two players, no tether, just reach the goal together","New Pass the Bomb mode - one player holds a ticking bomb, touching your partner passes it","Chained/Casual/Bomb no longer require both players to touch the goal at the exact same instant","Grinder Alley (level 6) eased further - slower saws, hanging spikes removed"]},{"v":"1.2.0","items":["Added Chained mode (2-player tether, dedicated levels) and Time Trial (2-player race)","Added a boss level - The Carver","Added online Party system with room codes, ghost trails, and shared stats","Distinct death sounds per hazard type (spikes, saws)","Blood no longer paints over walls or spikes"]},{"v":"1.0.0","items":["First release - 10 solo levels, wardrobe cosmetics, blood physics"]}]},"pantry":{"nm":"Alien Attack","href":"pantry.html","entries":[{"v":"2.14.0","d":"latest · Oct 11, 2026, 11:14 AM ET","items":["FRIENDS LIST ON EVERY MULTIPLAYER GAME. The main menu now has a friends panel down the side with an Add friend button - add someone by the username they picked at sign-up. It's one list shared by Meat Lad, Beetdown, Alien Attack and Starforge, so a friend you add once shows up everywhere","INVITE FRIENDS TO YOUR PARTY. While you have an online party open, a mini friends list sits at the side of the menus with a green dot for whoever is online and what they're playing, plus an Invite button","INVITES POP UP WHEREVER YOUR FRIEND IS - in this game, another game or the main menu - and Join takes them straight into your party, opening Alien Attack for them if they were somewhere else"]},{"v":"2.13.9","items":["EVERY LARDER GETS ITS OWN CUSTOM DECALS NOW instead of sharing the same two generic filler shapes. 30 new hand-drawn objects, two bespoke per sector, on top of its existing landmark and primary icon - satellite dishes and moon rocks on Lunar Command, spore pods and vine tangles in Xeno Jungle, gear stacks and pipe joints in Robot Foundry, specimen jars and data panels in the Laboratory, cargo crates and dock beacons at Orbital Dock, geodes and moonglow on Crystal Moon, gas tanks and atmo vents in the Terraformer Yard, neon signs and hull chunks in Scrap City, biomass pods and vein pipes in the Bio Reactor, engine chunks and spark wires at the UFO Crash Site, ice shards and frost vents on Ice Relay, egg clusters and hive veins in the Alien Hive, power cells and antenna masts on Solar Array, turret stubs and shield nodes on the Defense Ring, energy conduits and core plates in the Core Chamber"]},{"v":"2.13.8","items":["MAP DECORATIONS AREN'T 30 COPIES OF THE SAME ICON ANYMORE. Visible now (last update), but every larder was still scattering one single bespoke shape 30 times, which reads as repetitive no matter how solid it is. Mixed in two new theme-neutral filler shapes - a rock/debris cluster and soft glow motes - so each larder shows a few different things instead of one shape stamped everywhere, with its own bespoke icon still winning most of the rolls so the larder's identity still leads. Also threw in a few oversized copies of the main icon for some size variety instead of everything sitting in one narrow range"]},{"v":"2.13.7","items":["EVERY LARDER LOOKS LIKE ITSELF NOW INSTEAD OF A FAINT TINT ON THE SAME GRADIENT. All 15 maps already had a real per-map theme underneath (a hand-drawn landmark, ~30 scattered props, a distinct surface texture pattern, unbuildable obstacles) - it just wasn't visible, sitting at a washed-out .5 alpha for props and roughly .07-.16 for the surface patterns. Props/landmarks/obstacles bumped to a solid .82 by default, surface textures roughly doubled, terrain shading darkened, prop count up from 20 to 30 and bigger, so each sector actually reads as its own place at a glance again","Fixed the landmark's intended dimmer 'echo' outline actually being invisible - decoIcon used to force its own alpha internally no matter what the caller had already set, silently overriding it every time"]},{"v":"2.13.6","items":["EVERY TOWER AND BUG BLINKS NOW. One addition to the shared eyes() helper (not touching any of their ~30 individual draw functions) so the whole roster picks it up for free - each blinks on its own rhythm instead of the entire board blinking in unison"]},{"v":"2.13.5","items":["BUGS HAVE FACES NOW. Every tower already had cute cartoon eyes (see the shop, the board, anywhere) - the whole enemy side of the board never did, reusing the exact same eyes so the art style stays one consistent language instead of a second bespoke look. A little side-to-side glance keeps a swarm of the same kind from all staring in lockstep","SMOKE/POOF EFFECTS ARE A CARTOON CLOUD SHAPE NOW instead of one soft gradient blob - a cluster of flat-filled lobes, the same comic-book \"poof\" language, showing up everywhere a puff already did (placing, selling, splash hits, upgrades, hero level-ups) since it's all one shared effect","TOWERS AND HEROES BREATHE NOW - the existing idle bob squashes a touch wider at the bottom and stretches a touch taller at the top, the same cheap squash-and-stretch cheat every bouncy cartoon mascot uses, riding on the bob animation that was already there"]},{"v":"2.13.4","items":["TAP THE WAVE COUNTER TO SEE WHAT'S COMING. A real QoL gap - there was no way to plan a purchase around the next wave without just eating it blind. Shows the upcoming wave's enemy mix (icons + counts), boss waves called out distinctly, no pausing required since it's meant to be a quick peek mid-round"]},{"v":"2.13.3","items":["SELLING A TOWER LEAVES A PUFF OF SMOKE AND FLYING PARTS BEHIND NOW instead of just vanishing - the same \"something happened here\" beat placement already gets, in reverse"]},{"v":"2.13.2","items":["THE WAVE COUNTER PILL NOW FLASHES WHEN YOU SEND OUT A NEW WAVE, matching the cash and life pills, which already flashed on a gain/hit and made the wave number the one pill in the HUD that just sat there"]},{"v":"2.13.1","items":["HERO LEVEL-UPS ARE A REAL CELEBRATION NOW instead of a silent stat bump - a gold spark burst and ring hit the hero right where they're standing, a floating \"LEVEL UP\" pops up over their head, and there's a tiny freeze-frame for impact. Visible to everyone in co-op, not just whoever's hero it was"]},{"v":"2.13.0","items":["MOBILE: THE SHOP NO LONGER PERMANENTLY EATS A THIRD OF THE SCREEN. It used to sit as a fixed sidebar, which on a narrow phone squeezed the whole (landscape-shaped) play area into a thin strip - it now docks to the bottom as a collapsible drawer instead: collapsed, it's just a slim handle, so the board gets almost the full screen; tap it to slide the same card list up as an overlay. Picking a tower auto-collapses it so you can immediately tap the board. The tower click panel moves to dock above the handle so nothing overlaps","BUYING AN UPGRADE IS A REAL MOMENT ON THE BOARD NOW, not just a number changing in a panel - a burst and ring in the path's own colour hits the tower itself, with a tiny freeze-frame for impact","Shortened some of the more cramped upgrade-panel button text (\"unlock this in the shop first\" -> \"unlock in shop first\", etc.) so it reads faster and fits tighter panels better"]},{"v":"2.12.8","items":["MOVED THE 2-PATH CAP OFF THE ACCOUNT-WIDE UNLOCK SCREEN AND ONTO EACH TOWER ITSELF, WHERE IT ALWAYS SHOULD'VE BEEN. Unlocking tiers with XP (shop, double-click a card) is unrestricted again - bank XP into all three paths if you want, nothing's ever permanently locked out for the tower type. The 2-path limit now applies per PLACED TOWER instead: any one tower can only ever commit cash to two of its three paths, but different towers of the same type are free to pick different pairs","Reverted the save-data migration from 2.12.7 (it doesn't apply any more - there's no account-wide 2-path limit to violate)"]},{"v":"2.12.7","items":["FIXED THE 2-PATH CAP NOT ACTUALLY STICKING - it only blocked NEW third-path unlocks going forward, so anyone who'd already unlocked tier 1 in all three paths under the old rule (basically everyone with existing progress) could keep right on upgrading that third path forever. Existing save data now gets corrected on load: whichever two paths are furthest along are kept, the weakest third path gets locked back to 0"]},{"v":"2.12.6","items":["CROSSPATHING TIGHTENED: ONLY TWO OF A TOWER'S THREE PATHS CAN EVER BE USED, ACCOUNT-WIDE, PER TOWER TYPE - not even a free tier 1 in the third any more. Pick your two paths (one can go all the way to tier 5, the other caps at tier 2) and the third stays fully locked forever for that tower type","Fixed the Grand Master / Master Of The Fleet achievements again - max possible unlocked tiers dropped from 8 to 7 now that the third path no longer gets a free tier 1"]},{"v":"2.12.5","items":["UPGRADING A TOWER COSTS REAL CASH AGAIN, in-game, per tower - like the classic economy always worked. Unlocking a tier with XP (in the shop's tower-info screen, double-click a card) only makes it AVAILABLE account-wide for that tower type; you still pay cash to actually equip it on any one placed tower, same price as before","THE OLD IN-GAME UPGRADE PANEL IS BACK - click a placed tower and its 3 paths show right there with a buy button each, no separate full-screen page. The full-screen upgrade screen is gone","The shop's tower-info popup (double-click a card) is interactive now instead of read-only - click a glowing upgrade there to spend that tower type's banked XP and unlock it account-wide","A freshly placed tower now starts with none of its tiers equipped, even tier 1 - it's unlocked account-wide from the start (free XP cost, same as before) but still needs a cash purchase in-game like every other tier","Fixed the Grand Master / Master Of The Fleet achievements requiring 9 unlocked tiers when the crosspathing shape has only allowed 8 (5+2+1) since the last update - they were quietly impossible to earn"]},{"v":"2.12.4","items":["CORRECTED HOW ACCOUNT-WIDE UPGRADES ACTUALLY WORK. Unlocking a tier no longer auto-applies it to every placed tower of that type - it makes that tier AVAILABLE account-wide, and you still open each individual tower's own panel to apply it there (free, no extra cost, since it's already paid for - just not automatic). Crosspathing reverted to the classic shape: one path to tier 5, a second to tier 2, the third stays at its free tier 1","FIXED A REAL BUG the above surfaced: the upgrade screen could throw and silently fail to open at all for a tower with any path still below its next unlockable tier - a near-guaranteed situation for any lightly-upgraded tower - because of an empty CSS class being passed to a browser API that (unlike jQuery-style helpers) throws on that instead of ignoring it","Shop cards now show a small pulsing green arrow when that tower type has enough banked XP to unlock something right now","The tower info popup and the in-run upgrade panel both show XP as a big, bold, green number with a lightning icon now instead of a buried \"X tiers unlocked, Y XP\" sentence"]},{"v":"2.12.3","items":["TOWER UPGRADES ARE PERMANENT AND ACCOUNT-WIDE NOW, PER TOWER TYPE - the way this was actually meant to work. Unlock an upgrade on a Cleaver Bot and it's not just that one Cleaver Bot any more: every Cleaver Bot you ever place, this run or any run after, already has it, forever, the same way a real BTD6 unlock works. Each tower TYPE has one shared, permanent XP pool now instead of every placed instance starting from zero","EVERY PATH'S TIER 1 IS FREE FROM THE FIRST PLACEMENT - all three, not just one. The meaningful choice starts at tier 2; crosspathing shifted to match (two paths can go past tier 1, one of those two can go all the way to tier 5, the other caps at tier 3 once it does)","XP EARN RATES CUT SIGNIFICANTLY (roughly to a sixth to an eighth of before) - this pool now compounds across every instance placed in every run forever instead of resetting each placement, so it has to build slowly or mastery would trivialise after a handful of runs","UPGRADE CELLS SHOW A SMALL ICON FOR WHAT THEY ACTUALLY DO NOW (damage, speed, range, pierce, splash, crowd control, economy, armour), reusing the same icon set the shop's tower preview already had, instead of relying on the description text alone"]},{"v":"2.12.2","items":["CO-OP MONEY IS NO LONGER SHARED. Everyone gets their own balance now - your own placements, sells, hero level-ups and kill rewards only ever touch your own cash, and each round's income/bonus pays every active player their own full amount instead of one pool everyone drew from. A new Send button next to each other player in the Players panel lets you wire them cash directly","Fixed the hero row on map select rendering its icon bigger than the row was built for (a shared CSS class was overriding the intended size), which made it visually crowd into the hero's name text next to it"]},{"v":"2.12.1","items":["EVERY TOWER NOW STARTS WITH ITS FIRST UPGRADE ALREADY UNLOCKED, FREE. Towers earning their own XP from scratch (see 2.12.0) meant a freshly placed tower had nothing to lean on until it fought its way to its first tier - now tier 1 of its first path is already applied the moment you place it, combat, support and income towers alike"]},{"v":"2.12.0","items":["TOWERS DON'T LEVEL UP ANY MORE - THEY EARN THEIR OWN XP AND YOU SPEND IT, BTD6-STYLE. Every tower now has its own XP pool, earned purely from damage it deals and things it pops (support/income towers that never damage anything earn it for the rounds they stand through instead). Tap Upgrades on a selected tower to open a full-screen tree: 3 upgrade paths as rows, 5 tiers each running left to right, tier 5 always a hidden ❓ until you actually own it. Every upgrade now costs XP straight out of that pool - no more cash cost, no more \"reach level N first\" gate blocking a tier you could otherwise afford","Heroes are unaffected - they still level up from run XP exactly as before. This only touched towers","Achievements and the shop's tower-info preview updated to match: \"Grand Master\"/\"Master Of The Fleet\" now track fully committing a tower's upgrades (one path maxed, a second at tier 2 - the real ceiling under crosspathing) instead of a tower \"level\" that no longer exists","Fixed a co-op sync bug the new upgrade screen would have made obvious immediately: guests only apply the host's game-state snapshots while state is literally \"play\", but opening any full-screen panel (hero, upgrades) pauses locally - a guest who opened their upgrade screen would stop receiving snapshots entirely, so their own purchase wouldn't visibly go through until they closed and reopened it. Snapshots now apply whenever a run is actually in progress, paused or not"]},{"v":"2.11.2","items":["A CO-OP PLAYERS PANEL. New button in the HUD (co-op only) lists everyone in the run with their name, equipped icon and banner colour - the emote picker now lives here too instead of its own separate button, since \"who am I sending this to\" is exactly what this list answers. The lobby list before a run starts also now shows everyone's icon/banner, not just your own","TOWER BUFFS MOVED OFF THE BOARD AND INTO THE UPGRADE PANEL. A support tower's buff used to draw a small permanent green arrow above every tower it boosted, all the time, whether you were looking or not. Select a buffed tower now and its panel shows a badge for each buffing tower (that tower's own icon, so you can see who) with a hover tooltip spelling out exactly what it grants","THE UPGRADE PANEL NO LONGER ALWAYS SITS BOTTOM-LEFT. It now opens on whichever side of the board the selected tower ISN'T on, so placing something on the left half doesn't bury it under its own panel the moment you click it","Fixed a gap in leaving-the-party cleanup: when a non-host player disconnected mid-run, only the host actually removed them from its player list - other connected guests kept a ghost entry around forever after just showing the toast. All clients clean up properly now"]},{"v":"2.11.1","items":["ACCOUNT XP NOW COMES FROM POPPING ALIENS, not from winning or clearing a round - a loss where you still popped plenty progresses your account exactly as much as a win with the same pops would. There's no separate win bonus any more, on purpose","FIXED SIGN-IN BEING UNREACHABLE ON MOST PHONES (and plenty of shorter desktop windows too) on the game-select page. The page centred its content vertically assuming it would always be shorter than the screen; the moment the games list dropped to one column instead of three, that assumption broke and the header plus the whole sign-in form rendered above the top of the screen with no way to scroll up to them","SOLO PLAY SKIPS THE SEPARATE HERO-PICK SCREEN. Map select now shows your current hero right above a single Deploy button - tap the hero row to change who you're playing as, but the extra full screen in between is gone for the common case of already knowing who you want","THE SKILL CONSTELLATION'S COLOURS ACTUALLY MEAN SOMETHING NOW: light blue = affordable right now, yellow outline with a brighter purple fill = already learned, instead of every branch showing its own colour once owned (which read as noise, not progress)","RENAMED THE TWO CURRENCIES THAT NEVER FIT THE REWORK: Preserve Jars (skill tree) is now Data Cores, and Crumbs (shop) is now Scrap. Save data is untouched - this is display text only"]},{"v":"2.11.0","items":["A REAL PROFILE SCREEN. The top-left account card now opens its own Profile page instead of Settings - equip an owned icon or banner right there and see it applied immediately, both on the card and on the profile page itself, plus your full progress stats. Settings keeps its own copy of the stats and the danger-zone reset","THE SHOP HAS A VENDOR BOT NOW. A robot behind the counter on the left whose eyes and head track your cursor (or your last tap, on touch) - smoothed rather than snapping straight to it. Also added 13 new items across emotes, hit colours, banners and icons","LEVELING UP MID-RUN NOW ACTUALLY POPS UP MID-RUN. Account XP used to only land as one lump sum at the very end of a run, so a tower-unlock choice never showed up until the results screen - by which point the run was already over and there was nothing to place it into. XP now lands as each round is cleared, and a tower choice pauses the game and pops up right then so you can actually deploy what you just unlocked","SELECTING A HERO NO LONGER FORCES OPEN ITS FULL INFO POPUP EVERY TIME - a single click just selects it now (for comparing heroes by clicking through the row); double-click for the full description and skill breakdown","AN ACTUAL INVITE BUTTON IN CO-OP, next to Copy Code - hands your party code straight to your device's own share sheet (texts, Discord, whatever's installed) instead of making a friend copy-paste it manually. A real friends list with online status and join requests isn't possible without a backend server this static, peer-to-peer site doesn't have - party codes are random and one-time-only, so there's no stable identity to build a friends list on top of - and building a fake one that looks like it works would be worse than not having it"]},{"v":"2.10.3","items":["PLACING A ROBOT OR HERO HAS SOME IMPACT NOW - a quick dust ring and spark burst in the robot's own colour when it lands, instead of just silently appearing. Small thing, but every kill already got this kind of punch and placement never did"]},{"v":"2.10.2","items":["A REAL ACCOUNT CARD, TOP LEFT, LIKE BTD6. Your name, equipped icon, level and an actual XP bar toward the next level - the account-level system that gates the tower roster and hands out hero tokens has existed since v1.33 but was never more than a line of text buried in Settings. Tap the card to jump into Settings","ACCOUNT LEVELING IS A REAL XP BAR NOW, not a flat +1 per win. Every round played earns XP toward the next level - win or lose, though a win earns more - instead of only a full clear ever moving the needle at all","TOWER UNLOCK CHOICES NOW QUEUE PROPERLY. A big XP gain can cross several unlock milestones in one run; previously that silently discarded all but the last choice-of-3 and could even offer the same tower in two different choices at once. Every milestone now queues its own choice and they resolve one after another","SUPPORT ROBOTS (Upgrade Hub, Credit Bot, AI Core - the ones that don't fight, just make everything around them better) are now always the last towers offered by a level-up choice, so a fresh account sees fighting robots to unlock first","HERO TOKENS now come every 3 levels while any hero's still locked, same as before - but once every hero is unlocked, the grant shrinks and starts coming every single level instead of every other one, so the trickle never fully dries up"]},{"v":"2.10.1","items":["FIXED A ROBOT UPGRADE BUG FOUND BY ACTUALLY LOOKING AT A MAXED-OUT ROBOT: the 'final form' power nova was wired to trigger at 10 combined upgrade tiers, but the upgrade rules themselves cap a robot at 7 (5 on one path, 2 on a second - only one path can ever go past tier 2) once you're two paths deep. The nova could never actually fire. It now triggers off fully maxing a path instead, so it's the reachable payoff it was meant to be"]},{"v":"2.10.0","items":["SKILL TREE REBUILT AS AN ACTUAL CONSTELLATION. Tap the glowing orb top-right for a real hyperspace warp transition, then spend Preserve Jars among the stars instead of scrolling a list - each skill chain radiates from a centre point, and the line to whatever a skill needs only lights up once you actually own both ends","TOWER UPGRADES ARE MORE UNIQUE. Ten upgrade paths that were pure damage/speed/range stacking now gain a real effect partway through (weaken, burn, slow or splash) instead of just bigger numbers all the way down. Credit Bot's \"Jackpot\" upgrade is an actual jackpot now too - a real chance-based bonus payout, not just another flat number wearing the name","COSMETICS ACTUALLY DO SOMETHING. Emotes, hit-effect colours, and your co-op lobby icon/banner could all be bought and equipped before with nothing anywhere reading them back - the whole shop was a dead end. Your hit colour now shows on your own kills, your icon and banner show in the party list, and a new HUD button lets you fire an owned emote that syncs to the rest of your co-op party","REDESIGNED THE SHOP TRAY, THE TITLE SCREEN'S BUTTONS, AND THE BOARD ITSELF. Shop cards are square icon-and-price tiles two to a row instead of a scrolling list of name plates. The Towers and Almanac buttons did the exact same thing, so there was only ever one; the title screen's buttons got reorganised and a fullscreen toggle was added. The game board also stopped being hard-capped at 1280px wide - on any wider monitor that meant two solid dead borders either side of the whole game, not just the map","MOBILE FIXES. The skill constellation's outermost stars could land off the right edge of a narrow phone screen; the shop tray had touch-action:none left over from drag-and-drop that was never actually built, which blocked scrolling almost everywhere once cards went two-per-row; and the fullscreen button did nothing at all on iOS with zero feedback (it has no requestFullscreen API for a plain page) - it now says so and points at Add to Home Screen instead","A FIRST REAL LOOK AT THE GAME WITH SCREENSHOTS INSTEAD OF BLIND CODE CHANGES turned up bugs no amount of reading the source would have caught: craters everywhere (title screen, map thumbnails, in-run decoration) were a barely-visible dark fill under an almost-complete bright ring, reading as stray circles rather than craters. The walkable path was still hardcoded pantry brown on every map regardless of theme - Lunar Command's cyan never actually made it onto the one thing aliens walk down. And the map exit was still a literal illustrated jam jar, now a glowing command beacon","FINISHED THE RENAME. \"Pantry Panic\" wasn't the only leftover text - \"Pick a larder\", \"Into the pantry\", achievement names/descriptions, the win screen title, several co-op status messages, and every place enemies were still called \"bugs\" instead of aliens were all still live UI text a player sees constantly, not just old changelog entries. Save data keys and the co-op matchmaking prefix were deliberately left alone - renaming those would silently break existing players' saves and cross-client matching for no visible benefit"]},{"v":"2.9.0","items":["ALIEN ATTACK. Pantry Panic has its final name now - changed everywhere it's shown: the game-select page, the browser tab, the settings screen and the title logo. Old changelog entries below keep the old name since they're a record of what shipped at the time","THE TITLE SCREEN IS A REAL MOON BATTLEFIELD NOW. A starfield, a distant Earth watching from orbit, and a crater-pocked lunar horizon behind the logo - with two defenders on the surface below actually firing on the aliens marching at them. The last rework's changelog claimed this already happened; it hadn't, the title screen was still the old pantry-shelf-and-jar scene underneath the new logo the whole time","HEROES NOW GAIN REAL ARMOUR AS THEY LEVEL, not just a size bump. Towers have visibly grown riveted plating the deeper you invest in an upgrade path - heroes never got the equivalent, only a bigger silhouette and an orbiting-star effect. The moment a hero's first skill unlocks they now gain plating in their own colour, and their second (final) skill adds a third plate plus a soft power nova, both in-run and in the Heroes hub preview. Old Tin, Sprout and Meat Lad are deliberately left out of this - they keep their fixed, recognisable look","Cleaned up two dead, never-called copies of the hero art function left over from the original Robots vs Aliens rework (only the last one of the three ever actually ran) - same hero look as before, just ~190 fewer lines of unreachable code"]},{"v":"2.0.0","items":["ROBOTS VS ALIENS FULL VISUAL REWORK - replaced the old Pantry Panic brown visual identity with a bright blue/white futuristic command theme; rebuilt the title scene as a moon battlefield with moving robots, UFOs, laser fire and ground aliens; removed the old Pantry Panic wording from the visible UI; and gave every map a distinct sci-fi location identity such as Lunar Command, Xeno Jungle, Robot Foundry, Xeno Laboratory, Orbital Dock, Crystal Moon, Terraformer Yard, Scrap City, Bio Reactor, UFO Crash Site, Ice Relay, Alien Hive, Solar Array, Defense Ring, Alien Garden and Core Chamber. Each map now uses a dedicated landmark and themed environment instead of generic pantry props.","ROBOTS VS ALIENS - all 21 towers now have final robot art, every one a proper mechanical robot with its own silhouette, weapon and personality: Flicker Bot, Pepper Bot, Return Bot, Shaker Bot, Drill Bot, Steam Bot, Sticky Bot, Upgrade Hub, Credit Bot, Launch Bot, Cleaver Bot, Mixer Bot, Thermo Bot, Cryo Bot, AI Core, Shredder Bot, Slosh Bot, Trap Bot, Roller Bot, Vortex Bot and Growth Bot. Categories are now Basic Robots / Heavy Machines / Advanced Robotics / Robot Support, Old Tin/Sprout/Meat Lad have new robot/alien-flavoured descriptions, and all 14 enemies are renamed aliens with a pulsing bioluminescent glow and a running-lights boss ring instead of a gold crown.","EVERY TOWER'S UPGRADE TREE HAS BEEN REWORKED with unique mechanics in place of flat plus-damage/plus-range stacking: armour corrosion (weaken), cleaving/blast shockwaves (splash), bleed and burn effects, EMP shutdown and heat-haze slow fields, multi-target drilling and rail-piercing, full-circle wide-blast attack modes, sticky fields, gravity/suction vortexes, nanite infection, hunter-AI target marking, ice-shatter vulnerability, and cleaner single-purpose buff paths for the support robots. This also surfaced and fixed a real engine gap: weaken, burn and splash effects on towers were silently doing nothing on snipe and cone-type attacks (only the default projectile path applied them) - Growth Bot's own base weaken stat had been non-functional since it shipped. All four attack types now apply every effect correctly. Every tower and enemy kept its internal id throughout, so existing saves, unlocks and mastery progress are untouched","ROBOTS NOW PHYSICALLY GROW AS YOU UPGRADE THEM - a robot with 10 tiers invested across its two open paths stands visibly ~25% bigger than a stock one, with riveted armour plating appearing once you're 6+ tiers deep. Max both paths out and it triggers a pulsing white 'final form' nova, so a fully-kitted-out robot reads as dramatically more powerful from across the board, not just from its upgrade panel - the T5 transformation the design brief called for, applied generically across all 21 robots at once","HERO UPGRADES GOT THE SAME TREATMENT - Commander Gordo's level-10 'Overdrive' skill now actually fires twin pulses instead of just adding flat damage, and Unit F0RK's level-13 'Ground Skewer' cracks the ground on impact for a real splash hit instead of a numbers-only damage bump. Along the way, twin-shot was wired up for heroes for the first time (it only ever worked for towers before). Heroes now also visibly grow as they level - a level 20 hero reads as noticeably bigger than a level 1 one, matching the growth robots already get from their upgrade trees"]},{"v":"1.45.0","items":["TOWER UPGRADES ACTUALLY CHANGE THE MODEL NOW, not just a ring around it - the tower's own body colour shifts toward gold/cyan/purple as you invest deeper into a path (like BTD6's Glue Gunner recolouring per tier), and each path pins a distinct badge shape (blade/bolt/burst) onto the tower instead of a floating dot. The Crumb Flicker's 'Both Hands' upgrade and the Boomerang Pin's 'Two Pins' upgrade are real double-shot mechanics now, and the model visibly grows a second flicking arm / second pin to match - more upgrades across the roster will get unique mechanics like this over time instead of flat stat stacking"]},{"v":"1.44.1","items":["HEROES NOW SHOW THEIR LEVEL VISUALLY TOO - orbiting stars in the hero's own colour build up every 4 levels, and hitting the level 20 cap gets a pulsing gold ring so a fully-levelled hero visibly stands out on the board"]},{"v":"1.44.0","items":["TOWERS NOW VISUALLY CHANGE AS YOU UPGRADE THEM - each of the 3 upgrade paths gets its own colour (gold/cyan/purple) shown as small orbiting studs around the tower that grow with the tier bought, so you can read a tower's build at a glance without opening its panel. Commit to two paths at once and the tower also gets a shared pulsing ring to show the combo is active. The tower panel's path labels now show a matching colour dot too"]},{"v":"1.43.0","items":["EVERY SINGLE TOWER AND HERO NOW HAS ITS OWN FIRE ANIMATION - the fire-reactive treatment previously limited to a handful of towers is now on all 20 towers and all 12 heroes: muzzle flashes, sparkle bursts, splashes, flour puffs, coin pops, blade-swipe streaks, spin-up boosts and more, each matching what that specific tower or hero actually does when it attacks"]},{"v":"1.42.1","items":["TOASTER AND FREEZER RAY JOIN THE BESPOKE FIRE ANIMATION LINEUP - the toaster's bread now launches sharply out of the slots with a heat-glow puff on every shot instead of just idly bobbing, and Freezer Ray's frost crystals flare longer and flash frigid white right as it fires"]},{"v":"1.42.0","items":["OVEN BLAST, MOUSE TRAP AND MIXER BOT GET BESPOKE FIRE ANIMATIONS instead of only the generic recoil every tower shares - the oven's door glow blooms wide on every shot, the trap's jaws snap sharply shut instead of just wobbling, and the mixer's blades spin dramatically faster right as it fires"]},{"v":"1.41.1","items":["BUGS GET A REAL DEATH ANIMATION NOW instead of just vanishing the instant they pop - their own sprite squashes flat, spins and fades over a beat, on top of the existing particle burst"]},{"v":"1.41.0","items":["SNIPER BEAMS AND CONE ATTACKS ARE TINTED PER-TOWER NOW instead of every single one being the same generic grey-white flash. Freezer Ray's beam actually reads as icy blue, Oven Blast's cone as fiery orange, Corkscrew's shot as wine-red, and so on for every cone/beam tower and hero - the beam also got a bit more visual weight with a soft outer glow"]},{"v":"1.40.0","items":["TOWERS AND HEROES IDLE-BOB NOW instead of standing perfectly still between shots - a faint sway, phase-offset per placement so a row of the same tower doesn't bob in unison","BUGS POP INTO EXISTENCE NOW instead of just appearing - a quick overshoot scale-in right as they spawn, same idea as a cartoon entrance"]},{"v":"1.39.1","items":["BUGS FLASH WHITE WHEN THEY'RE HIT instead of just silently losing health - every landed hit now has a visible moment of impact on the bug itself, not just the floating damage number"]},{"v":"1.39.0","items":["PROJECTILES NOW LOOK LIKE WHAT FIRED THEM. Crumb Flicker throws an actual crumb, Toaster launches a toast slice, Honey Pot drips a honey teardrop, Salt Shaker scatters grains, Cheese Grater flings shreds, Soup Ladle lobs a splash of soup, Blender Vortex spins out chunks, Pepper Grinder puffs dark flecks - instead of every tower firing the same recoloured blob"]},{"v":"1.38.1","items":["Fixed the new hero shop slot not updating to \"In the pantry\" right after you actually placed your hero - it kept saying \"Free\" until something else happened to refresh the shop"]},{"v":"1.38.0","items":["YOUR HERO NO LONGER SPAWNS STUCK TO YOUR CURSOR THE SECOND A RUN STARTS. It's a slot at the top of the shop now, same as a tower - tap it (or your placed hero) whenever you're ready instead of it following your mouse from the first frame","BALANCE PASS: the Popping skill branch was a flat +4 damage to every single tower and hero at once for just 9 jars, which especially trivialised low-damage towers - cut to +2, and its range bonus trimmed too. Bugs also gain health faster round over round now. If the game was still feeling too easy after the last tuning pass, this should bite more"]},{"v":"1.37.0","items":["REDESIGNED ACHIEVEMENTS AND THE SKILL TREE. Achievements are grouped into Runs/Combat/Collection/Mastery, each with its own colour and icon, and every progress-based one (pop counts, jar totals, boss kills, map completion, tower mastery) now shows a real progress bar instead of just locked-or-not. The skill tree is numbered and colour-coded per branch to actually read as a tree instead of a plain list"]},{"v":"1.36.0","items":["A REAL SETTINGS MENU - see your progress at a glance (level, unlocks, tokens, jars, crumbs, wins) and, if you ever want to, wipe Pantry Panic's save completely and start fresh (with a proper \"are you sure\" step first - it's permanent)","THE TITLE SCREEN'S ICON ROW IS BIGGER AND LABELLED NOW - Towers, Achievements, Shop, Skills, Almanac, What's New and the new Settings button all have visible text under them instead of relying on a hover tooltip to know what they do"]},{"v":"1.35.0","items":["FIXED TWO DIFFERENT TOWERS BOTH BEING CALLED \"ROLLING PIN\" - the primary-class boomerang one is now Boomerang Pin, so it's no longer confusable with the military-class wide-crushing Rolling Pin","A NEW FLYING ENEMY, THE WASP - fast, light, shows up from wave 16 to bridge the gap between Moths and Hornets, and (like every flyer) can only be hit by air-capable towers and heroes","THE TOASTER NOW HITS FLYERS TOO, so it's not just a slower Cheese Grater anymore - it was the closest thing to a genuine duplicate in the roster"]},{"v":"1.34.0","items":["HERO TOKENS NOW ACTUALLY BUY SOMETHING. New saves start with just Chef Gordo unlocked - every other hero has a token price now (3-6 tokens depending on how strong they are) and shows up greyed out on the pick screen with its cost until you buy it. Tap a locked hero to unlock it for good once you've saved up. Existing saves keep every hero they already had unlocked, same as towers"]},{"v":"1.33.0","items":["A REAL PROGRESSION SYSTEM. New saves now start with just the four cheapest towers, one per class - everything else unlocks by playing. Win a run to level up: every 2 levels from level 3 you pick one of 3 random locked towers to add for good, and every 5 levels from level 6 you earn Hero Tokens (a new currency, more of these coming). Once every tower's unlocked, Hero Tokens start coming every 2 levels instead, in smaller amounts. Anyone with an existing save keeps every tower they already had - nothing already unlocked gets taken away","The title screen shows your level, tower unlock progress, and Hero Token count, and the Almanac marks which towers you haven't unlocked yet"]},{"v":"1.32.1","items":["THE PATH YOU'VE BEEN LOCKED OUT OF (by committing to the other two) NOW LOOKS OBVIOUSLY DIFFERENT from one that's just not affordable or levelled yet - desaturated and dimmer, so it reads as \"not this run\" instead of \"not yet\""]},{"v":"1.32.0","items":["MONEY TOWERS SHOW THEIR LIFETIME TOTAL NOW - the sidebar panel breaks down what you spent on them, how much they've actually made, and your net so far, plus pop counts now show for every tower type, not just the ones that fight","BUFFED TOWERS SHOW A LITTLE ARROW OVER THEIR HEAD, and their panel now lists exactly which support towers are buffing them","A TOWER CAN ONLY CARRY ONE COPY OF A GIVEN BUFF NOW - two of the same support tower in range used to stack their bonus twice; now the second one is redundancy, not a second buff. Different support towers still stack fine with each other"]},{"v":"1.31.0","items":["OBSTACLES ACTUALLY BLOCK YOUR SHOTS NOW. Crates used to just take up a cell you couldn't build on - now they genuinely block line of sight, so a tower can't snipe through one to hit a bug it shouldn't be able to see (flying bugs still fly right over them, as you'd expect)","EVERY TOWER AND HERO HAS A REAL ATTACK ANIMATION NOW - a punchy little recoil and scale-kick right as they fire, on top of the existing muzzle glow, instead of just launching a projectile with no visible action behind it"]},{"v":"1.30.0","items":["ONE UPGRADE-TREE MENU INSTEAD OF TWO. Double-clicking a placed tower on the board used to open a second, different full-screen panel on top of the sidebar one - that's gone now. Double-click still only does one thing: previewing a tower's tree from the shop before you've bought it. Once it's placed, click it and use the sidebar panel to actually upgrade or sell it, same as always","Also fixed the shop's upgrade-tree preview panel being cramped into a narrow box meant for a much simpler layout - it's properly sized now"]},{"v":"1.29.1","items":["FIXED DOUBLE-CLICKING SHOP CARDS NOT OPENING THE UPGRADE TREE. Every single click on a card was rebuilding the entire shop list to update its selection highlight - on a real double-click that could tear down and recreate the exact card the second click was about to land on, so the browser never saw it as a double-click. Selection now just toggles a class on the existing cards instead of rebuilding anything"]},{"v":"1.29.0","items":["DOUBLE-CLICK A TOWER IN THE SHOP TO SEE ITS FULL UPGRADE TREE before you even buy one - all three paths, every tier, base stats, and how far you've permanently mastered it, same idea as the hero upgrade-tree viewer","THE SHOP LIST IS NOTICEABLY MORE COMPACT NOW - smaller padding and tighter category headers so more towers are visible without scrolling","TOWER ICONS IN THE SHOP GET THEIR OWN SIGNATURE COLOUR RING NOW too, same treatment as heroes got, so towers in the same category are easier to tell apart at a glance"]},{"v":"1.28.0","items":["TAP A HERO ON THE PICK SCREEN TO SEE ITS FULL UPGRADE TREE - every ability, what level it needs, and whether you've ever actually reached that level with this hero before (tracked permanently, across every run)","HERO ICONS ARE EASIER TO TELL APART NOW - each one gets a ring in its own signature colour instead of just its group's colour, so heroes in the same family (all four kitchen staff, all three money heroes) don't blur together","THE HERO PICKER FITS MORE PER ROW NOW so there's less scrolling to see the whole roster"]},{"v":"1.27.0","items":["EVERY LARDER GETS ONE UNMISTAKABLE LANDMARK - an oversized version of its own scenery prop (a giant loaf, a giant snowflake, a giant wheel of cheese...) planted somewhere on the board, with its own shadow and rim-light, so each map has one specific thing you'd recognise it by","THE TRAIL ITSELF LOOKS LIKE A REAL SURFACE NOW - tile seams running its length instead of a flat coloured band","REDESIGNED THE ENTRY AND EXIT - bugs now pour in through a glowing directional arrow, and the exit is an actual illustrated glass jar with a lid and a highlight, replacing the plain circle-and-label"]},{"v":"1.26.0","items":["COMBAT HAS REAL IMPACT NOW. Every hit throws a spark burst and a floating damage number, splash hits get a shockwave ring, and every bug's death has proper punch to it","BOSS DEATHS ARE AN EVENT - a full explosion (shockwave, smoke, debris, screen flash) plus a screen shake and a brief hit-stop instead of the same small pop everything else gets","BOSSES NOW ANNOUNCE THEMSELVES when they spawn - a banner across the top of the screen and a shake, so you know something's different about this wave before it reaches you","A touch more life in the upgrade buttons - they lift and glow on hover/press instead of just changing colour"]},{"v":"1.25.0","items":["THREE NEW BOSSES. Wave 10 was always the Grub King - now wave 20 brings the flying Hive Queen, wave 30 the heavily armoured Ironclad Behemoth, and wave 40 the fast, vicious Silver Widow, each with its own look and its own threat, not just a bigger number on the same boss","A SOFT VIGNETTE around the board for a punchier, less flat look","SIX NEW ACHIEVEMENTS covering Insane wins, boss kills, meeting all four bosses, maxing out a tower's mastery (and every tower's), and clearing the three hardest larders","EIGHT NEW STORE ITEMS - two emotes, two hit-effect colours, two banners, and Hive Queen / Silver Widow icons","Fixed the Every Shelf achievement still saying \"twelve larders\" after the larder count grew past that"]},{"v":"1.24.0","items":["THE BOARD DOESN'T LOOK LIKE A SPREADSHEET ANYMORE - the always-on grid lines are gone, replaced with soft uneven ground patches so the larder reads as real terrain instead of a lattice of cells","EVERY LARDER NOW HAS A FEW SOLID OBSTACLES - crates that block a cell outright, laid out once per map alongside the path so there's real tactical texture to work around, not just an open floor"]},{"v":"1.23.0","items":["EVERY LARDER HAS REAL SCENERY NOW instead of just a coloured gradient with a path on it - each map scatters its own themed background props (loaves, chilis, jars, snowflakes, cobwebs, cheese wedges, pretzels and more) across the board, laid out once per map so it stays consistent every time you play it","Four new prop types (cheese, egg, pretzel, spoon) and a reshuffle of which map uses which, so far fewer larders share the same scenery anymore"]},{"v":"1.22.1","items":["Fixed dropping a dragged tower back over the shop sidebar sometimes trying to place it instead of cancelling - the drop zone check wasn't accounting for the shop sitting visually on top of the board's canvas"]},{"v":"1.22.0","items":["FIXED THE UPGRADE PANEL EATING CLICKS. It was silently rebuilding every single one of its buttons several times a second to keep stats fresh, which could yank a button out from under your cursor mid-click - it now updates numbers in place instead and leaves the buttons alone","DRAG A TOWER STRAIGHT FROM THE SHOP ONTO THE BOARD to place it - tap-then-tap still works too, this is just a faster option alongside it","SUPPORT AND INCOME TOWERS CAN LEVEL UP NOW. They never land a kill so they never earned the XP that unlocks their own upgrades - they now earn it for the round they just stood through instead","A NEW INSANE DIFFICULTY for anyone who's cleared Hard and wants worse","THREE NEW END-GAME LARDERS - Spice Cabinet Snake, The Long Cold Aisle and Pantry's End - tighter and nastier than anything before them","The four tower classes (Everyday Tools, Heavy Kitchenware, Cooking Magic, Pantry Support) now show what they're about right in the shop and Almanac, BTD6-style","A first-pass difficulty tuning: slightly less starting cash, bugs gain health a bit faster round over round - the last few versions added a lot of power (5-tier upgrades, permanent mastery), this claws a little back"]},{"v":"1.21.0","items":["TOWER MASTERY IS PERMANENT NOW. Once any Toaster (or any tower) you've placed reaches the level an upgrade needs, that upgrade is unlocked on every Toaster you ever place again - this run or a future one - no more re-levelling the same tower from scratch every time","IN CO-OP, YOU CAN ONLY SELL, UPGRADE OR RETARGET YOUR OWN TOWERS NOW - everyone else's are labelled 'Owned by' their name so you always know whose is whose","THE HERO SELECT SCREEN IS ONE GRID NOW instead of six separate scrolling sections - every hero's visible at a glance, still colour-coded by group","Cut a hot per-frame performance sink where every tower re-scanned the entire tower list just to check for nearby support buffs, every frame - it now only scans actual support towers"]},{"v":"1.20.0","items":["CO-OP HEROES, FOR REAL THIS TIME. Every player - host and guests alike - can now place their own hero on the shared board, and everyone sees everyone else's hero fighting right alongside the towers, not just the host's","WHEN SOMEONE LEAVES A CO-OP RUN, everyone still in the pantry gets a toast about it now, not just the host","Trimmed some redundant data out of the co-op sync packets and eased the sync rate back slightly to help both host and guest framerates in bigger multiplayer games"]},{"v":"1.19.0","items":["EVERY TOWER GOES FIVE TIERS DEEP NOW, up from three, matching BTD6's real depth - that's 126 brand new upgrades across all 21 towers, each with its own name, description and icon. Mastery level requirements now stretch across the full 1-10 range (level 2/4/6/8/10 per tier) so the deepest upgrades are a genuine end-game reward, and the same crosspathing rule still applies: two paths at once, and going past tier 3 in one locks the others there"]},{"v":"1.18.0","items":["TOWERS FLASH THEIR GROUP COLOUR WHEN THEY FIRE. A quick coloured pulse under every tower each time it shoots - amber for Everyday Tools, steel for Heavy Kitchenware, orange for Cooking Magic, green for Pantry Support - so the board reads as more alive and colourful during a real fight, not just in the menus"]},{"v":"1.17.0","items":["TWO NEW BUGS. Silverfish are small, quick and show up from wave 7 - blink and they're past you. Spiders show up from wave 15, tougher and eight-legged, and burst into a trio of ants when they finally go down","A COUPLE OF THE MUTED BUG COLOURS GOT BRIGHTER - Weevil was a flat brown that blended into everything, now a proper olive green; Moth was a washed-out tan, now a dustier lavender that actually reads as its own colour next to the rest of the swarm"]},{"v":"1.16.0","items":["TWO NEW SUPPORT HEROES. Old Tin was carrying that whole group alone - Coupon Clara throws hard-thrown change with real pierce and a smaller but steady payout, while Miser Morty barely fights at all but ends up paying out more than anyone by the late game if you can protect him"]},{"v":"1.15.0","items":["SIX NEW TOWERS - two more in every category except Support. Cheese Grater and Soup Ladle join the Everyday Tools, Mouse Trap and Rolling Pin join Heavy Kitchenware, and Blender Vortex and Yeast Bloom join Cooking Magic. Each one has its own gimmick (rapid melee shredding, splash soup with a burn option, a devastating slow snipe, a wide crushing arc, an all-directions blade burst, and a weakening cloud) and the full three-path, nine-upgrade treatment like every other tower"]},{"v":"1.14.0","items":["TARGETING MODES, LIKE BTD6. Every tower that fires at one bug at a time now has First/Last/Close/Strong buttons in its upgrade panel (both the sidebar and the full-screen view) - pick off the toughest bug in range, focus whichever's nearest, or stick with the default of hitting whoever's furthest along the path first"]},{"v":"1.13.0","items":["THE SHOP IS ONE LIST NOW instead of four separate category tabs - every tower's right there as you scroll, grouped and headed by category, each card tinted with its group's colour so you can still tell Everyday Tools from Cooking Magic at a glance without having to click between tabs first"]},{"v":"1.12.0","items":["THE ALMANAC IS SORTED BY GROUP NOW, with a header between each one, instead of towers appearing in a random-looking order that didn't match their category colours","AUTO-START NEXT WAVE. A toggle in the pause menu - turn it on and the next round begins the moment the last one clears, no need to tap GO every time"]},{"v":"1.11.0","items":["TOWERS NOW EARN MASTERY THROUGH USE. Every tower has its own level, gained from XP its kills bring in, and each upgrade tier needs a minimum level on top of its cash cost - the deeper tiers have to be earned by actually fighting, not just bought the moment you can afford them","DOUBLE-CLICK A PLACED TOWER for a full-screen view: all three upgrade paths side by side with a proper icon and description on every single tier, the tower's level and XP bar, and one click to buy the next upgrade right from the card - instead of a tiny sidebar list one path at a time","EVERY UPGRADE HAS A REAL ICON NOW, picked to match what it actually does - a burst for damage, a bolt for speed, a target for range, rings for splash, a shield for armour-piercing, and so on","CLICK YOUR HERO ON THE BOARD to open their panel too, not just the HUD button"]},{"v":"1.10.0","items":["CO-OP ACTUALLY SYNCS NOW. It used to only share the wave number and a vague hint that a bug spawned - everyone's towers, bugs, and cash were really just separate, disconnected simulations running side by side. The host now runs the real shared game and streams the board to everyone else in real time, and placing, upgrading or selling a tower goes through the shared game so your teammates actually see it. Heroes aren't part of the shared board yet - that's still local-only per player, a follow-up for later","FIXED THE HOST BEING ASKED FOR THEIR HERO TWICE when starting a co-op run - once in the lobby, then again on a whole separate screen right after clicking Start. It only asks once now, matching what guests already saw","FIXED A TOWER'S \"POPPED\" COUNT NEVER MOVING in the upgrade panel. Kills were never actually credited back to the tower that landed them, so the stat sat at zero forever no matter how much a tower fought - every kill, including burn damage over time, now correctly credits the tower that caused it"]},{"v":"1.9.0","items":["A REAL BACKGROUND ON THE TITLE SCREEN. Layered shelf silhouettes with scattered jars, cans and produce receding into the dark, a warm glow behind the logo, and slow-drifting crumb dust - instead of a flat brown wall behind the menu buttons","THE IN-RUN HUD GOT A REAL LOOK. Cash, lives, wave and hero level pills now have icons and a richer card-style background instead of plain text on a flat box, and the cash/life pills give a little pulse whenever they change so gains and losses actually register"]},{"v":"1.8.0","items":["FIXED A REAL MOBILE BUG. On a phone-width screen the shop tray (186px) and the tower upgrade panel (238px) together were wider than the whole screen, so about 20% of every upgrade button sat underneath the shop and missed taps. Both shrink on narrow screens now so they never overlap","BIGGER TAP TARGETS on the shop's category tabs (Prim/Mil/Magic/Supp) - they were only 25px tall, well under comfortable thumb size"]},{"v":"1.7.0","items":["TOWERS AND BUGS ARE COLOUR-CODED BY GROUP NOW. The Almanac gives every tower and bug its own themed card background (Everyday Tools, Heavy Kitchenware, Cooking Magic, Pantry Support for towers - each bug gets one built from its own colour) instead of a flat grey box, and the in-run shop marks each tower's category with a coloured dot next to its name","FIXED THE ALMANAC'S ICONS BEING COMPLETELY BLANK. They were being wiped by a DOM re-render right after being drawn - a real, previously-invisible bug, not just a visual polish item"]},{"v":"1.6.0","items":["A REAL HERO MENU. Tap your hero's level pill in the top HUD any time mid-run to open a full panel - portrait on a colour-coded background for their group, XP bar to the next level, and every ability listed with the level it unlocks at","SPEND CASH TO INSTANTLY LEVEL UP YOUR HERO. Right from that same panel - costs scale up with level so it's a real choice against buying towers, not a free ride, but early levels are cheap enough to be worth grabbing"]},{"v":"1.5.0","items":["ECONOMY REBALANCED. Early kills used to pay almost nothing next to the automatic round bonus (a wave-1 ant was worth $1) so fighting barely mattered until wave 10+ - every bug's cash reward is up so popping things actually feels worth it from the first wave","HARD MODE NOW ACTUALLY PAYS MORE. Difficulty had a reward multiplier that only applied to Preserve Jars between runs - Hard enemies have 2.3x the health but you earned the exact same cash as Easy the whole run. Hard and Medium now earn real in-run cash bonuses (+35% and +15%) to compensate for the tougher fight"]},{"v":"1.4.0","items":["EVERY LARDER NOW HAS ITS OWN LOOK. Map thumbnails and the in-run board itself pull a real colour theme per map (dark pantry, red spice rack, blue-white freezer, green garden, gold grand hall...) instead of every map reusing the same flat brown background - each one also gets little scattered deco icons (loaves, chillies, snowflakes, jars...) and a tier badge so the select screen actually shows what map you're looking at","BUGS ARE DISTINGUISHABLE BY SHAPE NOW, not just colour. Ants are slim, roaches are wide and flat with long antennae, beetles and weevils have a shell seam and shine, weevils get a pointed snout, hornets get banded stripes, moths get bigger patterned wings - so you can tell what's coming at a glance instead of squinting at colour"]},{"v":"1.3.0","items":["THREE UPGRADE PATHS ON EVERY TOWER, all fifteen of them, matching the real BTD6 rule: you can only put points into two of the three, and once one path passes tier 2 the others are capped there. Forces a real build decision instead of just maxing both paths","FIXED A REAL UI SYNC BUG. The shop and the open upgrade panel only refreshed on specific clicks, so if your cash grew from kills while you were browsing, the affordability and prices you saw went stale until you closed and reopened - now they refresh live, several times a second, without you touching anything"]},{"v":"1.2.1","items":["FIXED CRUMBS ONLY PAYING OUT ON A WIN. They're supposed to be earned just for finishing a round, whether you win or lose - a loss was quietly paying zero"]},{"v":"1.2.0","items":["TITLE SCREEN REBUILT. Three big buttons across the bottom - Play, Co-op and Heroes - with a row of smaller icon buttons above for Towers, Achievements, Shop, Skills, Almanac and Changelog","A HEROES HUB. The new Heroes button lets you browse and equip your default hero without starting a run. In co-op, everyone gets their own \"Change hero\" button in the lobby while waiting for the host to start","TEN ACHIEVEMENTS to track down, from your first win to clearing every larder with every hero","A SHOP for cosmetics - emotes, hit-effect colours, banners and icons - paid for with Crumbs, a new currency earned just for finishing a round, win or lose. Purely cosmetic, no gameplay effect"]},{"v":"1.1.0","items":["FIXED THE GO/FAST-FORWARD BUTTON BEING UNCLICKABLE. The HUD bar spanned the full screen width while the shop panel painted over the same top-right corner, physically hiding the button underneath it the whole time","SIX MORE TOWERS, fifteen in total - Toaster and three military/magic pieces (Meat Cleaver, Mixer Bot, Freezer Ray, Oven Blast) plus Recipe Book, a support tower that speeds up every tower on the board, not just nearby ones","FOUR MORE HEROES, ten in total - Baker Jo, Granny Spice, Butcher Rex and a crossover guest, The Lad, on loan from next door's platformer","SEVEN MORE LARDERS, twelve in total, up to tier 12's Grand Larder","MAP SELECT REBUILT. Larders now show an actual little picture of their path and page through six at a time instead of a wall of text cards","HERO SELECT REBUILT. All ten heroes are visible at once in a scrolling list, grouped and colour-coded by type - Kitchen Staff, Cutlery & Tools, Magic & Mystic, Money & Support, Garden Guests and Special Guests"]},{"v":"1.0.0","items":["PANTRY PANIC IS HERE. A tower defence about bugs raiding the larder - nine towers across four categories, six heroes, five larders, three difficulties, a permanent skill tree and online co-op","NINE TOWERS IN FOUR CATEGORIES. Primary (Crumb Flicker, Pepper Grinder, Rolling Pin), Military (Salt Shaker, Corkscrew), Magic (Tea Kettle, Honey Pot) and Support (Spice Rack, Piggy Bank). Every one has two upgrade paths, five tiers deep between them","SIX HEROES, each with their own attack and two abilities that unlock as they level. Chef Gordo speeds up as he cooks, Nana Crust lobs splash pies, Sir Fork hits like a truck up close, Madame Whisk weakens what she touches, Old Tin prints money, and Sprout is on loan from Beetdown","FIVE LARDERS that get longer and more tangled - The Long Shelf, Spice Route, The Crossing, Cupboard Maze and The Deep Larder","THREE DIFFICULTIES. Easy, Medium and Hard change bug health, bug speed, your starting cash and how many lives you get","A SKILL PANTRY. Win a run to earn Preserve Jars, spend them on permanent upgrades across four branches that carry into every future run","BUGS SPLIT WHEN THEY POP, same idea as the balloons - roaches burst into ants, weevils into beetles, and the Grub King at the end of every larder takes some doing","MENU MUSIC and an animated title screen, matching the other two games"]}]},"beetdown":{"nm":"Beetdown","href":"beetdown.html","entries":[{"v":"2.2.0","d":"latest · Oct 12, 2026, 8:32 PM ET","items":["SMOOTHER ON MEDIUM AND HIGH. Those two settings were laggy, so the expensive parts were rebuilt: splats and scorch marks are now drawn as light sprites instead of being painted into the huge ground picture, the night lighting uses a much cheaper method, the ground is baked a little smaller, very large or sharp screens draw fewer pixels, and weapons are pre-drawn once instead of every frame. It looks the same but runs a lot faster","PICK YOUR NEXT ROOT. Level-ups no longer hand you a set root. When you earn a pick, choose any Easy root you like. Once every Easy root is open you choose from the Medium ones, then the Hard ones. The root screen is now sorted Easy, Medium, Hard with a header for each, and locked roots say whether you can pick them now","ROOM TO BREATHE ON THE ROOT SCREEN. On a big screen the root tiles, the preview and the details panel are bigger and more spread out","FRIENDS LIST ALWAYS SHOWING IN CO-OP. On phones and tablets the friends list now sits right under the plot instead of hiding behind a button","NO THUMBSTICK ON PC. The on-screen stick only shows up on touch screens (or if you pick Mobile)","TWELVE NEW GADGET WEAPONS. A new set of ordinary guns and tools with a garden-shed paint job, with its own set bonus (crit chance and attack speed): Gnome Pistol (every 6th shot crits), Sprinkler SMG, Weed Machete, Fence Nailer (goes through three pests), Hose Shotgun (shoves pests back), Hedge Trimmer, Fence Zapper, Fertiliser Grenade, Gnome Turret (sets down gnomes that shoot on their own), Flowerpot Rocket (homes in and blows up), Scarecrow Rifle (huge range, goes through five pests) and the Seed Drill Gatling (spins up to fire twice as fast). All the old weapons are still there","PUNCHIER WEAPONS. Guns kick back and flash at the muzzle, gadget guns spit out shell casings, melee swings leave a brighter trail, weapons are drawn a bit bigger, and every hit now bursts with a flash and sparks where it lands","FASTER, SHORTER, BUSIER WAVES. Waves last 16 seconds at the start and 34 at most (they used to be longer), pests spawn sooner and there are a lot more of them, each a little less tough","MORE LIKE BROTATO. Pick your starting weapon on the root screen (the root's favourite or any tier 1 weapon). The market shows how much damage each weapon and pet has done this run, and the end screen ranks them. Four new items: Duct Tape, Gnome Hat, Tool Belt and Can of Petrol"]},{"v":"2.1.0","d":"Oct 12, 2026, 7:28 PM ET","items":["PLAYER LEVELS AND ROOT UNLOCKS. You now have a player level that goes up with how long you last and how many pests you squash, and it takes a while. New players start with just the Seedling. Level 3 opens the Beet, then every other level opens the next basic root, easiest first. Once every basic root is open, those levels give you a pick and you choose which advanced root to open. Existing players get their level back from the record they already have","DAILY QUESTS FROM WORMSLEY. A worm in a tiny top hat and a monocle now lives behind the Quests button on the title screen. He hands out five odd jobs a day (they reset at midnight New York time), like squashing a certain pest, reaching a wave or digging up crates. Each one pays player XP and seed money that's added to your next run. He has a lot to say, and you can poke him","DIFFICULTY DOTS ON EVERY ROOT. One green dot is easy, two yellow dots is medium and three red dots is hard, shown on the root tiles, the root details and the Bestiary, along with a line saying what the root is for (Tank, Kiter, Crits, Economy and so on)","PESTS LOOK MORE REAL AND ARE A BIT BIGGER. All sixteen pests were repainted with jointed legs, segmented antennae, veined wings, compound eyes, hairs and shine, and they're about a third bigger. Bosses are a little bigger too","TOUGHER PESTS HAVE SPECIAL ATTACKS, AND YOU CAN SEE THEM COMING. Hornets and rats dash at you, snails, spitters and spiders spit a spread of shots, grubs throw clods of dirt, beetles whack the ground in front of them and the mole cricket now warns you before it pops up. Elites that don't have an attack of their own slam the ground. Every attack shows a red warning first (a line, cone or circle) and the pest glows red while it winds up","EIGHT NEW WEAPONS, twenty-six in all, and every weapon now has its own trick shown in the market: Acorn Launcher (shots bounce three times), Bean Boomerang (flies out and comes back), Thistle Mine (plants mines), Dibber (3x crits), Garden Rake (pulls pests in), Wooden Mallet (slams the ground and dazes), Dandelion Puffs (seeds that home in) and Chilli Flamer (a short burst of fire that burns everything). Old weapons got tricks too, like the Pea Shooter's fat pea, the Hoe's extra materials and the Scythe healing you","EVERY ROOT HAS ITS OWN STARTING WEAPON AND A CLEARER JOB. Starting weapons were changed so no two roots start the same, and their stats and perks were tuned so each one plays differently and none starts much stronger than the rest","BIGGER PROJECTILES. Your shots, pests' shots and lobbed shots are all bigger and easier to see","MORE BROTATO STATS. Melee damage, ranged damage and elemental damage are new stats that only boost that kind of weapon, with level-up cards and items for each","TWENTY NEW ITEMS, seventy-three in all, like Sharp Pruners, a Seed Drill, a Witch's Cauldron, a Paper Kite, Binoculars, a Golden Watering Can and a Lucky Acorn","FIVE NEW PETS, including rare ones. An Earthworm that tunnels under pests, plus rare pets that turn up far less often in the market: a Stag Beetle that flings pests, a Dragonfly that zips about, and the legendary Praying Mantis (hits two pests and can crit) and Barn Owl (swoops through a whole line of pests)"]},{"v":"2.0.0","d":"Oct 12, 2026, 3:44 PM ET","items":["BEETDOWN IS REBUILT FROM THE GROUND UP WITH A REALISTIC LOOK. The plot is now a real night allotment - loose tilled soil with damp and dry patches, furrows, pebbles, clods, fallen leaves, puddles that reflect the sky and railway-sleeper edging - lit by lantern posts, the glow around each root and every shot and burst. Everything casts a soft shadow, pests leave splats on the soil and a Graphics setting (Low, Medium, High) keeps phones smooth","EVERY ROOT HAS A NEW LOOK AND NEW ANIMATIONS. All twenty roots are repainted with real shading, skin texture and leaves, and they now walk on little root legs, lean into turns, breathe, blink, flinch when hit, keel over when they go down and pop out of the soil at the start of a wave","FOUR NEW ROOTS: Carrot (long range, weak up close), Corn (an extra kernel on every shot), Pea Pod (brings a pet and has room for more) and Aubergine (Bloom weapons hit harder). Twenty-four in all","EVERY ROOT HAS ITS OWN PERK NOW, Brotato style - the Garlic stinks out anything near it, the Chilli sets crits on fire, the Thistle sprays thorns when it's hit, the Pumpkin grows every wave, the Potato's harvest keeps growing, the Toadstool leaves spore clouds where pests die and so on. The perk is shown when you pick a root","YOUR WEAPONS FLOAT AROUND YOU AND AIM THEMSELVES, like Brotato - each one turns to its own target, recoils when it fires and swings or stabs when it's a melee weapon","TEN NEW WEAPONS, eighteen in all: Pea Shooter, Cob Popper, Pumpkin Mortar, Thorn Crossbow, Garden Shears, Spade, Reaping Scythe, Pollen Wand (arcs between pests), Spore Puffer (poison clouds) and Sun Lens (a beam). Every weapon belongs to a set - Tools, Seeds, Thorns or Blooms - and carrying two, three or four of a set gives a bonus","TWENTY-THREE NEW ITEMS, fifty-three in all, including ones that change how you play: a Clay Piggy that pays interest, Twin Seeds for an extra projectile, a Garden Gnome that hits harder while you stand still, a Glass Bell Jar that blocks a hit each wave, Hot Mulch that sets pests alight, a Frost Cloche that slows them, a Bee Box for an extra pet slot and more. New stats come with them: % damage, range and tending (pet strength)","TWO NEW PETS - a Bumblebee and a Toad - seven in all, and pets now get stronger with tending","EIGHT NEW PESTS, sixteen in all: locust swarms, snails that leave slowing slime, spiders that spit webs, rats that wind up and charge, puffballs that burst when they die, mole crickets that tunnel under you, fire ants that come in packs and mealybug nurses that heal the pests around them. Elite pests turn up on waves 8, 12 and 17 and always drop a crate","SEVEN BOSSES WITH REAL ATTACKS. Wave 5 is the Grub King or the Rat King, wave 10 the Mole or the Mother Spider, wave 15 the Wasp Queen or the Crow Baron, and wave 20 is the Blight Weevil, which changes its attacks halfway down. Bosses telegraph slams, charges, bullet rings, web volleys and dive bombs before they land, and get a health bar at the top","MORE LIKE BROTATO BETWEEN WAVES. Level-ups no longer pause the fight - you pick them all at the end of the wave from upgrade cards that come in four rarities. Seed crates dropped during a wave are opened afterwards (take the item or sell it), and berries that drop now and then heal you","A NEW CO-OP MENU WITH YOUR FRIENDS LIST DOWN THE SIDE. Make or join a plot for up to four, pick your root, and see who's online - invite them, join their plot or accept friend requests right there. On phones the friends list slides in from the side. Enemy shots and boss attacks now hit everyone in co-op, not just the host, and you can see your friends' weapons firing","A COMPACT NEW UI THAT FITS THE SCREEN. The title, character select, co-op menu, market, level-up and pause screens were all redesigned to fit without scrolling - spread out on a PC and stacked tighter on a phone, with a floating joystick anywhere on the screen, a smaller HUD with a big wave timer, and a tidy market with stats and set bonuses on the side (a Gear / Stats switch on phones)","The title screen now plays out live behind the menu, and the Bestiary has tabs for roots, pests, bosses and weapons"]},{"v":"1.11.0","d":"Oct 11, 2026, 11:14 AM ET","items":["FRIENDS LIST ON EVERY MULTIPLAYER GAME. The main menu now has a friends panel down the side with an Add friend button - add someone by the username they picked at sign-up. It's one list shared by Meat Lad, Beetdown, Alien Attack and Starforge, so a friend you add once shows up everywhere","INVITE FRIENDS TO YOUR PLOT. While you have an online plot open, a mini friends list sits at the side of the menus with a green dot for whoever is online and what they're playing, plus an Invite button","INVITES POP UP WHEREVER YOUR FRIEND IS - in this game, another game or the main menu - and Join takes them straight into your plot, opening Beetdown for them if they were somewhere else"]},{"v":"1.10.1","items":["FIXED CLOUD SAVES SILENTLY NEVER LOADING. The code asked Firestore whether a document existed by calling exists() as a function, but in this version of the SDK it is a plain property - so the call threw, got swallowed by the error handler, and your online save was quietly ignored every single time. Progress made on another device will come back now"]},{"v":"1.10.0","items":["A LITTLE SEEDLING NOW HOPS BACK AND FORTH BETWEEN TWO SOIL BEDS ON THE TITLE SCREEN, with an aphid crawling along the gap underneath - a small animated mascot for the menu, matching the one Meat Lad has always had"]},{"v":"1.9.0","items":["ORIGINAL MUSIC. A relaxed garden theme plays on the menus, and a combat theme kicks in during a run that gets more intense the harder the difficulty - more layers, faster tempo, busier drums the deeper into Rotten Harvest territory you go. All written from scratch with the Web Audio API, no samples"]},{"v":"1.8.0","items":["NEW UI, MORE MOBILE FRIENDLY. Fixed double-tap accidentally zooming or selecting text, and dragging on menus no longer triggers the browser's pinch-zoom - both were mobile browser default behaviours that had never been switched off","PC OR MOBILE - pick on the main game-select page (or change it anytime from the button there). Mobile keeps the on-screen joystick, PC hides it since keyboard controls already work","SPAWN WARNING MARKS. Pests now show a shrinking ring where they're about to appear a beat before they do, so incoming waves feel readable instead of things just popping in on top of you","PETS ARE BIGGER AND GLOW. Each one now has a soft coloured glow matching its type and is noticeably larger both on your character and in the shop","ITEM ICONS ARE CLEARER. Every weapon and item icon now sits on a tier-coloured ring with a dark backing circle so they're easier to tell apart at a glance, and render at higher resolution so the detail doesn't blur"]},{"v":"1.7.2","items":["THE MAIN GAME-SELECT PAGE NOW RUNS ON THE SAME FIREBASE ACCOUNT AS BOTH GAMES. It was quietly running its own separate, older login system this whole time - signing in there did nothing for Beetdown or Meat Lad. Sign in once on the main page now and both games recognise you immediately, no second sign-in anywhere"]},{"v":"1.7.1","items":["FIXED A BROKEN SCRIPT. A batch of curly/smart quotes had snuck into the changelog text and were breaking the page's JavaScript outright on some browsers - some buttons silently did nothing because the script had already crashed before it could wire them up. All fixed","THE PAUSE BUTTON NO LONGER SHOWS ON THE TITLE SCREEN. It's only meant to appear during a run - a missing style rule meant it stayed visible everywhere","The home button now reads \"Back to Main Menu\" instead of just \"Games\""]},{"v":"1.7.0","items":["FIREBASE AUTHENTICATION. Accounts now live in Firebase with email + password sign-in. One login for both Beetdown and Meat Lad, same friends list everywhere","CHARACTER SELECT COMPLETE OVERHAUL. The picker is now full-screen with your selected root centered and huge on the left, stats and bestiary below. In co-op, your teammate's picks show on the right half as live-rendered character art (not icons), updating instantly when anyone changes their pick. No more separate basic/complex sections - all 20 roots in one smooth scrolling list","COMPACT HOME BUTTONS ON BOTH GAMES. Top-left corner back button on Beetdown (left arrow with kick animation) and top-right on Meat Lad (right arrow with flip animation), both branded to each game's colour scheme. Same navigation, less screen real estate","UI POLISH EVERYWHERE. Cleaner account sign-in with proper visibility and better feedback. All buttons and inputs are sharper and more responsive"]},{"v":"1.6.0","items":["PICKING A ROOT NOW SHOWS IT PROPERLY. The character screen has a preview panel on the left - the root you have selected, drawn full size, with its description underneath and its bestiary record below that (which difficulty, if any, you have cleared while playing it specifically). The pick grid sits alongside it so you can flip through them and watch the preview update"]},{"v":"1.5.1","items":["FIXED SIGN-IN. \"Can't reach the account server\" was real - the account index had been silently deleted by the free host it lives on (it enforces a strict 24-hour lifetime with no way to renew it, which nobody realised until it actually expired). A fresh index is in place and sign-in works again. Any account made before this fix is gone - sorry, there was no way to recover it. This backend is not durable long-term and needs a proper home"]},{"v":"1.5.0","items":["FIXED: THE FRIENDS LIST DISAPPEARED THE MOMENT YOU MADE A PLOT. It lived inside the same block that gets hidden once you're in a plot - it's moved out and stays visible the whole time you're on the Co-op screen, signed in or not, in a plot or not","FRIENDS NOW GENUINELY LINK BOTH GAMES. Add a friend in Meat Lad and they show up here, and back - one shared list either way. This also caught and fixed a real risk: Meat Lad's save used to overwrite the WHOLE shared account document, which could have silently wiped a signed-in player's Beetdown progress. It now merges instead of overwriting, and Meat Lad can sign into an account that was first created here","A REAL READY SYSTEM. The market button is now Ready, not Next wave - the round only starts once everyone in the plot has readied up, shown live as it happens, so nobody's shopping gets cut short by someone else's itchy trigger finger","DYING IN CO-OP ISN'T THE END ANY MORE. Go down mid-wave and, as long as someone else carries the wave to the finish, you're back at full health when the next one starts","PETS. Five companions to buy in the market - Firefly, Ladybug, Field Mouse, Sparrow and Hedgehog - each fighting on their own beside you. Buy a duplicate to level one up instead of wasting a slot, same as weapons","NINE MORE ROOTS, twenty in total. Five straightforward ones - Leek, Kale, Parsnip, Yam, Cabbage - and four that pull several stats at once and want you to lean into a build: Artichoke, Ginger, Squash, Nettle. The picker is grouped into Basic and Complex now so twenty roots stay easy to scan instead of one long scroll","SIX MORE ITEMS, thirty in total, every one with its own drawn icon - Rain Barrel, Iron Trowel, Four-Leaf Charm, Vulture Feather, Sundial and Old Boots","EVERY PEST AND BOSS HAS REAL SHADING NOW, not a flat fill, plus a faint idle breathing motion so nothing sits dead still on screen","ROOTS ARE COLOUR-CODED BY THE HARDEST DIFFICULTY YOU'VE CLEARED WITH THEM, with a key on the character screen and the bestiary explaining which colour is which"]},{"v":"1.4.0","items":["FOUR REAL BUGS FIXED. The lock button now actually holds - an item you lock stays reserved across waves until you buy it or unlock it, not just through a single reroll. The joystick can no longer pop up or eat taps while you are typing a code or your name; it only exists during a run. If you were not the party leader you had no way to pick your own root and were stuck with whatever the host chose - now everyone picks their own, and changing your mind after connecting actually reaches the host. And dying in co-op used to end the run outright, which froze the whole plot if the HOST was the one who went down - now the plot keeps fighting until every last one of you is down, decided once, for everyone","ONE SIGN-IN FOR BOTH GAMES. Sign in on the game-select screen (or in either game) and both recognise the same session - no separate login for Beetdown any more. Still the exact same shared account as before, this just adds a third place to start from","A BESTIARY. Every root, pest and boss in one place from the title screen. Pests and bosses stay hidden as a \"?\" until you actually kill one for the first time, which pops up a small discovery notice when it happens","ROOTS ARE COLOUR-CODED BY DIFFICULTY. Clear all 20 waves on a tier with a given root and its card picks up that tier's colour, on the character select screen, the party screen and the bestiary - with a key explaining which colour is which","THE MORE PEOPLE ARE IN A PLOT, THE MORE IS ON DISPLAY. The party list now shows how many pests and bosses you personally have discovered"]},{"v":"1.3.0","items":["FRIENDS AND INVITES. Sign in on the Co-op screen - it's the same account as Meat Lad, so signing in with the same username and password there makes you the same person here, with one shared friends list. Add a friend by username, and once you've made a plot, invite them straight from the host panel","DIFFICULTY LADDER. Five tiers from Sprout up to Rotten Harvest, each one noticeably tougher than the last. You start with only Sprout open - clearing all 20 waves on a tier unlocks the next one, and there's no skipping ahead. Pick it on the character-select screen, or as the host before starting a plot","THE MORE OF YOU THERE ARE, THE HARDER IT GETS. Co-op now scales with how many are actually in the plot - more spawns, tougher pests, harder hits, bigger bosses - so a full plot is a real fight and not four people mowing down a solo-sized wave. Scaling is based on who's in the run, not who's alive this second, so dying never makes it easier","Run results now show which difficulty you played and how many were in the plot"]},{"v":"1.2.1","items":["FIXED THE LOCK BUTTON. It was a button nested inside another button, which real browsers route taps for unreliably - it now sits in a plain card, not another control, and works every time","Fixed the reroll price display, which was being wiped out on every single market redraw and quietly stopped updating","Found why buttons low on the character-select and party screens sometimes did not respond: the movement joystick sits on top of the whole screen and was capturing taps everywhere, all the time - not just during a run. It now only intercepts touches while you are actually playing","Items in the market are a bit cheaper across the board"]},{"v":"1.2.0","items":["STOPPED IT LOOKING LIKE A FAIRGROUND. Everything was neon purple, magenta and acid green, which was an eyesore and made the pests hard to pick out. The whole game now sits in a narrow, dirty, desaturated band — worn browns, dull olive, tarnished gold. Menus are flat panels with thin borders instead of glowing rounded slabs","THE ARENA IS QUIET ON PURPOSE. The planted beds and bright crops were competing with the enemies for your attention. It is packed dirt now — tonal patches, grit, cart ruts, a few tilled strips, stones, dead stalks and planks, all kept within a shade or two of each other, with a dark board fence and a vignette so the middle reads clearest","YOU STAND OUT NOW. Your root is drawn larger than everything else, outlined in pale cream, and sits inside a ring on the ground so you can always find yourself in a crowd. Every pest gets a dark rim so it separates from the dirt","FIVE NEW ROOTS — the Onion is layered in armour but hits softly, the Chilli is vicious and made of paper, the Pumpkin is enormous and slow, the Toadstool drains life and slips punches, and the Potato digs up extra materials every wave. Eleven to choose from","THREE NEW PESTS — ticks that swarm you fast in numbers, hornets that dart about and shoot, and grubs that are slow, armoured and take some shifting","SEVEN NEW ITEMS — Copper Kettle, Ball of Twine, Bent Trowel, Storm Lantern, Rusted Anvil, Jar of Nettles and the Old Scythe. Twenty-four in all","THE MARKET WORKS PROPERLY NOW. Tier odds shift as the run goes on and with your luck, so early shops are mostly commons and late ones can turn up something worth having. You can sell any weapon or item back for roughly half — though never your last weapon. The reroll price climbs each time you use it and resets when the next wave starts. Your full stat line and your gear sit alongside the offers"]},{"v":"1.1.0","items":["THE PLOT IS A REAL ALLOTMENT NOW, not a grid. Four raised beds with timber edging and planted rows, gravel paths crossing the middle, a greenhouse, a water butt, a wheelbarrow, weeds, mushrooms, stones and puddles - and fireflies drifting over the lot. It is dug fresh every run, so no two plots look the same","EVERY ROOT HAS ITS OWN BODY. The Beet is a heavy bulb with a tap root and a scowl, the Radish is pink on top and pale below, the Thistle is a spiked ball with a tuft, the Garlic has clove seams and a papery stalk, the Turnip has purple shoulders, and the Seedling is a sprout on a stem","EVERY PEST IS ITS OWN CREATURE. Aphids scuttle on six jointed legs with antennae, slugs ooze along on eye stalks leaving slime, wasps have striped abdomens and a stinger under beating wings, beetles have a split carapace and mandibles, and spitters lug a swollen sac behind a nozzle","The four bosses are properly built: the Grub King is a segmented grub in a crown, the Mole has digging claws and a pink snout, the Wasp Queen is crowned with triple wings, and the Root Weevil is an armoured dome on a long snout","Every weapon, item and perk has a drawn icon, and so does every root on the character screen - no more reading a list of names","Crit, dodge, life steal and harvesting are now real stats. Crits hit for double and pop in gold, dodges say so, life steal trickles health back, and harvesting pays out at the end of each wave","Five new items to chase them with - Whetstone, Tumbleweed, Leech Root, Harvest Basket and the Allotment Crown - plus four new level-up picks","Items and weapons have tiers now, colour-coded from common to legendary, and you can lock an offer in the market so a reroll leaves it alone","The market shows your full stat line, so you can see what a purchase actually did","Sped the drawing up: the glow on fireflies, materials and shots was being blurred by the canvas every single frame, which is ruinously slow. Those are baked once now and stamped down instead"]},{"v":"1.0.0","items":["BEETDOWN EXISTS. A twenty-wave survival arena in a night allotment - you move and dodge, your weapons fire themselves, and you spend what you scavenge between waves","Six roots to play, each with a real trade-off: the Beet hits harder but lumbers, the Radish is quick and fragile, the Thistle hurts anything that touches it, the Garlic heals itself, the Turnip starts rich and the Seedling is plain but takes every upgrade well","Eight weapons - slingshot, seed spitter, thorn whip, pitchfork, beet cannon, nettle sprayer, bramble orbit and the hoe - and you can carry six at once. Buying a duplicate levels it up instead of wasting the slot","Twelve items to stack: compost, fertiliser, sunlamp, root boots, bark plate, dew drop, magnet root, lucky clover, thorn coat, greenhouse glass, sharp gravel and the scarecrow","Five pests with different jobs - aphids swarm, slugs soak damage, wasps dart in fast, beetles hit hard and shed armour, and spitters keep their distance and shoot","Bosses on waves 5, 10, 15 and 20: the Grub King, the Mole, the Wasp Queen and the Root Weevil","Level up mid-wave and pick one of four upgrades; the market between waves offers weapons and items with a reroll","Online co-op with a five-letter code, same as Meat Lad. Whoever makes the plot runs the waves and the pests so both screens agree, and you share the wave but keep your own gear and levels","Runs on phones - drag anywhere on the left half of the screen to move"]}]},"linework":{"nm":"Linework","href":"linework.html","entries":[{"v":"1.6.1","d":"Menu Unblocked","items":["Fixed: a corner button was covering the ☰ menu. The ☰ menu is back, with Back to Main Menu inside it."]},{"v":"1.6","d":"Way Home","items":["Back to Main Menu button in the ☰ menu (top right) takes you straight to the game select."]},{"v":"1.5","d":"Room to Breathe","items":["Harder networks pull the camera back much further at 500 riders carried.","Everyone gets a big pull-back at 1,000, whichever network you're running.","Kesridge and Rushgate reworked — water and rush hour now, not terrain.","Two new networks: Millbrook and Saltmarsh, ten in total."]},{"v":"1.4","d":"City Limits","items":["The map now expands every 500 riders carried, and the camera pulls back to show it.","Pinch, scroll, or drag empty ground to look around the city yourself.","An overcrowded platform snaps the view back to normal automatically.","Three new networks: Rushgate, Three Rivers, The Wilds."]},{"v":"1.3","d":"A Better Way In","items":["A proper Play button, and a full-screen How to Play.","The upgrades panel is now genuinely toggleable — tap it, or Escape, or the map behind it.","Fixed the dock shifting under your finger right when you meant to tap something."]},{"v":"1.2","d":"The City Talks Back","items":["LINEWORK Transit: every city gets its own name, and so does every station.","Short service announcements — new stations, crowding, a line running smoothly.","Parks, downtown blocks, and river bridges give every map its own layout."]},{"v":"1.1","d":"Drawing Lines That Feel Right","items":["Swipe across several stations in one motion to lay a whole route at once.","Drag a line's end to extend it; slide back the way you came to trim it.","Day and night now cycle through the week, with glowing lines after dark."]},{"v":"1.0","d":"First Service","items":["LINEWORK opens for business: draw lines, run trains, keep the platforms clear."]}]},"quackpot":{"nm":"Quackpot","href":"quackpot.html","entries":[{"v":"Oct 12, 2026","d":"Upgrade rebalance and new upgrade menus","items":["Upgrades that did the same thing were merged or cut: Saddle Bags, Lucky Nose, Coin Multiplier, Sprint Power, Range (now part of Muzzle Velocity), Boss Chance (it never worked), Spread Choke, Ricochet Plate, Magnet Core, the dog coats, the Lucky Charm and the Trader's Ledger. Pet speed and carry now only come from Pet Shop training","Overpowered upgrades were toned down: Damage, Fire Rate, Critical Chance and Power, Duck Value, Luck, Gilding, Multi Barrel and pet training all give less per level and stop sooner","Everything costs more: upgrades, guns, coolers, gear, pets and lakes. Rounds need more ducks and pay a smaller bonus","Skipping a round now pays just enough for one or two cheap upgrades and no feathers, and you can skip at most 5 rounds in a row before you have to clear one","New upgrade menus: upgrades are tiles grouped into coloured sections, each with a level bar and what the next level gives, instead of a long list of buttons","Anything you owned that was removed or capped has been refunded in coins and feathers"]},{"v":"Oct 12, 2026","d":"Your own lodge, a pet shop and a proper town square","items":["New: Your Lodge, a log cabin of your own with a fireplace. Use the wardrobe to change your shirt, vest, cap, trousers, skin and beard, check the trophy wall for your achievements, and read your hunting journal for your stats","Heading back from a lake now asks where to go: the Town Square or Your Lodge. There's also a My Lodge signpost by the truck in town","The Dog Depot is now the Pet Shop. Every pet has the same stats, so pick the one you like, then train them with permanent pet upgrades: Zoomies Training, Bigger Mouth, Keen Nose and Show Grooming. Pets you already own carry their old strength over as upgrade levels","The town square is solid cobblestone with no grass poking through, with a darker cobbled ring road round the fountain and cobbled paths out to every shop","A new two-tier stone fountain with water spilling over each bowl (the duck statue is gone) and six benches facing it","Fixed the Duck Exchange roof, which was sideways, and its sign is now in front of the columns where you can read it. The Pet Shop and Duck Gear signs are no longer hidden under their roofs","No more floating bits: the Upgrade Lab's vats stand on the ground, and loose roof trim and windows that stuck out of roofs are gone. Roof ends are filled in","Plants in the town planters no longer wobble, and the townsfolk are proper hunters now","The backyard pond has a natural, wavy shoreline, greener water and more lily pads, and the second dock is gone","Downed ducks land in all sorts of poses instead of all lying the same way"]},{"v":"Oct 12, 2026","d":"Everything rebuilt for a realistic look","items":["Every model in the game is new and far less blocky: ducks, hunters, trees, bushes, rocks, reeds, logs, docks, boats, the lodge, the cooler and each zone's props. Only the dog keeps its look","New ducks with real plumage for every species, folded wings that open into full feathered wings in flight, and new animations: blinking, looking around, quacking, preening, wing shakes, paddling feet and legs tucked up in flight","New hunter with realistic proportions, a sculpted face and beard, flannel shirt, vest, belt and boots, and arms that properly hold the gun","Realistic ground, sky, clouds, water and sunlight with soft shadows, and the ground no longer pokes up through the dock","Many more trees and bushes: leafy oaks, birches, pines with needles, meadow grass, flowers and reeds along the shore, with forests and mountains in the distance","Town buildings now have brick, stone, plaster and wooden board walls and shingled roofs","Smoother guns with rounded edges, a shaped wooden stock and glossy finishes","Each zone has its own ambience: wind, birdsong and water lapping, frogs and crickets in the marsh, creaking ice, crackling lava, city traffic and station beeps","Footsteps that sound different on grass, wood, stone, snow and water, wingbeats from ducks flying close by, a punchier gunshot that echoes off the hills, and a better splash","New Graphics setting in the pause menu (High, Medium or Low). Phones start on Medium"]},{"v":"Oct 12, 2026","d":"Hunt with friends, and a whole new look","items":["New Multiplayer menu on the main screen: open a party, share the 5-letter code, or join a friend's code","Your friends list is built into the Multiplayer menu - see who's online, invite them, join their party, and add or accept friends without leaving the game","Online co-op for up to 4 hunters: everyone shares the same lake and ducks, whoever downs a duck keeps it, and the party follows the host between lakes","Other hunters show up in their party colour with a name tag, their own dog, and their shots; a party list sits in the corner of the HUD","Rebuilt ducks with feathered textures, blinking eyes, quacking bills and paddling feet; bosses still wear their hats","Rebuilt dogs with jointed legs, floppy ears, a wagging tail and a panting tongue","Rebuilt the hunter: plaid jacket, vest, pack and bedroll, ear-flap cap, and a walk cycle with breathing and blinking","New textures across the world: grass, bark, wood planks and rippling water, plus waving meadow grass, lily pads, flowers, bushes, pines, drifting clouds and far hills","Rebuilt all 11 guns: wooden stocks, sights and scopes, and proper silly front ends - a taped spud tube, a striped popcorn drum, a rubber-duck sight, a spinning six-barrel toaster and more","Fresh menus and HUD: the main menu now sits over a live view of the lake, with rounder cards, new buttons and a new font","On phones, pop-up messages no longer cover the ability buttons"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Look at what you're buying","items":["Click any gun, dog, or cooler in the shop to spin it in a live 3D preview instead of just a flat icon","Added simple models for the 3 new guns so they don't all look like the starter Spud Launcher"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Cheaper gear, reactive UI, town fixes","items":["Gun and cooler prices cut roughly a third, with 3 new guns and 2 new coolers filling the gaps","Dogs no longer walk into town still holding ducks in their mouth - anything they're carrying gets stowed before the trip home","Fixed the cobblestone plaza looking like a field of boulders (the texture was tiled far too large)","Removed the floating path strips around the plaza - the cobblestone slab already covers the ground","HUD stat chips and shop item rows are more colourful and animated - coloured accents, hover lift, and an entrance animation"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Boss rework, town rebuild, dev menu","items":["Bosses: half health, no more glow halo, and they now drop in with a landing impact instead of just appearing","No new ducks spawn while a boss fight is active","Recoil and screen shake toned back down; bullets spin as they fly","Town: cobblestone plaza that no longer floats over the hills, a smaller fountain with no coins scattered around it, shops moved closer in, loose ducks removed","Backyard lake: the boundary fence no longer cuts through the water, and there are far more trees with real variety","Removed the odd decoy-duck cluster from the truck bed and the signs by the lake","Grass now has a speckled texture instead of a flat colour; all models are noticeably smoother","Fixed the dog's tail disappearing into its body","Mouse now always turns the camera once clicked - right-click is for zooming in only"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Rain, puddles, ripples, and a more reliable aim ring","items":["Rain now actually looks like rain: falling drops, splashes on the water, ripples spreading out, and puddles forming on the shore","The lake is glossier and a bit more reflective","Fog thickens a little while it's raining","Fixed the aim ring failing to show on far-off ducks and being inconsistent on bosses"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Duck Storm gets a warning, aim ring gets steadier","items":["Aim reticle is bigger and no longer drifts off a bobbing or distant duck","Duck Storm now gives you a jittery \"DUCK STORM\" warning and a rumble before the flock rushes in from the left","The old instant, no-warning duck storm is gone for good"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Precision crits, round timer, weather and boss cycles","items":["Aim reticle: a red ring over the duck you're aiming at, a small yellow ring inside it for a guaranteed 1.75x crit","Every 5 rounds a new species takes the spotlight, gets more common each round, then becomes that round's boss","Rounds now last 5 minutes - the boss shows up automatically if time runs out","Added a Skip Round button","Duck Storm now rushes in from the left instead of popping into the sky","Rain now happens sometimes - ducks caught while it's raining sell for 25% more","Auto-Cooler and Auto-Sell now cost 500 and 850 coins"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"A livelier title screen","items":["Ducks now fly across the title screen background","Fixed the How to Play / What's New popups rendering behind the menu","Auto-Cooler and Auto-Sell now cost 600 and 1500 coins"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Auto-cooler and auto-sell moved to the dog","items":["Auto-Cooler and Auto-Sell are now per-run dog upgrades, not permanent gear","Both are unlocked from the start, but you rebuy them each round","Dropped Fetch Range - dogs now fetch from any distance"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Better models and punchier gunplay","items":["Reworked duck, player and gun models with far more detail","Ducks now wag their tails, waddle and bob continuously","Recoil, muzzle flash and camera shake all hit much harder","Right-click now zooms in while aiming","Downed ducks stay on the ground until your dog collects them","Starting bosses hit for much less and stay near the lake"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Movement, guns and crosshair pass","items":["Saddle Bags now upgrades your dog's carry, not your own arms","Every upgrade shows a preview of its next level before you buy it","Walk cycle, landing squash, head-bob and a sprint field-of-view kick","Every weapon now has a real hand grip, trigger guard and foregrip","Crosshair reworked: a live hit-ring flash and a pulsing lock-on state"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Rebuilt the town's architecture","items":["Every shop remodelled from real profiles - bevelled windows, doors, roofs","Fixed roofs that were rotated 90 degrees off every building","Fixed every shop facing away from the plaza","Fixed sign text being hidden inside its own frame"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Town square and cheaper early game","items":["Redesigned the lodge into a proper square around a duck fountain","Five distinct storefronts instead of recoloured boxes","The first few levels of every upgrade are now heavily discounted"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Permanent shops vs run upgrades","items":["Guns, dogs, coats and gear bought in town are kept forever","Upgrades bought at the cooler reset when you head back to town","Added dog coats, worn gear, cooler tiers and cooler modules"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Rounds, storms and rude ducks","items":["The hunt is split into rounds, each harder than the last","Duck storms and a rare charging Raging Duck","Selling no longer covers the screen; shot a shader sky in instead"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"The lodge, research and the cooler","items":["Added a Hunting Lodge with a Research Bench and a Map Board","Ducks go to the cooler by hand instead of straight into a bag","Every duck banked is worth one feather, spent on research"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"A proper lake to hunt over","items":["Ducks fly left to right over open water in front of you","Fixed ducks spinning on the spot instead of turning smoothly","Added on-screen touch controls for phones"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Graphics and audio overhaul","items":["Cel-shaded materials, a proper sky and better lighting","Every sound effect rebuilt from scratch"]},{"v":"Aug 27, 2026","d":"Rebuilt Quackpot in 3D","items":["Replaced the flat clicker with a real third-person 3D game","Walk, aim and shoot, with a dog that fetches what you down"]},{"v":"Aug 26, 2026","d":"Quackpot added to the site","items":["A duck-hunting game joins the game picker"]}]},"deepcore":{"nm":"Deepcore","href":"deepcore.html","entries":[{"v":"1.0","d":"Breaking Ground","items":["Deepcore launches: a two-player co-op mining incremental. One player drills, the other refines, and each one pays for the other's upgrades.","Four resources in a chain: Ore → Alloy → Compound → Cosmic Energy.","Sync strikes: hit the rock and the crucible within half a second of each other to build a combo multiplier of up to ×2.","Heat: hard drilling runs hot and pays double ore above 70%, but at 100% the drill locks. Every refine press vents some of that heat.","Events: Cave-ins need six sync strikes in 15 seconds. Magma Surges need the Refiner to keep the Driller from overheating.","Collapse the Shaft once you have 10 Cosmic Energy to earn Core Shards for a permanent upgrade tree. Strata keep going past the Void Core forever.","Adaptive soundtrack: bass, drums and a lead come in as you work faster, combo up or run hot."]}]},"gridlock":{"nm":"Gridlock","href":"gridlock.html","entries":[{"v":"8.1","d":"Oct 27","items":["If the 3D view can't draw on a phone, the town now drops back to 2D with a short note saying why, instead of leaving an empty sky. Tap 3D to try again.","The game keeps running if something goes wrong in one frame, instead of freezing for good.","When a phone takes its graphics memory back and gives it again, the 3D ground is painted afresh (it used to come back black)."]},{"v":"8.0","d":"Oct 26","items":["Garbage: homes and businesses fill their bins once the town has about 60 people. Build a landfill, an incinerator (which makes a little power) or a recycling center, and its trucks come round to empty them. Bins left full make people unhappy, and in the end they move out.","Deathcare: a cemetery that fills grave by grave and a crematorium, each with hearses. Homes near one are happier.","Post: a post office with red vans and a mail sorting center. Homes in reach are happier and businesses pay a little more tax.","The Services bar is grouped into Safety, Health, Schools, Garbage, Post and Roads, and fire trucks, ambulances and other service vehicles may cut down pedestrian streets when the way round is long.","Info views: the new layers button (or I) colours the map by land value, happiness, building levels, power, water, traffic, road wear, pollution, noise, education or any service's reach, in 3D and 2D.","Land value: parks, services, shops, stops and water raise it, smoke and noise bring it down. It shows on plot and building cards.","Building levels 1 to 5: well-served buildings on good land with happy people level up over a few months, gaining households, jobs, floors and finishes.","Noise from busy roads, highways and industry bothers the homes nearby.","Trams: a tram depot, tram stops and a Tram line tool. Lines lay rails along the roads and three-part trams share the road with cars.","Taxis: a taxi depot whose yellow cabs fetch people from their door, so fewer drive.","Metro trains are now real three-carriage trains, and people can change between lines at shared stations.","Match days: fans travel to the stadium by car, bus, tram, metro or taxi, and all head home together after the final whistle.","The Transport bar has Bus, Tram, Metro and Taxi tabs, so it stays tidy on a phone."]},{"v":"7.9","d":"Oct 21","items":["Real 3D buildings: every house, shop, office, factory and service is built from proper walls, roofs, windows, doors, porches and rooftop kit instead of raised boxes, so 3D looks as good as 2D.","Houses come in many styles, colours and roofs, and shops are now real kinds: a bakery, cafe, restaurant, gas station, supermarket and more, each with its own sign and front.","More ways to make power and water: coal, gas, nuclear, solar and wind turbines with turning blades, and a water tower, a pump house that really draws from the lake, and a big water treatment works.","Streets have raised pavements and kerbs, zebra crossings at junctions, and far fewer manholes, set in the middle of the lane.","New junctions where three or more ordinary roads meet get traffic lights on their own, free. The Lights tool still adds or removes them.","Roads in 3D are real shapes now, with lane lines, kerbs, crossings, arrows and traffic lights on mast arms.","Four new road types: Avenue and Boulevard with a planted, tree-lined middle, a cheap slow Gravel road, and a Pedestrian street.","People walk the pavements, wait at the crossings and stroll and sit in the parks. Cars give way to anyone crossing.","The metro: build stations from Transport, tap them to draw a line (or close it into a loop), and trains run underground. People within a long walk of a station leave the car at home."]},{"v":"7.8","d":"Oct 21","items":["Schools: build an Elementary school, a High school and a College from Services, each a real building with halls, a sports field, a clock tower or a library dome. Families near them are happier.","An educated town brings in offices rather than smoky industry.","Buses: a new Transport tab with a bus depot, bus stops and a Bus line tool. Tap stops or road edges to draw a line, then add or take away buses. Buses pull in at the kerb, pick people up and drop them off, and people near a stop leave the car at home. Fares are income, and Transport has its own row and budget in Finance.","Six new parks: a playground, sports courts, a dog park, a sports field with floodlights, a botanical garden with a glasshouse, and a stadium that fills with a crowd on match days and brings in tourists.","People stand about and play in the parks.","Fire stations, police, hospitals, schools, daycare, road depots and parks reach about one and a half times further.","On phones the eight buttons along the bottom all fit in one row."]},{"v":"7.7","d":"Oct 21","items":["The City builder opens in 3D now, at a city-builder angle. You can still switch to 2D, and it remembers your choice.","The rows of coloured dots over buildings are gone: each building shows one pop-up icon for what's wrong, like a fire, no power, no water, too few customers or traffic, with a red ring counting down when it's about to close.","Tap any building or empty plot to open its card: its name, what it needs, its tax or households, parking, and which services reach it.","The top bar shows money with income per month, people, a happiness face and demand bars for homes, shops, offices and industry. Zone and service buttons have drawn icons.","Lusher 3D grass, rounder and leafier trees, and fine grass blades and leafy crowns up close in 2D.","Dust rises off building sites and rolls out when something is knocked down, chimneys smoke, and coins pop over the town at the end of each month.","Cars come as saloons, hatchbacks, SUVs, vans and pickups, mostly white, black, silver and grey like real traffic.","The middle mouse button turns and tilts the 3D view, and turning eases smoothly."]},{"v":"7.6","d":"Oct 20","items":["Zoning round any shape of land now fills it right up to your outline: slanted sides, triangles, L shapes, bends and curves, with no gaps, overlaps or plots poking out past it.","Zone a whole block at once and the plots line every road round it, meeting neatly in the middle.","New Split plot button in the Zone bar: tap a plot, pick how many with the slider, and it's cut into that many along its road.","Merge plots works on plots round a bend or on odd-shaped land too.","A house at the back of a deep plot has a driveway that stops at the edge of the road instead of running into it.","Demand is balanced: people move in while there are jobs going, shops follow people, and offices and industry come when people need work. The bars rise and fall with what your town is short of instead of sitting full."]},{"v":"7.5","d":"Oct 20","items":["The City map is four times bigger, with room for a much larger town, more countryside, rivers, lakes and mountains.","The highway in from outside still reaches as far in as before, so it's no further to join your roads up to it.","Much less stutter: building, panning and zooming out no longer freeze the screen for a moment, even on phones. The ground and woods are drawn ahead in pieces, and a growing town only redraws what changed.","In 3D, building something only redraws the ground right around it, so the view stays smooth while the town grows."]},{"v":"7.4","d":"Oct 19","items":["The ground is green everywhere now, with lusher and drier patches, wild flowers up close, and reeds along the shores.","Mountains are real: ridges and peaks of rock that stand up in 3D, with alpine grass low down.","Many more kinds of trees and plants: broadleaf, pine, fir, birch, poplar, bushes and reeds, with blossom and copper beech in gardens.","Water is a natural blue that deepens away from the shore, with gentle ripples and lapping foam, and in 3D it shines and moves.","More street lamps, closer together and in pairs on wide roads, and much brighter at night.","Houses look real: grey, slate, brown and clay-tile roofs, one or two storeys, some L-shaped, with solar panels, chimneys, garden sheds, patios, flower beds and hedges round the gardens.","Shops, offices, industry and mixed use have real roofing colours instead of bright blue, purple and orange, with coloured awnings on the shops.","In 3D, beach umbrellas, lifeguard chairs and the plant on rooftops now stand up for real."]},{"v":"7.3","d":"Oct 17","items":["Homes now come in Low, Medium and High residential. Medium grows real apartment blocks instead of long rows of townhouses, and the new High zone grows tower blocks and skyscrapers once the town passes about 150 people.","When you tap out a zone, a Houses bar shows the fewest and the most plots that land can take. Slide it to pick exactly how many houses you want.","Zone corners snap onto the edge of the pavement and onto block corners, so plots line up with the road.","Beaches: pick Beach in Parks, then tap round a stretch of shore or drag along the water's edge, and the sand follows the shoreline. Bigger beaches cost more and bring more tourists.","Marinas and lakeside pumps snap onto the nearest shore, facing the water, wherever you put them down.","Buildings only take up the space they really use, so things can go right up to them.","Shops, offices, industry, mixed use and houses all want to grow a lot more, kept in step with each other.","Plot yards are green grass, apartment roofs are grey and brown instead of green, and the mask icon over homes is gone."]},{"v":"7.2","d":"Oct 17","items":["A new look: the top bar and toolbars are joined-up panels in a road-sign typeface, picked tools are marked in road-paint yellow, and prices are plain text.","How to play folds away on the start screen, so Play is right there.","Zooming is smoother: the mouse wheel glides, and the map doesn't redraw the ground every frame while you zoom.","Buildings pop up with a little bounce and a puff of dust, and the money flashes green or red when the month's money comes in.","3D trees have fuller, lumpier crowns, and pines grow in tiers."]},{"v":"7.1","d":"Oct 17","items":["Merge plots: in Zone, tap Merge plots, pick plots side by side and tap Merge to make them one bigger plot. If anything's built on them, it asks before clearing it.","Dark mode now darkens the map too: the land, hills, lakes and roads.","A What's new list, here."]},{"v":"7.0","d":"Oct 17","items":["Whatever the town wants gets built, up to four buildings at once, and they go up faster.","An empty plot that can't grow shows why: no power, no water, no road in, or no homes joined to it.","Gaps too small for a plot go to the plot beside them.","$15,000 to start, roads half price, more power and water from each plant, and taxes up to 13% are fine.","Parking fees in Finance, up to $5. About $3 is the sweet spot.","A smaller daycare, whole numbers for power and water, and no Move map button on a PC."]},{"v":"6.9","d":"Oct 17","items":["New zones snap onto the corners and sides of zones already there."]},{"v":"6.8","d":"Oct 17","items":["3D buildings get real window frames and sills, balconies, office fins, shop awnings, parapets and chimneys.","Apartments in brick and render colours."]},{"v":"6.7","d":"Oct 17","items":["Smaller, better cars parked on proper drives, bigger houses and townhouses with a drive each.","Apartment blocks with their own car parks.","New 3D cars and trucks, a real water tower, cooling towers, chimneys and a salt dome."]},{"v":"6.6","d":"Oct 17","items":["Square corners: dotted guides show 90° and straight on while you zone, and corners snap to them.","The angle at each corner is shown as you go. Hold Shift to place one freely."]},{"v":"6.5","d":"Oct 17","items":["Finance: taxes, a budget for each service, and a year of money in and out. Tap your money or press M."]},{"v":"6.4","d":"Oct 17","items":["Zone by corners: tap round the land you want, then the first corner again."]},{"v":"6.3","d":"Oct 17","items":["Buildings and roads are built in front of you, with diggers, scaffolding, cranes and paving crews."]},{"v":"6.2","d":"Oct 17","items":["Draw round any piece of land to zone it, and deep land gets deep plots."]},{"v":"6.1","d":"Oct 17","items":["Sharper 3D, a tidier toolbar, a bigger map and a dark theme."]},{"v":"6.0","d":"Oct 15","items":["3D: the whole town stands up, and you can turn it round and tilt it."]},{"v":"5.3","d":"Oct 14","items":["Parking lots and garages, parks, beaches and marinas, hotels and a Mixed zone."]},{"v":"5.2","d":"Oct 14","items":["City services: power, water, fire, police, hospitals, schools, daycares and road depots."]},{"v":"5.1","d":"Oct 14","items":["Real plots along the road, cut evenly or at random, small, medium or large."]},{"v":"4.9","d":"Oct 14","items":["The City builder: zone land and the town grows by itself."]}]},"starforge":{"nm":"Starforge","href":"starforge/","entries":[{"v":"Oct 12","d":"10:30 PM ET","items":["STARFORGE 0.5: A REAL PLANET, NEW MENUS AND A BIG REBALANCE. You now mine a real-looking planet with oceans, clouds and city lights, crewmates ships show where they really are, drones and the Forge Tree are much cheaper, there are 6 new upgrades and 10 new tree nodes, crew roles and contracts make playing together pay off, and the new title screen has 3 save slots plus Host or Join (with join requests to friends)"]},{"v":"Oct 11","d":"7:00 PM ET","items":["STARFORGE 0.4: BETTER GRAPHICS AND A BLACK SCREEN FIX. Fixed the screen going black on phones. Starforge also gets a detailed new asteroid with molten cracks, a ringed planet with a moon in the background, new drone, hauler, rig and ship models, lots more effects (impact flares, sparks, flying ore, comet tails, a warp tunnel), a cooler UI and a Forge Tree that glows like a star map","STARFORGE 0.3: A NEW CO-OP IDEA. No more Miner and Refiner roles - everyone taps the asteroid and builds up their own rig, then the whole crew pours ore, alloy and Star Cores into one shared Forge Tree. Its 20 nodes are expensive on purpose and unlock new upgrades, big income multipliers and speed-ups for everyone. Also adds comets to catch together, a personal Quantum Leap, earnings while you are away, and a clean phone-friendly layout with Upgrades, Forge Tree and Crew tabs","STARFORGE 0.2: REAL ONLINE CO-OP FOR UP TO 4 PLAYERS. Host a party and friends join with a 5-letter code or link - no game server needed. Miners strike the asteroid, Refiners run a four-machine refinery chain that unlocks as you go, and both keep a sync combo going. Adds power and overclocks, Solar Flare and Quake events you beat together, a shared Quantum Leap vote with a skill tree, and an all-new 3D scene with new lighting, effects and animations. Its own What's new is in the game's menu"]}]},"risefall":{"nm":"Rise & Fall","href":"risefall.html","entries":[{"v":"1.0","d":"Launch","items":["A living history generator: pick a world from Small to Colossal and watch nations rise from a single camp — farming, trading, colonising distant continents, conquering, rebelling and winning independence."]}]}};
